!! Cold start ~ what a cold session reads, runs and obeys

!!! Read, in this order
# <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/noosphere-boot`>>, the boot seed, which every host seats whole at turn 1 by reference (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/seed-seat`>>); then this carrier, top to bottom.
# <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths`>> before the first commit: which session holds which ground, and the commit isolation that guards hands inside one session.
# <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/working-procedures`>> before the first test or wire: the red-first procedure in eight moves, the ladder for wiring a module that stands unreached, the instruments that read green while they lie, and the working agreements. Before any grammar work, <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/grammar-watch`>>.
# Once the memory stage stands (`L13`), its open-item index on the daemon wiki, before any file in the controller's scratch.
# Bring every ruling #/open names to the operator before a lane builds against it.

!!! Instruments ~ run each fresh before trusting a reading
* ''Branch'': `feature/green-jello-dinosaur-5`. `git rev-list --count origin/feature/green-jello-dinosaur-5..HEAD` reads the unpushed commits. The controlling session pushes only a head whose train read green over lanes whose CI and held-out read green, and only while no CI run stands in flight (`git push origin <sha>:refs/heads/feature/green-jello-dinosaur-5`); a lane pushes its own lane branch alone. Green has three readings: `node tools/ci-verdict.mjs --head <sha>` reads the branch's full CI as green, red or unread (a detached worktree names `--branch feature/green-jello-dinosaur-5`; the tool refuses a detached head without one), and prints beside it the held-out container drivers: the run that names the head, or else the newest run the branch holds, with that run's head, the commits between and each driver's result. A head no held-out run measured reads unread there, never green; the held-out reading says and never moves the exit code. Never a lane's to move, commit, stash or revert: `.worktrees/`, `.claude/worktrees/`, the `mempalace` submodule pointer, and the `VSCode-TW5-Syntax` pointer, which the operator pins at the released grammar while the grammar's own head stands ahead of that pin.
* ''Unit suites'': each package's own, under a scratch `LAR_ROOT` with `LAR_BAGS` unset; `node --test tools/*.test.mjs` for the tools. An import failure reads as zero tests, so read the file count beside the test count. `pnpm -r test` skips the root `tests/` directory by design.
* ''Typecheck'': `tools/typecheck-witness.sh`. Typecheck reads source and the binary reads `dist`, so run the built binary after any change that crosses packages.
* ''Witness fleet'': `bash tools/witness-all.sh`, the whole fleet, one run at a time through the battery lock, by a lane in its batch battery and again by the controller's train; a hand-picked subset reads greener than the tree. It holds out the witnesses its own `HELD_OUT` table names (the docker, browser and tree-clean ones). Capture its exit code directly, never through a pipe; `git status --porcelain` reads identical before and after a sound run.
* ''E2E'': every set rides CI (the lane branch's own `ci.yml` E2E job; `E2E_SETS` names the sets and the merge gate checks each set's union). `node tools/e2e-shards.mjs push <lane>` serves a bisect. A local run of one file proves that file alone.
* ''CI'' (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`): `gh run list --branch feature/green-jello-dinosaur-5` names the run for the pushed head; a later commit waits for the next push. `held-out.yml` runs on every push to a feature branch and to `main`, weekly, and by hand (`gh workflow run held-out.yml --ref <branch>`). It builds the product image once, runs one hosted job per cell (`node tools/held-out-cells.mjs cells` names them: each whole driver, and each scenario `tools/mesh-scenarios.sh --list` names), and its merge job prints one `HELD-OUT-DRIVER:` line per driver and one `HELD-OUT-VERIFY:` line. A cell that never answered reads red there by name. `node tools/held-out-steps.mjs <cell>` runs one cell's job on a desk, read off the workflow.
* ''The docker mesh'' (by hand): `crossing-witness`; `node tools/mesh-pins.mjs --up` stands the mesh pinned; `herm-mesh-witness` carries `node/alpha` across three hops; `mesh-scenarios nexus` (`tools/mesh-scenarios.sh --list` names every scenario and starts no build; `MESH_PROJECT` names the run's own compose project). The mesh publishes `MESH_PORT_*` on loopback, every node service requires `LARES_HARNESS_IMAGE`, and `tools/harness-image.sh` checks its digest. A hearth whose log carries a `[lararium] fatal:` line reads as not stood.
* ''Island capability record'': `tools/capability-inventory-witness.sh` measures one construction-time record each island carries: `caps.receipt`, `caps.cas`, `caps.dyad`, `caps.custodyWrite`, plus each source call that spawns an island or attaches that record, against `tools/capability-inventory.json`. The table measures reach, never policy (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/harbor#/the-harnesses`>>). A capability a class reaches where the table says it did not, a new or extra seam, a seam that left or a new ask reads red by name; a deliberate change re-writes the table in the commit that makes it (`node tools/capability-inventory.mjs --write`).
* ''The red register'': `packages/lararium-mesh/tests/unbuilt-laws.test.ts` declares each law the models imply and no code enforces; `tools/unbuilt-laws-witness.sh` reports a declared red that greened.
* ''Known environmental reds'': `lararium-tw5` `tests/syncer-back-parity.e2e.test.ts` (Chromium legs) and `tests/meme-routes.e2e.test.ts` fail where no browser or server socket stands; the browser suite reads `no tests` under a `HOME` that holds no Playwright Chromium. CI's browser cells run inside the pinned Playwright image, which carries every engine, and a desk reaches the same image through `tools/pw-container.sh` (`PW_IMAGE` names the pin).
* ''The operator's live `~/.lares` vessel'' holds an earlier shape that this build refuses. Never stand it here; it takes the fresh start, by hand (#/fresh-start).

!!! Steps
# `export LAR_ROOT=~/.lares-scratch/<lane>/root` before any in-tree boot or any `lares` command that touches the store. `LAR_ROOT` moves the store, the bags root and every projection mirror root; `LAR_BAGS` alone moves only the bags root, and the projection still writes the tracked `bags/` and `wikis/`. A staged e2e vessel takes its own scratch `LAR_ROOT` and no global `LAR_BAGS`. One `lares meme normalize|check` reads files and needs only a scratch `LAR_BAGS`.
# `LAR_ROOT` leaves these homes where they stand: `~/.mempalace` (the destructive `mempalace repave` among its readers), `~/.venv`, `~/.local/bin`, and the harness homes `~/.claude`, `~/.codex`, `~/.copilot`. `lares home` writes those homes under any `LAR_ROOT`, so a lane never runs `lares home tend`; `lares vessel` writes the scratch root alone.
# Build: `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile` only where a lead approved an install, then `pnpm -r build`, then `pnpm --filter @lararium/tw5 build:tw5-vendor` (the TW5 core blob that `head-parity` and the plugin smoke need), then `node tools/stamp-build.mjs`. A fresh `git worktree` holds empty submodules; populate them before building there.
# ''A founding wants a human route on a TTY.'' With no TTY it refuses `CredentialTtyRefusal`, and no flag or environment variable carries a credential. A harness founds through the test door: `tools/test-scratch-marker.mjs` stages the root, `tools/seed-test-custody.mjs` names the published test route, then `lares vessel found --skip-build`; `tools/unlock-test-custody.mjs` opens a marked scratch root. An agent may read vessel state and hand the operator the next founding command as text; the operator performs each founding and custody act. A walk that meets `CredentialTtyRefusal` routes through this test door, and never through a terminal driver that types a credential; a walk brief names the door.
# ''Every boot rests at the floor.'' A verb that needs sovereign state refuses at a standing floor and names `lares vault unlock`. A harness that reads the first `gate key:` line of a log reads the carriage relay's key where a relay stands.
# ''The container'': `tools/lares-container.sh` offers `up`, `down`, `found` and `logs` (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/harbor`>>). ''The browser surface'': `tools/browser-ci.sh` over `docker-compose.browser-ci.yml`, one project and image tag per builder (`BROWSER_CI_PROJECT`, `BROWSER_CI_IMAGE`).
# The tw5 plugin bundle (`packages/lararium-tw5/plugins/lares-memetic-wikitext.json`, `plugins/standalone/*`, `src/plugin-tiddler.generated.ts`) rides in git and must equal a fresh build: rebuild after any tw5 source or tiddler change and commit it with the change. CI's `build-freshness` job checks it. `genesis/` carries that bundle, the boot shadows and the engine blob by address, and the wiki inside a founded vessel runs the bytes genesis carries; a bake (`pnpm --filter @lararium/node build:genesis`) lands as its own labelled `GENESIS RE-BAKE` commit with the CAS change, and a vessel founded on the earlier seed re-founds. `tools/genesis-parity-witness.sh` reads the gap: a bundle that runs ahead of genesis reads red until a bake closes it or an entry in `genesis/unbaked.json` declares it.
# `lares-cli` `tests/pour-repave.test.ts` needs the dev-root marker (`LAR_DEV_REPO_ROOT=1` or the ignored `lar-dev-root.json`).
# Crew: <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/mixed-model-swarm`>> holds the seats, shapes, seat law, battery and lanes; the solo chain runs beside as many side lanes as hold disjoint files. A lead copies its resume sheet out of the session scratch directory after each commit: a restart empties that directory.

!!! The standing law that bites ~ read the carrier before arguing with it
* ''The frame stands as canon'': DOCTYPE, the SOH bearing, STX, `toml meta` first in the body, the body, ETX with its `ni:///sha-256;…` check, EOT. One span per carrier; a spelling outside the frame grammar tears the frame (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/memetic-wikitext-framing`>>).
* ''The frame's `?` and the gate's unechoed key stay distinct.'' `from="?"` declares an unresolved bearing at the grammar layer; the gate withholds a known key at the transport layer. Their one shared law reads at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/system-pattern-integrities#/threshold-pattern-integrities`>>.
* ''Ingest decides from a parse and a verdict.'' The deserializer stands as the pure meme-to-tiddlers function; `verdict()` (Match · Stale · Absent · Torn · Bare) lives in `@lararium/memetic-frame`; checks live in `carrier-check.ts`, repairs in normalize, policy at each door. A stale check on a human edit warns; a torn frame refuses; text with no frame reads as bare data, never a meme.
* ''Never refuse on the stock syncer path''; surface on the alert rail (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/syncer-back-parity`>>).
* ''The quoteblock floor fences at two grains'': the whole chunk inside the deserializer, and a child's body in place at the child gate (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/ahu#/quoteblock-floor`>>).
* ''An admit carries across a seal roll only through its roll anchors.'' The gate reads the presented admit against the carriage board's counted revokes and the kapae antigen, and folds no allow roster (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/membership-doctrine`>>).
* ''No global now'' and ''the fence'': #/rulings.
* ''Grounds before wins'': rank work by what inherits shape from what, never by confidence in the next wire (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/working-procedures#/grounds-before-wins`>>).
* ''One holder per store, one Lararium per human'': a second holder meets `StoreHeld` across containers and namespaces (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/harbor`>>).
* ''Fragments'': every `lar:` fragment roots at `#/…`, one raw `#`, `/` nests, `$` reserved (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/memetic-wikitext`>>).
* ''Grammar lives in the tiddlers'' (`packages/lararium-tw5/tiddlers/sigil-*.tid` generate `grammar-table.generated.ts`); sigil heads stay core ASCII; `aka` pins as information, `kanawai` pins as binding, `kahea` transcludes live.
* ''Early alpha'': no back-compat, no shims (one ruled exception: `G47`'s three sensorium placeholders), no legacy recovery; a stale thing aligns to the current frame and grammar, and a third human's arrival ends that line (#/rulings, the fence) (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/wild-bytes`>>). Derive, never enumerate. One function, one relation.
* ''A registry compiled about subjects refuses at any scope'' (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/registry-filter`>>).
* ''A relation's evidence keeps its type'' and ''a worker keeps its own storage scope'': <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/causal-island#/selected-contracts`>>.
* ''Shared tree'': never `git stash`; commit only your paths; never `--no-verify`; a spirit spawns no helper and never juggles another hand's uncommitted hunks; read an exit code directly; prove a zero with a known positive (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths#/law`>>).
* ''The node side routes nothing into the shore's files'' while a shore lane holds them: `packages/lararium-browser/**`, `packages/lararium-web/**`, `tw5-host-bridge.ts`, `tw5-projection.ts`.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/rulings`>>

!! Rulings ~ the operator's, as approved

!!! The live test and the fence
* ''One operator stands the hearth; two hearths stand the first Nexus'' (operator, 2026-11-07: //"We stand the hearth here as one operator with many faces. Later Freyja and I stand the first multi-hearth Nexus (2 household caps larariums in relation)."//). The founding stands one operator's household Lararium, that operator wearing many faces; the operator's daughter stands her own household Lararium later, and the two in relation form the first multi-hearth Nexus (`M8`). The face-switch act, the cabal plane, the custody seal and the engine fold land before that second hearth.
* ''The fence'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-16). Early alpha runs while one or two operators hold the mesh and ends when a third human's data and habits enter it; after that, data deserves a migration path. The second operator takes the seat beside the first as a QA tester who knows the mesh stays a sandcastle until then. As practice, not law, the destroy-class and shape-changing acts land before the second seat where they can: the engine re-mint and the genesis re-bake; the carrier and key-class changes; the Stable re-found (AID, charter, seat); every signed-shape change; every load-bearing rename.
* ''A working Lararium comes first'': Live Wiki Browser -><- Live Node, under manual QA on one machine. Sensorium work waits behind that milestone.
* ''A founding road carries its own succession.'' A reconstitution kit lets another competent operator stand a node and read its grammar; a rite held in one operator's head alone carries no succession.
* ''Face plurality stands as a day-one requirement.'' Each of the two humans wears at least three faces, each with a public Handle, on the first Nexus.

!!! The house as a container
* ''All CODE runs dockerized''; the host keeps only the hooks into the operator's environment (files and bags, session capture, the editor and CLI entry, custody).
* ''ONE Lararium per HUMAN, never per face.'' A human's faces keep their data together in one vessel (`PRIVATE-all, PUBLIC-one`); the recorded face-switch act carries plurality. No design assumes a container or a state mount per face.
* ''A SESSION wears the face; the vessel wears none'' (operator-ruled; quote license: //"a human might want a tab open as one Persona, and a tab open as another Persona... same vessel. Identity as relation."// and //"a CLI session wears its own face too … those act as ephemeral UX"//). A vessel holds its roots and one store. A session — a browser tab, a CLI run — contracts in as ONE persona, and several sessions may live at once over one vessel, each wearing its own. Every act carries exactly one face. Two faces live from one vessel never link on the wire: each session proves itself under its own face-scoped key, never under a key the vessel shares across them. The vessel-wide parts (custody, the daemon island) serve every session and let none read another's planes.
* ''What survives a re-founding'' stays DEFERRED until after the two-hearth Nexus (`M8`) (operator, 2026-11-08: //"Defer the archive, but note this tension ('what shall survive a re-founding, faces-bundles, emergency situations, restores from backups, etc') to the roadmap for after the 2-operator nexus."//). A re-found clears the house root's `hearth/` alone (`tools/lares-container.sh reset`), so the keys, the charter's AID and every CRDT history go with it, while `projections/` (every plaintext render, the private daemon and kahua folders among them), `sensoriums/`, `library/` and `config/` stand; the open questions hold together: a reset that archives the sealed root instead of deleting it, the face bundle carried across a re-found, emergency recovery, and restore from backup. The immediate half stands in code: essential user data and the AI memory stage reach disk as plaintext projections under `projections/`, the temp and draft layers ride behind a consent record, and the house root splits by survival class, so a bundle and a re-import carry them across (the survive-refound leap).
* ''Local-first first.'' Cloud "backup larariums" under other humans' delegated keys stand DEFERRED until the operator can deploy to a cloud surface. Nothing in the container or the face-switch work may foreclose a keyless ciphertext carrier later.
* ''The boundary'' (settled by the house under those rulings): one multi-arch image; `/workspace` mounts bags and wikis, `/state` mounts vessel homes and custody, never an image layer; the local socket stays INSIDE the container and the host CLI enters through `docker compose exec`; the app never receives the docker socket; mDNS offers unauthoritative LAN candidates and a pinned admission alone grants authority; DNS-01 wildcard TLS supplies browser secure contexts; a release manifest binds the image digest to the genesis, engine and grammar CIDs.

!!! No global now
* ''A wall clock never decides whether an act runs.'' Abandonment, revocation and expiry ride causal events; a timer serves only its holder's own liveness, and a device clock may sit under an adversary's hand. Web2.5 timers (TLS acceptance, OAuth tokens) sit at the edge of the causal island.
* ''Lapse rides a causal fence'': an epoch roll, a burn marker, or causal supersession at a verified relation fence. A replica missing that history reports an incomplete view, never "expired"; quiet never means lapse.

!!! The Stable state and its consequences
* ''Every Lararium holds a charter.'' One operator's charter reads as a SEED and two operators' seats in one charter read as a NEXUS (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/seed-and-nexus`>>). A household seed never grows into a Nexus; a Nexus founds fresh at threshold two of two. One "I" may hold several PersonaGroups. A Nexus grows by adding seats through its own rolls.
* ''A household seed serves its holder alone.'' A one-operator seed gives durable use without recruiting a mesh; useful local standing precedes every wider relation.
* ''Guests walk the household seed.'' Planes stay persona-scoped, so a guest sees its own documents, the public and infrastructure shelf, and every realm its worn face stands in; a face may stand in many, as a person belongs to many clubs, and a guest's other faces stand in none of them.
* ''Add-a-face (A).'' The daemon writes the public half; the terminal seals the recovery share; seating stays a separate act.
* ''Every leaf starts at the floor with its own root.'' A human may later JOIN a leaf to a PersonaGroup so its devices sync as one fleet; joining never takes the device's own root away.
* ''Founding persona zero belongs to standing'', never to a later verb. A capable vessel founds its household seed, so a "vessel with no charter" reads Mu.
* ''Stable folds into the re-found'' as its core.
* ''Forks report per board'': every presenter names every fork on the board it reads (the KERI watcher shape).

!!! Planes ~ cabal, realm, Nexus
* ''CABAL names the collective, REALM holds shared resources, NEXUS names the federated hardware mesh.'' The Nexus `@crossroads` board serves public-infrastructure docs and the Kahu Cabal keeps them. An operator's offering to a book club belongs in that club's realm, signed by the face worn there.
* ''Kahu drafts in the realm the charter derives''; publishing to `@crossroads` stands as a separate, recorded promotion act. A second Kahu realm gets made only for a genuinely different relation.
* ''Promotion approves the EXACT BYTES'' (hash, purpose, surface) under a threshold the charter sets, with a higher bar for charter, admission and trust-root changes. A CID, a clerk's edit or an active persona never stands in for collective approval.
* ''Steward signing.'' A scoped face signs acts that belong to one person; a transparent quorum Handle signs acts the collective owns; the root never appears publicly. A human wears MANY faces and many public Handles, each in many cabals, and signs a steward edge with the face or Handle worn IN THAT CABAL.
* ''Oracle answer'': one public answer per Nexus, signed by that Nexus's Kahu steward; a herm carries and presents it and signs nothing public. The engine floor rides as one shared content-addressed bake, and each answer names its floor CID (operator ruling).
* ''Herm tending'': tending may reach the herm's key; the house accepts that reach and names it (operator ruling).
* ''Introduction and admission'': a user introduces; an operator admits; the answer consumes the introduction (operator ruling).
* ''Names and counters'': a counter orders one writer's own emissions only, and the face lives at the session capability. The per-Nexus convergence secret and its keyring leave the model: nothing derives from one, the Stable founding act yields none, no seat act commits to one and no admission delivers one. (operator ruling; <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/cap-tier#/key-delivery`>>).
* ''Kahu acts fall into four chartered classes'': ordinary (1-of-1); high-impact — charter, admission, trust root (3-of-5, or 2-of-3 for a small body); emergency (2-of-3, a narrow pre-authorized power reviewed by a later causal act); revocation (the threshold of the original act). A challenge appends to the record and may pause LOCAL reliance; it never halts the protocol. Admission of a Kahu or a Lamplighter always takes the seat quorum, and no ordinary single-seat class covers it; kapae and rolls take the quorum too; the user road rides its own bound (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/seed-and-nexus`>>).
* ''Renewal runs as three sequences, kept apart'' (the realm feed, a per-renewal causal position, the KEL), and lapse arrives as three distinct events: signed departure, recorded removal by relation-scoped kapae, renewal supersession.
* ''A cancel crossing a herm yields three receipts'': the caller's abandonment, the relay's delivery (transit only), the vessel's outcome. A caller timeout never counts as a cancel; eviction answers `retention-gap`.
* ''One face at a time names WHO ANSWERS for an act.'' The switch law reads in `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/persona-policy#/one-face`.
* ''A session wears the face; the vessel wears none'' (operator ruling).

!!! Custody ~ the S key
* One S key, minted at the Stable founding, lives only inside LUKS-style keyslots, each a Clevis `sss` tree. Pin kinds: passphrase · paper (SLIP-39) · ember · tpm (one share only).
* ''EMBER'' names the Tang-shaped, per-relation device pin; it holds no share of S key. Only the blinded points cross on unlock.
* ''A backup route stands at every tier.'' Every keyslot tree, household or civic, keeps a human route (passphrase or paper); bind refuses a tree without one. Embers never stand as the only way in.
* ''The floor key keeps the vessel's public name''; the sealed custody root derives the veil and the sovereign caps. The veil change stays destroy-class.
* ''Unbind offers an S-key rotation loudly'', every time, never folded in silently.
* ''Locality law'': an unattended ember answers on the local link only, and only where the hearth holds a hardware freshness anchor the ember checks on each ask (#/rulings, the ember's key at rest); a relay pin wants an attending hand. The vessel rests at the floor and raises when t embers answer; the raise lapses by non-renewal, which reads as the raise's next causal boundary (#/rulings, how a raise lapses).
* ''HOT ⊥ COLD'': S key and keyhive state stay hot; the root, the recovery shares and the next-root seed unwrap per act.
* Measuring hardware x-only ECDH (phone enclaves) before trusting an enclave-held ember: approved, deferred.

!!! The hosted walk (F1)
* ''(c′) adopted.'' The HOST's gate reads and burns invites. The gate sorts every socket into same-operator · contracted · walker · stranger; under PRIVATE a stranger meets silence before any session frame. The guest carries its hosting grant; the hearth stores nothing about it. Invites mint by blind POPRF evaluation, so who-invited-whom stays non-computable. Revocation runs by kapae of the guest's leaf, by non-renewal, or by an epoch roll. Sybils meet tolerance, never detection.
* ''The inviter's face never signs an invite'': that signature would link the inviter at redemption. The hearth's blind evaluation carries the proof instead.
* ''V — vesting by class.'' A guest the HOST invites holds floor 1 at once; a guest a WALKER invites vests at the host's next roll. The roll reads as the household's consent to grow, and purpose labels separate the two classes without naming the minter. The built allowance carries the pre-answered dial of the user road's three; the operator's answer to a user's introduction stands owed, and until it lands a walker's token earns standing with no answer (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/membership-doctrine#/the-invite`>>).
* ''W — the hearth carries a walker's own documents'' as sealed ciphertext (carry ⊥ read), under a quota per guest. The PersonaGroup holds the durable copy; hearth carriage stays a convenience, never a backup.
* ''W reclaim.'' Quota pressure alone triggers it; lapse reads as a RATIO against the guest's own rhythm between contacts, so the host's roll cadence cancels; the most-lapsed-for-itself goes first; notice on the guest's next contact comes before any discard. The ruling sets aside counting the host's rolls: it measures the host's diligence, and diligence confers no title.
* ''N — a node may walk.'' The sorter sorts by proven relation, never by device type.

!!! The cleanup of the hosted walk (B1)
* ''The worn face hosts and walks.'' No roster order picks anything; a vessel wearing no face hosts and walks nowhere. Before its first hosting act in a Nexus, a roll refuses when the hosting doc holds an act this face signed that no local state holds.
* ''A herm answers the knock.'' A charterless herm answers any proven peer that knocked (`ownPlacePosture`): a waystone by class, never a broken lararium. A lararium reads its charter, and an absent or torn charter reads PRIVATE.
* ''A boot follows `LAR_ROOT`.'' A vessel isolated under `LAR_ROOT` projects into that root, never into the tracked tree; the mirror roots never read `LAR_BAGS`, so the isolation instruction names `LAR_ROOT`.
* ''Compartments outrank admission control.'' A walker reads its own hearth's hosting doc, never the carriage board: the carrier set stays a contract ledger read by those who hold contracts.

!!! The first ceremony, and the acts around it (operator ruling)
* ''Guests'': the first ceremony runs with the host's own invites only. The hosted road builds whole before the ceremony (#/the-attractor): its doors stand built and stay shut on the day.
* ''Reach on the day'': LAN only. The public domain's Handle table comes later.
* ''At two seats no seat signs any Kahu act alone.''
* ''An operator's own herm'': an operator contracts their own herm with one signature, scoped to one place and one Nexus, and it falls with a kapae of the operator's admit.
* ''The hosting key'': the operator's seat signs ONE delegation to a hosting key; that key signs each hosting act and seeds each epoch. A session wearing any other face signs nothing for the hearth.
* ''The cold-key card'': the house format — 64 hex plus a checksum, in groups of four — printing its purpose, chair label, generation and a short charter fingerprint.
* ''The DNS credential'': the registrar's credential rests as two files on the first operator's always-on node, outside both mounts, read by the one-shot issuer. That node issues one wildcard per machine, and the second machine receives its certificate by hand. After the hearth stands, move the credential into the vault's Porkbun VEIL slot (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/reach-plane`>>).
* ''A browser leaf holds a passphrase or paper slot before it holds a persona root''; the mint refuses without one.
* ''No recovery contacts on the day'': each face starts one-of-one on its own paper, and enrolment comes later by one typed first-enrolment event the face's own current key signs on its own log.
* ''Shore saves, as built'': a rename onto a standing title proceeds as an ordinary write at field grain; a tombstone moved into a bag that lacks the title refuses unless the hand asks to shadow.
* ''A seat gets ADDED, never replaced.'' The long-span act adds a holder to BOTH cabals — a Kahu seat and a Lamplighter contract — and the founders keep their chairs; one founding Kahu may hold seats across many Nexuses. The road proves the roll that adds a seat, and the new holder's carriage contract, first. A passed chair stays a possible act. A Kahu who works far from the other seats needs a co-signature for every quorum act, so the partial-and-cosign door carries a partial act across a relay and back with no clock.
* ''Per-Nexus identifiers'': an admit names a per-Nexus identifier, a random leaf no other Nexus can link. The face's lineage may re-issue it by a signed hand-over the gate verifies, and per-Nexus keys mint at random, never from the persona seed.
* ''Every boot rests at the floor until a human unlocks'' (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/waking-floor`>>). At the floor every fleet lane stays shut, and the custody write, receipt and dyad capabilities of the island record answer the daemon island alone. A bounded ember stays design input: it raises to SERVING alone, never signs or admits, runs on a count and never a clock, needs the device's own unlock plus a second signal, records each raise, and any other device of the human removes it.

!!! The models, each in its own carrier (operator rulings, approved as presented)
* ''Full models land early'': #/the-attractor.
* ''One roadmap'': this carrier. Standing procedures rest at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/working-procedures`>>, and the grammar watch at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/grammar-watch`>>.
* ''The sealed store'': cleartext lives in the live wiki and the operator's projected files alone. Private bytes take randomized authenticated sealing addressed over the ciphertext; the private tier takes no convergent sealing, and no opener returns unauthenticated bytes. A content key carries a version and rolls at a causal removal act. History at admission must have stood declared when written, and one logical bag name carries its key-roll epochs (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store`>>).
* ''The backup bundle'': a bundle holds ONE face, sealed under a route the person holds, and carries that face's root with its own lines and no key of any seat. Its memory rides as content keys, sealed inside the key island: a bundle opens every surviving value ever sealed under a version it carries, values written after its cut included, and no version minted later. A restore runs as a recovery act that takes a fresh veil and a new seat, and one face on a new device reads as the same audience for the versions that face already held (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody`>>).
* ''Removal's reach'': four edges take a marker (dyad, vouch, member, carriage). A vouch edge answers to its voucher and to a chartered quorum; a seal roll carries every shadow; a standing shadow reaches every gate and drives a key roll; a contested tie keeps the shadow up; mutual removal ends in an explicit fork (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae#/four-edges`>>).
* ''The PersonaGroup stands as a Keyhive group'', so a seat's revocation rolls the key of every bag the group reaches (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae#/keys`>>; a fresh group stays owed, `K12` of #/stride-1-spine).
* ''The hands'': every hand opens a session. A tasked spirit comes down to an AI, a Mask and the vessel's seat, attended or standing; the vessel's own key serves as a spirit's system identity; a Mask counts as no pet-name. The door carries and the hand authorizes, over one verb table, and a standing seat never signs an act that changes who holds authority (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/session-contract#/the-hands`>>).
* ''Sealed-record acts ride the custody door'', never a store verb whose output lands in the daemon's document in the clear (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/waking-floor#/the-shape`>>).
* ''Gate keys come in three tiers'': a floor route's keys derive from the floor key, and a sovereign route's from the sealed custody root (operator ruling); the serving route's bundle derives from SK, a third tier that leaves those two untouched (#/rulings, the ember's serving gate). A faceless place takes a fourth arm at the gate, the place arm, and a session opens before its certificate. The floor key rests outside the vessel key, and the face proof names what a holder of the disk without the passphrase reaches and never reaches (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof#/the-gate-exchange`>>).
* ''The widget render boundary stands withdrawn'', and the parser carries recovery (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/graceful-parsing`>>).

!!! Operator rulings, 2026-10-16 ~ approved as the controller's story leans
* ''A joined device reads the hearth's corpus and keeps its own.'' A joinee mounts the hearth's `@lararium` corpus read-only beside its own, and its writes stay in its own; the oracle plane (the hearth's catalog, daemon doc and faces' planes) never crosses (`device-admit.ts#admitCanonDocs`). The admit names that canon for the face it seats (`faceCustodyGroup`): a device of a face outside the Kahu cabal carries no canon name, mounts no document the hearth's wire holds back from it, and reads house canon from the floor, as that face does at the hearth. Item `W21` carries the build.
* ''The bag plane hides its creator before strangers carry it.'' The carried bag event's creator identifier in the clear gets measured red-first and ranked before wave three: ahead of anything that lets a third human's data cross a herm. Item `W25`.
* ''An agent's dissent drafts; a person's ask edits'' (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/session-contract`>> #/the-hands): an agent never overrides a person's edit, and a person's direct ask to edit a tiddler makes the edit the person's own act, bounded by the ask and the asker's authority.
* ''The system wikis release; every other wiki reads the floor'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-16). Only each Nexus's Kahu cabal writes the house canon live (`lares`, `lararium` and every disk-sourced holding but the board; 2026-11-11 below). Every other wiki, and every other hand, mounts them read-only by CID: the latest release that cabal approved. The release rides the Nexus's crossroads board as its public answer, naming the floor CID under the Kahu steward's seal. The cabal approves the exact bytes under its charter's threshold, and a herm carries the answer without signing it. Every wiki mounts that floor through `@oracle`, which never writes. A vessel moves its floor pin only when it accepts a newer signed release. With no Nexus speaking, it stands on its last accepted floor, or on genesis. The VESSEL pins the floor, one pin per (vessel, Nexus) (2026-11-11 below), so one vessel may hold two Nexuses' floors, and a face switch moves no floor. An edit aimed at the floor copies up into the wiki's own working layer, and an offering back rests as a signed entry on the Nexus's offer table beside the cabal's realm, never in it, where the steward's hand promotes it (the house's decision, 2026-11-13, overrulable: the road takes the built door's form, since realm drafts would seat the offerer inside the Kahu realm; <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/offering#/the-canon-offer`>>). A lone hearth is a Nexus of one, so its operator stands as its own Kahu. Item `W26` carries the build.
* DreamDOM's ruled questions stand in <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/dreamdom`>> #/open-rulings.

!!! Operator rulings, 2026-10-18
* ''Each browser tab asks for its own unlock.'' A tab that attaches to the shared holder opens the keystore by its own ask of the person; the holder carries no unlock between tabs. The reason reads off the session law: a session wears its own face, so each tab answers for its own opening (#/rulings, a session wears the face). The ask admits a tab to its face's open hive and derives no key: the holder keeps a face's keys while some session of that face stands unlocked, and the last to close locks it (operator, 2026-10-18; <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/session-unlock-2026`>> #/what-both-faces-hold). Item `B1` carries the build.
* ''A face's secrets never rest clear, even at a first join'' (operator, 2026-10-18, approving the house lean). A fresh device's fleet join binds a passphrase or paper slot before the join lands, since every face secret refuses to rest outside the slot tree. Item `B1`.
* ''On the node, the boot marks the unlock and the session marks the face'' (operator, 2026-10-18, approving the house reading). The per-tab ask binds browser tabs; a node unlocks once per boot through its custody door, and every CLI run, MCP seat and watcher proves its own face at the gate without asking again (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/session-unlock-2026`>> #/the-node-scope).
* ''The last tab closes, and the browser rests at its floor'' (operator, 2026-10-18, approving the house lean). The unlock lives in memory alone (the browser vessel's own worker, never the page: #/rulings, a page never stands as a vessel), so the browser vessel's sovereign composition ends with its last tab and the next tab asks again. Item `B1`.
* ''One Keyhive identity per PersonaGroup, none above them'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-18: //"it's the only way to retain control over faces that don't know about each other"//). Each face's PersonaGroup owns its Keyhive identity, its event store, its registry, its archive and its content-key seat; no vessel-wide identity registers a face's bags, delegates them, or holds a face's group. Two faces of one vessel then never link on the wire, a removal from one face rolls that face's bags alone, and a bundle restores one face and nothing of another. The tree holds `K15`'s steps 0 to 3 and 6, as read in code (one hearth observation still owed): a face's hive stands on its own atom, its own event-store scope and its own face door (`makeFaceBagDoor`), and the vessel identity registers only vessel-ground bags; the research meme records the earlier measure (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/keyhive-per-persona-group-2026`>>). House readings under the operator's confidence, each naming what flips it: a herm keeps a floor-only transport key, behind a red test that it can neither author, resolve nor observe a face's capabilities, and holds no Keyhive identity if that test fails; several face hives share one process only after a substrate probe reads them isolated, and each face's hive runs in its own worker otherwise; co-mingled history migrates by fresh identities and an old mixed archive refuses by name, with no bridge; a founder that cannot leave its group leaves by a fresh PersonaGroup. Item `K15` carries the build, folding `W25` and `K12`; `W26`'s slice 3 waits on its face context.
* ''A cabal shares through the faces its members choose'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-18, on the Teshima Family Council story). Kalani founds a private family council; Leilani, already standing on the DreamNet with several faces, chooses which of them join it, and a face she keeps back never learns the council exists. The council's hive reads as a Keyhive group with no key of its own: its members act through their own faces' identities, so nothing stands above them, and a presentation reads valid against the current set without naming which member made it (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/identity-classes`>> #/the-handle-chain). Any current member's face promotes into the council's canon; a graft takes the threshold; a face leaves by its own act, and another's removal rides `kapae` at the threshold or a fork that omits the captor (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/cabal-realm`>> #/the-unswept-corner); members read the owner-set as faces, never as humans. A private council declares no heraldry and lives in its members' address books under pet-names (cabal-realm #/heraldry). Item `K16` carries the story as `K15`'s acceptance.
* ''The asker seals'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-19, F6's custody fork). Once the browser holder owns the Repo and the daemon kernel, it routes each sealed write to the admitted tab whose session caused it, else to any admitted, unlocked tab of that write's face; with no awake admitted tab, the write waits, named `seal-awaits-session`, never silent and never decided by a clock. No key material leaves a tab, and each sealer holds the record-class key the occupied carrier's overwrite proof opens. A SharedWorker carries no promise to run while its tabs freeze, and no `MessagePort` event reports a peer's death (round one, 2026-10-19), so a seal that waits names its wait.
* ''A face's custody records seal under its own key'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-19). Each PersonaGroup holds an independent random face custody key (FCK) that only its own slot tree opens (passphrase, paper or PRF), never wrapped under the vessel key, so a holder of the S key or of another face's unlock opens none of its records. Face-private records: each face's veil archive, face-key custody, recovery contact sets, face-provisional, the fleet-join address secret and record, a pending join bound to its target face, the per-face device shares, and each face's partition of the handle book and roster; the vessel Keyhive archive splits per PersonaGroup under `K15`. A face bundle carries that face's identity, archive, FCK recovery route and recovery contact set, and nothing of a sibling. Read on 2026-11-14 and never walked: a lit sibling hands the worn face its cap events at every light (`operator-daemon-behavior.ts`), so each face's event store and archive name the other face, and a bundle cut from either carries that naming; `C1`'s second cut runs the hand-off only where a share entry names the pair. Today every hot and cold custody record seals under one S key (`vault-carriers.ts`, `browser-custody.ts`), so face isolation reads as routing alone until this lands. Item `K15` carries the build, and `F6` slice three lands with its step 3. How a vessel-common act unlocks dissolves (next ruling).
* ''A shared act never makes a shared secret'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-19; four ratcheted research rounds, <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/custody-per-face-2026`>>). Every act that touches more than one face sorts one of three ways: its private bytes DISSOLVE into the owning face's records (D), its public bytes stand at the FLOOR by design (F), or it names a vessel SECRET (S) whose principal and opener the house names record by record. A mixed carrier splits before it sorts. The browser holds no vessel key: founding, fleet join, slot changes, roster, archives, handle book, dyads, restore and clear sort D or F, so each tab asks only its own face's unlock and public floor work asks nothing. Phones, installed web apps, probes, the CLI, MCP and watcher seats and a native shell hold no S. A tablet waking floor carries ciphertext and public records unattended and decrypts no face's record. A container restarts at its floor and nothing unlocks it unattended. A guest island that owns a key store stands as its own vessel. A node holds S only for records it names: the serving key has no record yet, so it guards nothing today. * ''The place signs by a scoped signer under a pre-rotated KEL'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-19; a fifth research round, <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/custody-per-face-2026`>> #/the-place-signer). A node's place identity keeps a KEL whose inception commits the next key, and that next key rides the founding card beside the seed charter's cold next key, offline; the online place signer runs unattended for an enumerated allowlist of floor duties alone (the KEL's own events excepted, which the card's key signs), rests under the host's TPM-bound credential where the host offers one and an owner-only file elsewhere, and never rides an environment, an image or a log. A stolen online signer rotates away by the card; a lost card beside a stolen signer re-founds. The floor holds no custody key; it holds its own named place signer. The format change rides the founding bake (`X0`). The place KEL commits its next cold key on the founding card and rotates by that card (`W29`), and the seed census names every reach of the seed past the floor, K15's owed bonds among them (`tools/seed-census.mjs`).
* ''Custody and identity read by capability, never by host'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-19: //"I consider node and browser isomorphic-by-intent. Eventually, a browser-vessel somewhere may grow comparable capabilities to a node vessel, and this pattern cascades outwards to all future vessels."//). Three rules hold on every vessel, present and planned: a vessel meets each face through that face's atom alone, its veil signing every dial, join, enrolment, sibling channel and daemon proof made on that face's behalf, never one device-wide key; a vessel holds a place signer exactly while it stands a floor, for the floor's allowlisted duties, under the pre-rotated place KEL; and a faceless vessel reading another's floor proves nothing private, reading the public shelf under the floor card's pin. A node's daemon auth, device-key signer, sibling enrolment and group-secret opener read the one vessel seed today (`open-node-vessel.ts`), and a browser's do the same (`open-browser-vessel.ts`); each is a bond owed, carried by `K15`.
* ''A page never stands as a vessel'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-29: //"Approve your leans"//, after the mirror-thread talk-story). Every page holds a SURFACE with a port onto a vessel, in every setup: a browser on a node's own box, an anon-device browser vessel, a fleet of equivalent vessels. The no-VM-on-the-main-thread law reads "no sovereign VM and no key on a surface" (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/lararium-canonical-model`>> #/the-laws), and the DreamDOM mirror, a camera carrying a whole engine and no truth, honors it (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/dreamdom`>> #/open-rulings). Custody follows: the anon-device browser vessel keeps its opened key in its own worker, never in page memory (the per-tab ask stands, and the shared holder still carries no unlock); it seals its store through sealed-store steps 2 and 3; and it takes an unlock ceremony the surface cannot paint, the boundary sitting where the person types the secret (its shape stays research). The full mirror runs people's plugins only after those seal steps land for the browser store; no clear record stands under the full mirror, and the device key rests non-extractable. Browser persona roots already rest sealed once a slot binds (`browser-custody.ts`); only the place seed and veil archives may stand clear before a slot, and the opened tree key in page memory names the exposure this ruling moves.
* ''Identity enacts a bond'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-19). Lineage stays within a root, and covenant joins roots: a root (a compartment seed, a vessel key, a face custody key, a charter seat, a content key) derives children for its own duties alone, and any identity spanning two roots is a bond, an act both ends sign that seats a fresh key of its own, resting with the end whose privacy it serves and lapsing by its own act; no closure holds a root (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/identity-enacts-relation`>> #/identity-as-bond, its seven laws and bond table).
* ''A face's device veil is its own random key'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-19, re-reading the 2026-09-05 veil ruling). Each PersonaGroup's veil on a device mints at random when the device joins that face and rests under that face's FCK, so no device seed stands as an ancestor of any face's veil; it stays vessel-local, never presents, and reads mint-shaped to every observer. The group-secret opener follows it.
* ''Each realm stands under one charter, a seed's or a Nexus's'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-18). A council that meets at one hearth stands under that hearth's seed charter, and its guests' chosen faces walk there with no Nexus founded; a council two hearths carry stands under the Nexus those two found fresh, since a Nexus names the operators' relationship (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae`>> #/edge-hands). A guest sees every realm its worn face stands in.
* ''A write lands on a working layer, and canon takes it by promotion'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-18). A `LOAD` lands on a wiki's working slot alone; canon takes content only by the cap-gated promotion `MOVE` (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/wiki-layer-ontology`>>), and genesis and founding seed canon through their own named door. An act aimed at a wiki no island mounts asks the vessel to mount it, and refuses gracefully, naming the cause, when the mount cannot stand. Item `W27` carries the build.
* ''Every bag opens as its own wiki'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-18). The floor recipe names the bag alone over `@oracle`, derived and never minted, and an absent record reads as that floor, never as "no such wiki"; a composed wiki's record, which a hand authors, lays several bags and plugin offerings beneath the wiki's own canon bag (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/wiki-layer-ontology`>> #/quine). No act enumerates bags to mint wikis. Item `W27` carries the build.
* ''One owner per registry; the floor reads by CID'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-18: //"Architecture stands, leans stand"//). The `@oracle` document as a registry every vessel writes retires: one id carried one `lares` and one `lararium` pointer for the whole fleet, and a joined vessel's pointer replaced its hearth's, a shape a two-vessel probe and three shard runs measured. `@oracle` stands as the floor's read-face over a manifest CID, which a wiki mounts read-only by the pin its vessel accepted. Each registry carries only what its scope's owner writes: a vessel's bootstrap holds its own document pointers and its control home its accepted floor pins; `@catalog` the operator's documents; the Kahu realm the live `lares` and `lararium` documents and their drafts; the Nexus's offer table, beside that realm, the signed offers any face makes toward canon (the house's decision, 2026-11-13, overrulable: the road takes the built door's form; <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/offering#/the-canon-offer`>>); the crossroads board the Nexus's one public answer. The leans the ruling adopts: the Kahu realm holds the live system documents, with `bags/` projected on the Kahu's vessel alone; a one-seat founding lands a counted 1-of-1 Kahu-cabal admission for persona zero's group, so one predicate decides every hand's reach into system canon; the release answer, the Nexus's one public answer, names the Nexus AID, the charter epoch, the release before it and the floor manifest CID, sealed by the steward, with the promotion approval over the manifest's exact bytes riding attached; a vessel records an accepted release as one pin per (vessel, Nexus) in its control home that only a person's act moves (operator ruling 2026-11-11: the vessel pins the floor, faces pin nothing); genesis bakes the first floor, a corpus CID in the seed. A signing domain of its own carries the release, `floor-release`, so no seal over another shape verifies as one (operator-approved, 2026-10-18). The shape and its slices stand at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/w26-floor-lane`>>; items `W26`, `W21` and `P8` carry the build.

!!! Operator rulings, 2026-10-31
* ''Recovery contacts, custodians and the Handle'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-31: //"Approve recovery contact, and during this rewrite we should use Handle (& explain it) over 'name'."//). A person who holds a recovery key over one face's key history and signs its rotation reads as a ''recovery contact'', the field's own word for the role. A quorum of a face's recovery contacts rotates the face's root beneath its fixed KEL prefix, so the face keeps its ''Handle'': the public, self-certifying card a face publishes and others recognise by its own key, never by a registry and never by a pet-name (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/heraldry`>> #/ruling). A holder of one share of a root seed reads as a ''custodian'' (`CustodianTag` already says so). A custodian card returns memory, a recovery contact returns the Handle, and neither stands in for the other. ''Guardian'' keeps only its human sense (a child's legal guardian) and the fiction (the Lares as guardian spirits), and the Kahu keep their own name. Text a person reads says "your Handle" where it means what recovery restores, never "your name". The rename across canon, code and the CLI rides one lane, with a weld test before it.
* ''Two of three recovery contacts'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-31: //"2 of 3"//). A face enrols exactly three recovery contacts at threshold two: one lost contact leaves recovery standing, and no single contact can rotate the face alone. Enrolment refuses any other count or threshold by name. The face's own self-recovery digest counts as none of the three (operator, 2026-10-31, approving the house lean): it keeps its own road, a seed in hand rotating the face alone, so the quorum that can rotate a face never includes a key the person lost. Item `K18` carries the door.
* ''The memory-stage hearth comes first'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-31: //"I need a locally working node+browser joined (all vessels start at anon floors, so the full rites), so you all can stop using md in an untracked scratch dir & start using the daemon wiki as a 'memory-stage' (see neuroscience + Ai developments) for all design tracking and self improvement feedback loops."//). One operator's node and browser stand joined on one machine, each from its anon floor through the full rites, and the house tracks its design and runs its feedback loops on the daemon wiki in place of untracked scratch files. The rung leads the road (#/the-attractor, #/the-order); its items read in #/stride-0-hearth. House reading: the stage runs on plain tiddlers, tags and one index view, with no embedding, no vector index and no sidecar, so the sensorium arc's deferral stands as written (#/the-road, off this road).
* ''The daemon wiki holds the memory stage, and the sensorium waits'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-31: //"Pono, we raise the wiki (and hold the sensorium work). That all reads pono intent for the `daemon` wiki itself (the other wiki-domains serve different intents)."//). The hearth stride raises the daemon wiki as the house's memory stage; no other wiki domain takes that intent. The sensorium re-stand stays held behind it.
* ''The working surface is the repo checkout'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-31: //"Approve (b), the working surface IS the SDM repo checkout after all (but this should be configurable for other operators or myself to do a 'non-dev install' later)."//). The operator's house mounts the development checkout as `/workspace`, so its disk projection writes the tracked `bags/` and the `wikis/` beside it and git holds the memory stage's history. `LARES_WORKSPACE` keeps the choice an operator's own: a non-dev install points it at a corpus directory of its own (`L16`). Test and lane boots keep their scratch `LAR_ROOT` and never touch this surface.
* ''Narration lives in the wiki, and the flat memory folds into it'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-31: //"Once the wiki sits live and all agents can write memes to it, query it using filter language, etc, then we need to enforce that for all 'self-narration design and talk story' that used to end up on disk (and note in the ~/.claude 'memory system' that always get loaded that we intend to o-takiage memories from that flat file store to the wiki, and the main memory file should point directly to the MCP capabilities on its first line."//). `L17` gives the wiki its filter, `L18` fences narration onto it, `L19` folds the flat memory store into it.
* ''A single operator's re-found reads idempotent by intent'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-31: //"As git holds all history & refounding for a single operator reads idempotent by intent (we can freely tear down identity & costs at any time), we need this goal added to the overall roadmap. Combine the road/roadmap & reprioritize with these adds."//). While one operator holds the mesh, a re-found tears identity down freely: git holds the history, so a teardown costs nothing git cannot restore, and the same rites land the same house shape again under fresh keys. A re-found runs the way the fresh start runs, by hand and reversible, never through a pave or wipe command (#/fresh-start). It narrows no standing ruling, and the house reads it beside them: the fence holds, so the shape cuts land before the second operator seats a key and a re-found stays cheap until her pins depend on it (#/rulings, the fence); the live test still runs with two operators from its first day; after `X0` a re-genesis costs every CID a second operator pins (#/stride-3-bake), while a single operator's identity re-found before her seat costs nothing; old shapes still burn, so a format cut re-founds and no source reads pre-cut bytes (#/rulings, the fresh start). `X0b`'s idempotent-by-intent pass sweeps the code a re-found replays, and the memory stage keys its memes so a re-pour reproduces them (`L9`).
* ''The test re-cut moves before the founding'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-31: //"Approve lifting the test re-cut ordering to before founding"//). This lifts the 2026-10-29 ruling that set the re-cut after the founding (#/rulings, the test refactor). Red-first commits, the train's merged-tree run, CI as the verdict and one Sol read per train stay. Train T carries it (#/stride-t-recut).
* ''The re-cut's house leans, approved whole'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-31: //"Approve your leans on all forks"//):
** the composer's stage-cut runs before the founding only as a named single-owner step, after boot characterization tests (`T12` after `T6`)
** the dead-export sweep burns the 53 names nothing references; the 16 barrel re-exported names wait on their own ruling (`T4`)
** wave A and the pure readers open as lane slots free, four lanes at most
** shared stages live in `lararium-mesh` (transport-free stages) and `lararium-tw5` (keel stages), with no new package
** a witness pins that the glass mirror's plugin set never holds a daemon-only title (`T15`)
** the verb providers' matrix shaping lifts into a daemon-only `*-vm` module, and sensorium state stays in py (`T14`)
** cut order: the pure readers first, the doors and boot cells after `u1`, the share inputs after `ralias`
* ''The cut sorts by capability'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-31: //"Browser vessels do use all these caps, and may enact a 'node-like role' later, so hodl that isomorphic-by-intent-and-cap-stack design."//). Dials, relays, face-gates, the share verdict and the membership holders read as ''cap-stack stages'' behind ports named by capability (serves a gate · dials · relays · carries for others), never as a node shell. Only the substrate binding (thread kind, store kind, socket server) stands per host. No stage, port or package carries a host name (#/rulings, custody and identity read by capability, never by host).
* ''Every cut candidate takes a full cold read first'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-31: //"Pono, I want the 'cut candidates' to get batched Sonnet-scout-spirit cold-readers in full before we even touch them."//). No lane opens on a cut item (`T4`, `T5`, `T9` to `T14`, `T16`, `T17`) until a Sonnet cold reader, batched by seam, has read its whole span, never by excerpt, and its report stands. The read names what each candidate does, the state it shares, its callers, the laws it touches and what the cut risks; a lane brief cites it.
* ''Each cut read collides with a second reader'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-31: //"collide each spirit's findings with another Sonnet cold reader that has internet research caps, then land all of their 2-wave researches into the meme."//). Six wave-one readers read the cut candidates in full by seam; six collision readers re-read each span, ruled every finding confirmed, refuted or narrowed, searched outside precedent, and named what wave one missed. The train's kit holds what survived both waves (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/test-recut-road`>> #/the-reads).
* ''Stryker runs in a sandbox probe'' (operator approval, 2026-10-31: //"Approve the Stryker 10.1.1 probe in a scratch worktree"//). `inPlace` stays false, local reporters only.

!!! Operator rulings, 2026-11-01
* ''Train Q, the kupono grammar cut'' (operator approval, 2026-10-31, every house lean with the chiasmus folded in; scheduled 2026-11-01). One lane re-cut the whole sigil grammar and every bag before the memory stage takes its first tiddlers: the bare arrow left every sigil for `from=` and `to=`, no `@` follows whitespace, every sigil value takes a backtick under a name, the turn frame folds into `set lares=` with `aim`, `steer` and `yield`, and the turn harvesters burned. Its rulings, the `steer` name, the `slot=` and `action=` names, a steer carrying no `from` (operator, at the seed's gate: //"I don't think from= is needed inside a chiasmus."//) and the seed's ceiling lifted past 40K (operator: //"We can exceed the 40K ceiling if needed, as we now deterministically load the boot seed via hooks."//) among them, live in <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/grammar-watch#/the-kupono-cut`>>, with its one provisional clause on the turn frame. Landed, PR #149 (#/o-takiage).
* ''Before Q'' (operator rulings, 2026-11-01: //"Approve all this before Q"//, then //"Approve, hold the gh batching for later- enact all others now & flow in parallel fashion to the gh batching"//). The scratch fixture, the ten-job CI, the local merge queue and the native KDF landed ahead of ''Q'' (PRs #142, #145, #146, #147); the hosted merge queue's wiring, its trial and the span's ruleset wait on the operator's admin step and return as their own small lane, and ''Q'' opens without waiting on them. A local merge queue in the land tool (contract D) takes the hosted queue's model meanwhile (operator, 2026-11-01: //"I meant use their mental models locally to land our queue without the rebase waits."//). Local CI runners stay out (//"Reject local runners"//).
* ''The fast tier runs related tests'' (operator ruling, 2026-11-01: //"Approve the fast-tier lever"//). A lane's local fast tier runs each touched package's related tests, never its whole suite; whole suites, every e2e set and the slow witnesses run in the cloud (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/mixed-model-swarm`>> #/the-battery).
* ''The fiction and the art take their own o-takiage'' (operator ruling, 2026-11-01). The RPG chapters and the concept art keep an o-takiage rite fitted to their own wiki domains, designed from the pono sweep, and the lares design laws never bind them by default. Item `L20`.
* ''Train R, block and inline sigils at parsing parity'' (operator approval, 2026-11-01: //"Approve 1 + 2a(3 floor) cleanup of inline vs block sigils to tw5 pono parsing parity"//). A block sigil owns its line, the seal rides the ETX foot as a named value, a sigil whose `>>` leaves text after it reads inline as TiddlyWiki splits it, and trailing text reads as a recovered node with a diagnostic, never silent. Two addons ride it (operator, 2026-11-01): every code fence in the boot seed names its grammar, so the syntax plugin colours it (//"code-fenced blocks in the boot seed should get the grammar marked explicitly"//), and a fence opens and closes with more ticks than the longest run inside it. It follows ''Q'' as its own train and keeps the before-`L4` property. Landed, PR #150 (#/o-takiage).
* ''The legacy checks burn once the trains land'' (operator rulings, 2026-11-01: //"once this train goes through, we should o-takiage even those 'legacy checks' to get back to pono early-alpha evergreen state"//, then //"hold to alpha evergreen & after all this lands o-takiage the checks that deny stale/legacy forms. Especially the file-path tiddler field wards (pono to purge them, but it would then block legit user choices if kept)."//). A check that refuses only an old spelling burns; a law guarding a live invariant keeps. `file-path` retired as the house's meta field in ''Q'', so the name returns to user space as a plain field, and its deserializer deny, its `meme check` red and its witness leg burn with the rest. `uri-path` keeps (operator: //"`uri-path` serves the wiki as a toml-tiddler-field, keep that."//). A Sonnet cold reader, then a blind Opus security reader, sort the checks before the lane burns any (#/side-lanes, the legacy-check pyre).
* ''The deps move before the founding'' (operator rulings, 2026-11-01: //"I want to collide those old holds"//, then //"Approve both leans pending the trials, flow on"//). The engine holds read as claims under test: Automerge moves to 3.5.0 if its trial reads green, Keyhive to 0.6.0 as the one port before the founding if its trial shows the CGKA standing from the Archive and the port runs one or two days, else 0.3 holds; `vitest` 5.0.5 and the hygiene bumps ride the same lane. Subduction and Onomancy stay out, and a TiddlyWiki re-pin stays an epoch act. Landed, PR #151 (#/o-takiage): Automerge moved to 3.5.0, and Keyhive holds 0.3 on its trial's red; the tail rides #/side-lanes.
* ''The seal binds the head, paired with the phantom-record cure'' (operator ruling, 2026-11-01: //"Approve, pair it with the phantom-record cure"//). The seal covers STX through ETX, so the SOH head's `to=` and `namespace=` sat outside it, and a promotion's `carrierHash` bound the bytes, never the head address (''R''s security read, INFO). The head binds through the sealed meta: its `to=` answers to the meta `title`, else to `lar:///` joined to the meta `uri-path`; a split between them reads torn (`head-meta-split`); a meta naming neither reads head-unbound, shown by name and never read as a match or a tear; `namespace=` binds only where the meta carries one (the shape ruled by the house under the operator's delegation). The phantom-record cure rides the same lane (#/side-lanes, harden).

!!! Operator rulings, 2026-11-04
* ''The herm floor reframe'' (operator: //"Herms are the floor capabilities of all Lararia (a lifted herm floor + Pronaos = Node Lararium)."//). A Node Lararium holds a lifted herm floor and the Pronaos. The island capability record composes along the cap stack, floor courses first (`NodeHermResult.islandCaps` carries the floor record), the lift on top (`caps.receipt` names a LIFT capability, since wiki islands exist only on a lifted floor); a herm never stands as a party beside a vessel. Q1 (one answerer per `receipt:request`) landed in PR #157, and the follow lane carried the rest (PR #160, #/ashes `cap-stack`).
* ''The wards bind the daemon and kahua wikis; user wikis keep full caps'' (operator: //"We do want users to have full caps over their wikis, but the daemon/kahua wiki should get bound by these wards."//). A user's own wiki keeps promote, kāpae and lift advisory, and the AI drives every act beside the operator there. The daemon wiki and the kahua stage bind their code-bearing acts, StartupActions included: a StartupAction there runs from genesis or the promoted set alone, and its promotion rides the ward. Bound acts: `promote-commit`, `kapae-lower`, item 7's house cap and push lei. Free acts: `kapae-raise`, `promote-drop`, `orphan-drop` and `promote-keep`; a kahua lift rides the ward since 2026-11-05 (#/rulings). Item 7 builds the ward and the founding waits on item 7; PR #157 carries no interim refusal. The `lift.capability-tension` loop closes for daemon and kahua when the ward lands and stays open for user wikis.
* ''The commit relay and the lead mode stay as a dev tool with a documented trust boundary'' (operator, 2026-11-04). A lead's tree counts as trusted code, and the relay's reach beyond that stands as a ruled exception to the read that found it; the broker that replaced the relay's in-memory key holds the boundary (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/seat-harness#/the-trust-model`>>).
* ''Never a passkey'' (operator: a passkey //"binds us to un-pono web2.5 registries, we aim past that for pono web3+ DreamNet"//). No WebAuthn registry gates any act; the hand gesture rides keys the face holds.
* ''A fresh grant unlock per ward act''. The face grant key stays sealed while custody stands unlocked; each ward-gated act unlocks it fresh from the passphrase through the minted KDF port (native scrypt on the host, noble in a worker, identical bytes), signs once and zeroes it in `finally`. The unlock runs in the vessel's worker, never on a page.
* ''The co-sign binds by reach and ignores vessel class''. The cross-device co-sign binds acts whose effect leaves the device (`kapae-lower`, the house cap, push lei); a local `promote-commit` needs the fresh unlock alone. A device holds its own atom and custody, so a browser vessel and a node vessel count as two even on one metal, and two tabs of one vessel count as one. The ward reads devices and reach, never class, so a browser-only user fleet binds the same way; a one-device fleet needs the fresh unlock alone until a second device enrolls. A lost co-signing device pauses the cross-reaching acts until re-enrollment through the recovery rite.
* ''`T21` to `T25` land before `X0`'' (operator, 2026-11-04). The five test-recut security and correctness items precede the bake (#/stride-t-recut).
* ''`L19` lands in the kahua working top, then the hand lifts in batches'' (operator and house, talk-story). The daemon wiki stays control-plane only; memories follow the face. Items `L19` and `L21`.
* ''The harness settles before standing'' (operator: //"Settle the AI harness, and we can wrap that up and do deep reads and debugging after the wiki server stands up."//). Terra's nine rounds closed the harness; it lands by its own PR, and the deep read and debug pass resume after the wiki stands (#/the-order, rank 31). Item 7 enacts the wiki-daemon key cap at standing (operator: //"enact the full wiki-daemon key cap design now"//).
* ''A group kill refuses any pgid at or below 1'' (measured: a test fake with pid 1 turned the supervisor's group kill into `kill(-1, SIGKILL)` and ended every process the operator owns, twice on 2026-11-04). Every group kill rides one guard (`killGroup` in `tools/launch-codex.mjs`), test fakes use a large unused pid, and signal tests run inside a bwrap PID namespace.

!!! Operator rulings, 2026-11-05
* ''Flow to the standing'' (operator: //"Pono, Approve. FLOW to the standing of the first live Lararium."//). The founding line (#/the-order, rank 32): `S2`'s marker cure, `S2b` the browser ward, `S3-pre-b` the founding and join wiring of the per-face vessel tag, `S3b` the gate hook and the CI container (landed, PR #164) precede the rite, and `S4` to `S6` follow the standing. The house ruled `S3-pre-b` founding-critical under that delegation: a founding admission without the tag would refuse under the later law, and the only cure would re-found.
* ''Kahua lifts stay warded'' (operator, after the four-scale talk-story). At every scale the reach law holds: the working top serves as the free local floor the AI writes on, and a lift crosses devices, since the stage syncs to every device the face wears, so it takes the face's own hand, one passphrase per verified batch (`L21`), never an operator's or a temple's. Memory steers the spirit (the Lares read the kahua at cold boot), so it carries weight the way code does. Civic and shared stages compose several warded hands by threshold, and each plane's count rests on its own civic ruling (unbuilt). The ward travels with the face across temples with no registry. The batch lift serves as the household's feeding rite, its cadence wholly the person's own, with no timer and no nudge. This amends the 2026-11-04 line that named every lift free; the `lift.capability-tension` loop closes for the daemon and kahua wikis with the ward (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/dreamnet-architecture#/faces-fleets-and-the-ward`>>).
* ''Fleets overlap, with one worn face per device or session'' (operator; trued to the lit set, 2026-11-11 below). A human holds plural faces, each its own fleet, together a private cabal of one; one vessel may stand enrolled in several and keep several OPEN, each sealing under its own key, while it wears ONE, and no record links a human to a device. Distinctness reads by the face's own atoms: a co-sign answer counts only when its signer sits in that face's device set and differs from the asker. A vessel's key never enters a face admission, since that would link a person's faces. A per-face blinded vessel tag, `HMAC(tag secret, face group)` signed by the per-face tag key that HMAC seeds and unlinkable across faces, catches a vessel admitted twice into one face. The tag secret mints at random on the device, one per face, at the founding (the founder's face) or at the join request (the joinee's face), and rests in that face's own D record, vessel-local (`bundle: stays`): no place, floor or root seed derives it, so a stolen place seed computes no face's tag, a re-join on the same face presents the same tag, and a place rotation leaves every face's tag standing. The admission also carries the device's per-face WARD KEY (`wardKey`, under the tag key's signature; operator, 2026-11-06, approving the house's defaults): the public half of the key a fresh ward unlock of that face on that device opens, derived from the face's own ward key on a node and minted at random per face on a browser, so it links no two faces either, and beside it that key's own proof of possession (`wardPop`, its signature over (group, atom, ward key) under `ward-key-pop`, signed inside a fresh ward unlock), so a device that names another's key seats nothing; a co-sign approve counts only beside that key's signature over (the answer, the ask's digest, the fleet frontier the answerer read), and a rewrap changes the ward's wrapping, never that key. A memory moving between faces counts as a carry and needs both faces' hands. Remote lifts ride the intent plane: a proposal posts by digest with its body onto the face-private plane for review and loads as nothing, one warded lift intent lands on any vessel of the face, and the vessel holding the bytes verifies and moves them locally. Owed: a guard so no loader takes a proposal body on the plane for memory.
* ''The ward's reach'' (operator and house, talk-story). Every act that reads the persona-root seed and changes who belongs to the face or what the face trusts becomes a ward act: admit, fleet-join, recovery-contact enrolment, handle and nexus publication, the founder certificate, the seal ceremony and a holder's edge kāpae, beside the bound acts of 2026-11-04. A root-reading act measured to run unattended moves to the device-atom signature under the face grant chain and prompts for nothing. One prompt per act, never cached. A face founded before the ward refuses bound acts by name with its cure, and nothing rewraps silently or reads the old shape. The operator's real face is born with its ward key at the fresh founding. `F11`'s vault and admit verbs ride the same ward.
* ''Item 7's house calls'' (S3 to S6, talk-story, 2026-11-05). The swarm holders start through a ward act; a seat's reach to the unix socket gets assumed until `S4` measures it; the co-signing device fresh-unlocks too; set-aside devices count toward the device total and never co-sign; with no capability descriptor the relay refuses at start and writes nothing; the receipt trust root names the daemon's vessel key as a residual; the house cap and lane shares sync fleet-wide on the face-private board, while go, roster and receipts stay device-local. The design rests in <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/seat-harness#/the-swarm-slices`>>.
* ''The act ward'' (operator asked for the full floor). On the daemon and kahua wikis, an action string runs in a throwaway copy of the wiki, its recorded diff is judged, and a clean diff applies as itself; a code-bearing `meme_act` there refuses outright until the ward stands. User wikis run acts live with full caps. The design rests in <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/kahua-memory-stage#/the-hearth-lane`>>.
* ''Browser and Node join the founding'' (operator, 2026-11-05: //"Browser + Node is part of founding."//): the browser vessel lift (`B1`, its carrier `pono/browser-vessel-lift` on its own lane) rises before the rite, and the joined node and browser founding gate precedes the operator's hands.
* ''Land all before the rite'' (operator, 2026-11-05: //"Land all before the rite, no 'build a now, b later'."//): the owed items and every lane in flight land first (#/the-order, rank 32).
* ''Tabs carry live sessions; one face per session comes later'' (operator, 2026-11-05: //"Multiple tabs will carry live sessions, and later can change face (ONE FACE per session later on the roadmap)."//): each tab holds its own unlocked session now; the face a session wears changes by the recorded switch act on the road's later `ONE FACE` stride. The slice design rests in `pono/browser-vessel-lift` on its lane.
* ''The ceremony document is approved'' (operator, 2026-11-05): the clean ceremony document takes every secret over a service-worker-brokered port (`B1` slice 1b); no secret crosses the page's own thread.
* ''"Realm" names the DreamNet realm alone'' (operator, 2026-11-05): a JavaScript execution context reads as a thread, never a realm.
* ''Readers on call'' (operator, 2026-11-05): Terra and Luna readers run when a lane needs them, and one online reader runs once, approved; the Codex pool floor of the Terra ruling below still decides each launch.
* ''The 180 s CI-read pace'' (operator, 2026-11-05; PR #166): a pace for a tool's own reads at the web2 edge, never a deadline or a verdict (the quota-driven pace stands owed as `G54`).
* ''Terra reads at the punctures'' (operator, 2026-11-05, replacing 2026-11-04's Sol for the final wiki lanes). One cold Terra read seat (`gpt-6.1-terra`, high effort, read mode, briefed blind) reads a lane's diff before its hand-back wherever the lane touches capability, custody or caps paths; the lead cures its findings red-first; a blind Opus reader collides with it on any custody-touching path. The Codex pool's build floor decides whether the seat runs, and below it the Opus read stands alone.

!!! Operator rulings, 2026-11-06
* ''Flow the order'' (operator, 2026-11-06: //"Approve your moves, flowstate & OODA-HA loops."//). The controller flows the order without asking where canon or a talk-out settles a fork.
* ''Keep the Lararium first'' (operator, 2026-11-06: //"I don't want to expand scope, tho the secret store reads as very pono to get right."// and //"We can iterate on the secrets vault after the wiki stands, it was improtant to lock the design. Focus on the Lararium as priority."//). `P27` lands before the rite as ruled: the required floor readings, sealed registry and share tightening at four crossings. The vault's store, onboarding, key delivery and recipient lifecycle stand designed in <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/reach-plane`>> and build after the hearth stands, as the first lane the memory stage tracks.
* ''Reach slots'' (operator, 2026-11-06: //"VEIL for Porkbun, pono."// and //"All POEE hands sit inside my Nexus, because there are no other nexuses yet, pono."//). <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/reach-plane`>> holds the slots.
The operator approved the house's defaults (operator, 2026-11-06: //"Approve your default leans so far."//). Each bullet below cites "operator, 2026-11-06, approving the house's defaults", and a cold Luna read held each against the memegraph's laws first, its note folded in.
* ''The ward key rides the admission'' (operator, 2026-11-06, approving the house's defaults). Each device's per-face ward public key rides its signed admission (`wardKey`, covered by `vesselSig`, the tag key's signature over group, atom and ward key) beside its proof of possession (`wardPop`), and a ward-act approval carries that key's signature over the answer, the ask's digest and the frontier the answerer read; the vessel key never enters a face admission (the fleets-overlap ruling, 2026-11-05). An approval binds one ask's digest, and the frozen spend refuses its replay. A ward key never rotates silently: a rewrap keeps the key, and a device whose ward goes missing re-joins as a new vessel (`ward-key-would-rotate`). The law rests in <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/seat-harness#/the-swarm-slices`>>.
* ''The asker's session seals'' (operator, 2026-11-06, approving the house's defaults; it sharpens "the asker seals" of 2026-10-19 above). The asker's session seals, on the vessel worker's thread, while that face's session stands unlocked, and `seal-awaits-session` stands. When the asker cannot answer, another admitted, unlocked session of the same face takes the seal, and the takeover refuses once no session of that face stands unlocked. Carriers: <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-wave-b-lane#/the-reds`>>, and `pono/browser-vessel-lift`, carried by the b1 lanes' carriers at their land.
* ''Removal wins at every reader'' (operator, 2026-11-06, approving the house's defaults; from the edge-frontier ruling "a raise shadows its target at once for every reader that sees it"). A reader whose own fleet frontier counts a device set aside refuses that device's outcomes and its certificate alike: the gate's certificate check and the reader's outcome check both refuse it, and a reader whose frontier has not yet counted the set-aside reads against its last verified view (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof#/the-offline-root`>>). A refused cap tip reads the tightest cap that ever stood on its line (`braked`). The law rests in <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/seat-harness#/the-swarm-slices`>>.
* ''The sealing scope'' (operator, 2026-11-06, approving the house's defaults; from the 2026-10-12 ruling). Every wiki content store (each bag's CRDT document, on node and browser) and the CAD/CAS store rest sealed; planes and boards stay clear and hold signed acts only, never a private payload; cleartext rests only in the disk projection and the live wiki in memory; a public bag rests under its published key. The sealed-store carrier's #/owed holds it, carried by the b1 lanes' carriers at their land.
* ''The catalog splits by face'' (operator, 2026-11-06, approving the house's defaults). No vessel-wide content key stands: each face holds its own catalog (`D`, sealed under the face), and a floor catalog names public bags alone (`F`); the daemon bag splits into the floor core (`F`, signed) and each face's daemon wiki content (`D`). The floor catalog's fields stand enumerated: a public bag's id, its kind and its published lookup reference, nothing else, so no private bag's name or metadata reaches `F`. The sealed-store carrier holds it, carried by the b1 lanes' carriers at their land.
* ''A timer save signs under its session'' (operator, 2026-11-06, approving the house's defaults). Every change books the session that wrote it, and its save signs under that session; the lock and the stand-down flush first; an unbooked save refuses and waits in memory, never written clear. A crash loses only an edit that never reached a booked save; the vessel reports what has not reached durable storage and promises nothing more. The sealed-store carrier holds it, carried by the b1 lanes' carriers at their land.
* ''Same-face tabs share one provider'' (operator, 2026-11-06, approving the house's defaults). One Keyhive provider per face lights at that face's first unlock; each later tab proves its own unlock; the face's last session closing drains in-flight verification, then drops the provider and its keys. Carriers: <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k19-hive-lane#/the-slices`>>, and `pono/browser-vessel-lift`, carried by the b1 lanes' carriers at their land.
* ''The ceremony's answer kinds'' (operator, 2026-11-06, approving the house's defaults). The clean ceremony document hosts a passphrase answer (custody and ward) and a WebAuthn PRF answer (custody only); the ward refuses a passkey, and a PRF slot never stands as the sole copy. PRF only wraps a custody key: it never authenticates and never gates an act (the 2026-11-04 ruling "never a passkey": no WebAuthn registry gates any act). Carrier: `pono/browser-vessel-lift#/the-ceremony`, carried by the b1 lanes' carriers at their land.
* ''The vessel reads its own durability'' (operator, 2026-11-06, approving the house's defaults). The vessel reads its own storage durability (`persisted()`, the storage class) and names erasable storage before a founding or a join binds; a wiped keystore boots as a new vessel, by name. Safari's seven-day rule rides as a measured platform condition, dated and sourced, never as an evergreen constant, and a granted persistence request guarantees nothing beyond what the platform reports. Carrier: `pono/browser-vessel-lift`, slice 7, carried by the b1 lanes' carriers at their land.
* ''One route for place and face'' (operator, 2026-11-06, approving the house's defaults). At the browser founding one typed route binds both the place tree and the founder face's tree; the two trees stay independently keyed and wrapped (one human prompt, two keys), and the node asks for each tree each time. Carrier: `pono/browser-vessel-lift` and custody, carried by the b1 lanes' carriers at their land.
* ''The unwired code takes a zero-trust review first'' (operator, 2026-11-06: //"The unwired code should get zero trust critical reviews before wiring (might be legit old design, might be pono but last steps unfinished design)."//). Code that production never calls (the unwired-code scout at `ffe05bc79`) earns no wiring by its presence: a zero-trust critical review decides retire, wire or reshape, then the work. Its items land before the rite: `L28`, `K22`, `K23` and `P27`.

!!! Operator rulings, 2026-11-06 (late, Lares-Awakens's seat)
* ''No clock decides a wait'' (operator, 2026-11-06: //"No timeout, 'No global now'"// and //"Approve your lean, event-only first"//). Every wait ends on the product's own events; a liveness window bounds silence only where the page offers no terminal signal. Test and hook budgets count as clocks too, and a sweep retires them (`M6`).
* ''Versions fall under no global now'' (operator, 2026-11-06: //"'No global now' applies to 'version numbers' (old web2.5 concept we discard) too."//). A version literal stands as debt to burn, never a pattern to copy; the evergreen witness counts the debt and the burn starts at the domain registry (`M6`).
* ''Free-form roots read pono'' (operator, 2026-11-06: //"free-form roots read pono, the prior sessions just fell into a basin with ha.ka.ba as a stable root."//). A commit subject's `lar:` URI takes any three-term root; `ha.ka.ba` stays the stable API root, never a requirement.
* ''Every vessel fix checks for a lift'' (operator, 2026-11-06: //"any 'vessel' codefixes should be checked for idempotent-by-intent lifts to /mesh or to mirror behaviour between all vessel types."//). A fix that touches a vessel composer, host or gate server answers, in its commit, whether the cure belongs in `@lararium/mesh` as one relation or mirrors across node, browser and herm.
* ''The broker's test door'' (operator, 2026-11-06: //"Approve, build the test door on tools-ground"//). A walled lead asks the broker to run named test files outside the wall (a PID namespace, scratch homes, loopback alone, the Node toolchain read-only, the tallies handed back); the docker socket stays the controller's alone (`M1`).
* ''The root cards retire'' (operator, 2026-11-06: //"I lean retire, but this needs a confimation of most pono action. Luna researcher w/ internet caps please."//; the Luna research confirmed RETIRE). `--split-root` and `vault restore --cards` retire; the Handle returns through recovery contacts or the self-recovery seed, memory through bundles (`M7`).
* ''The face lease's end state'' (operator, 2026-11-06: //"Build the end state. What's the end state here?"//; the Terra research named it). The face delegation lease retires to anchor-pinned persona lineage and remove-wins standing at every authority reader; the roll guard stands until a census proves every reader enforces set-aside (`M6`).

!!! Operator rulings, 2026-11-07
* ''The focus test'' (operator, 2026-11-07: //"Pono, approve your leanss (talk them all out again n the open)."//, approving the house's lean: //does `M5` (page, CLI and MCP on one wiki) arrive sooner because of this? Where the answer reads "no, but it prevents a repeat," the item waits unless the repeat cost a real day//). Every new tools or process item meets this test before it opens. Measured against it: the broker's test door passes (every lane before the rite runs e2e through the controller's hands until it stands); the launcher's `P1-3` flake passes (two CI recurrences, each a full rerun); the pono lines on the remaining refusal messages wait.
* ''The controller seat stays Opus and swaps at every batch land'' (operator, 2026-11-07, same approval). The spend reads on session length before tier, so the seat passes to a fresh session by message at each batch boundary; a Claude lane lead runs Sonnet by default and Opus for a seam, custody or security lane, and every custody slice keeps its blind Opus read. One batch's controller spend, read from its transcript, tests the lean.
* ''The spend gauge rests until the wikis stand'' (operator, 2026-11-07: //"set the spend number down, we'll resolve that after the wikis stand also"//). The window-A disagreement between the two spend readings waits; no lane steers by the 80 : 20 percentage until it reconciles.
* ''No backward compatibility in early alpha'' (operator, 2026-11-07: //"'no backward compatability needed in early alpha' remains house law."//). A rename, a re-key or a shape change carries no record across it, and every CRDT doc re-genesises until the founding bake (`X0`); the registry's domain renames re-key their records under this law.
* ''The field collides before it rules'' (operator, 2026-11-07, same approval). The outside research on multi-model swarm designs returns through a Hodge and Podge pair of Codex readers, measured against the house's own spend and failure readings, before any of it becomes a ruling.
* ''Codex carries the lanes'' (operator, 2026-11-07: //"Shift more work onto Codexs please."//). Lane leads run Codex by default; a Claude lead stands only where a lane needs custody or ward reds, and the blind Opus read stays on every custody slice. Failed CI logs go to a Codex read seat, never the controller's context, and a read seat's brief names its attachments by absolute path (the broker-owned seat directory receives only the brief).
* ''The swarm moves into the Lararium'' (operator, 2026-11-07: //"after the Lararium stands we move these into the Lararium 'capability stack' either as parts of the product code, parts of the TS -> cjs tiddlywiki plugin code ('inside the VM code'), or as appropriate 'causal island shore connectors'. Then the daemon can drive it all from inside the Lararium."//). After standing, the multi-model swarm harness (today's `tools/` broker, walls, launcher, lane state, push grant, gate and land train) moves into the capability stack, each part to the home its nature names: product code, plugin code running inside the VM, or a shore connector across a causal-island boundary; the daemon then drives the swarm from inside the Lararium (#/the-order, rank 31; `L30`).
* ''The test harness ideas rest until the Lararium stands'' (operator, to Lares-Awakens, 2026-11-06/07: //"Set the test harness ideas down, we'll dogfood those into the Lararium once it stands."//). New test-harness ideas wait for the standing Lararium and enter it by dogfooding, beside `L30`; before standing, the harness takes only the cures a lane's work needs.
* ''The stories' leans'' (operator, 2026-11-07: //"Pono, approve your leans, flow on OODA-HA"//, closing the user-story talk-out of both shores). (1) One labelled genesis re-bake runs in the pre-rite, after every version burn and the lease end state land, so the founding inherits no version literal. (2) The memory stage writes plain tiddlers freely; a daemon or kahua code act still takes the hand gesture the ward ruling names (2026-11-04); a cold read of the kahua-memory-stage carrier confirms the reading before `L4p` opens. (3) The founding stands one operator with many faces; the operator's daughter stands her own hearth at `M8` (#/rulings, the live test and the fence). (4) The vessel's force-kill of a wedged worker stands only as a named `LIVENESS` bound, checked where `clock-sweep` and `b5b` meet. (5) The secrets vault builds before `L30`: the daemon drives the swarm only once its credentials rest in the vault, never on a surface.
* ''Two house readings under the clock law'' (controller, 2026-11-07, under the 2026-11-06 delegation). The clock census reads `// PACING: <reason>` beside a clock that decides nothing (a poll cadence or debounce whose end an event or a named `LIVENESS` bound decides), the way it reads `// LIVENESS: <reason>`; a marker without its reason counts as unmarked. A teardown drain runs in two phases with no island clock: the island submits every owed write and sends `receipt:asks-done`; the vessel answers as asked and sends `receipt:close`; the port closing or the worker exiting ends receipts the same way, an owed title reporting unsaved by name (`L29`).
* ''Talk it out, then decide'' (operator, 2026-11-07: //"AIs often fall into a trap of holding a slot in the turn for 'only the operator can decide' items -> mu that, we talk it out, apply the mu tools, talk story over 2026/5492 user stories, and decide. only then do remaining uncertainties surface to the operator"//). Every fork weighs against the house laws by name: no global now, no backward compatibility in early alpha, causal islands, identity-as-relation, Infrastructure-as-Myth. The seat and every lead decide, name the fork and the reason, and surface only an uncertainty that survives the talk-out, with a default. Classifier refusals, destroy-class acts and the operator's own hands still surface at once.
* ''Test and collide canon'' (operator, 2026-11-07: //"apply the 5th commandment of the pentabarf (don't just beleieve everything, test and collide. We grew this system and its docs FAST and there's bound to be stale leftovers or places where the deisgn and code drifted but one feels most pono). If uncertainty spikes and can't be talked out, do a 'Luna class, 2 wave research pass'"//). A ruling or a brief that rests on a canon claim measures the code first; on drift the most pono side stands and both sides are named. A spike that the talk-out cannot settle takes a two-wave read (one breaks the claim, one collides the first), then the talk-out.
* ''The face lease's end state, reshaped'' (controller under the talk-out law, 2026-11-07; collided by a Luna two-wave read, HOLDS-WITH-SLICES). The face delegation lease retires; every authority reader requires anchor-pinned persona lineage and the remove-wins standing of the relation it reads, and refuses absent, forked or rolled-back proof by name. The fleet-wide epoch roll goes, since it assumes every island hears it. Whole-fleet removal stands as set-asides on each known device-face dyad, through a census that refuses an incomplete view, or the `K18` re-anchor once its door lands; the four marked edges hold no face-wide marker (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae#/four-edges`>>). An `owing` reading counts as standing. The roll guard and the epoch fields burn last, after the census proves every reader enforces set-aside.
* ''A lock always drops its signer'' (controller, 2026-11-07). Security outranks durability: a seal or open ask ends on its answer, the island's exit, or a named `LIVENESS` silence bound that refuses; the signer drops in every case, and a save left unsealed waits in memory by name, never rests clear.
* ''Cures land inside their lane'' (controller, 2026-11-07, on the reserve seat's lean). A read's finding on the lane whose work it names cures in that lane before it lands; an item parked after the land reads as building a now and a later.
* ''The live Lararium pulls hardest'' (operator, 2026-11-07: //"the attractor of 'standing a live Lararium' milestone pulls stronglhy"//). The road's spine runs `M3`, `M4`, `M5`; every slot a seat opens answers first whether it brings the live Lararium sooner (the focus test, sharpened).
* ''Codex seats carry even the critical path'' (operator, 2026-11-07: //"Continue, use Codex seats even for critical paths (then Sol+Opus reviews)"//). A critical-path or seam lane runs on Codex seats; while the pool stands above a lead seat's ceiling, Codex build seats edit the lane's worktree (network off) and the lane's conductor reads each diff and commits it; every such slice takes a Sol read and a blind Opus read before land.
* ''The seam testimony stays testimony'' (controller and reserve seat, 2026-11-07). Opus and Sol leads cured in single steps three cross-package seams where Terra leads stalled; the observation stays confounded by second attempts, by the broken test door Terra worked behind, and by labelling a lane a seam after it stalls. Lease-end slice 2 goes to Terra first through the fixed door as a lead seat once the pool reads under the lead ceiling (a build seat's conductor, committing each diff, would confound the measure), recording steps to green and brief size, before any ruling moves.
* ''The Codex pool line softens to 20%'' (operator, 2026-11-07: //"Set the codex pool limit down to 20% (its really a soft limit)"//). A lead, build or research seat launches until 20% of the weekly pool remains, so each used-percent line stands at 80% and meets the floor; a read seat keeps its own floor. The line bounds a fresh launch, never a running seat, and stands for the operator to move again (`pool-line`, PR #198; <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/seat-harness#/the-spend-guard`>>).
* ''The dogfood/keeper split'' (operator, 2026-11-07: //"Pono, approve the dogfood/keeper split."//). Two hearths of one kind answer two intents. The DOGFOOD hearth stands early from the runbook's own primitives, re-standable as often as needed (a single operator's re-found, 2026-10-31), so the house works from a live Lararium; `M5` builds on it as the spine. The KEEPER rite keeps every qualification the 2026-11-05 and 2026-11-06 rulings bind (#/milestones, `M4`); the dogfood stand waives none of them.
* ''One controller stands the dogfood Lararium'' (operator, 2026-11-07: //"Yes, Kia'i works to stand an early Lararium we can 'dogfood' from, so that handoff sits pono with the next milestone."//). The harness lane closed into the seat once its batch landed (PR #202); one controller holds the road, and the harness items it carried rest in seat-harness #/owed.
* ''The Lararium stands before the harness moves in, and before any measurement'' (operator, 2026-11-07: //"We stand the LArarium and dogfodo the harness INTO the LArarium before all that (delete that last direction from the plan/roadmaps, it's just more churn)"//). The order runs: the dogfood Lararium stands, `M5`'s memory stage builds on it, the harness moves into it (its controls as signed tiddlers in the daemon wiki, <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/seat-harness#/the-swarm-controls`>>), and only then does measurement work open (the outcome ledger, the cached-token measure, the capability manifest's drift test).
* ''The dogfood proof set'' (operator, 2026-11-07: //"1) yes this needs a deterministic set of e2e tests, but please try to find existing ones to update first (hand this design+build to a one-off Luna spirit)."// and //"3) incude this in the dogfood stand/proof. 4) agree, we need the main repo on the full read-to-dogfood stte, then a full rebuild, then a check tht the live Lararium will launch containterized and pono-web3-local-first."//). A deterministic e2e set, built from existing suites first, proves the dogfood stand in CI (lane `dogfood-proof`, a Luna seat). The stand opens only after the main repo reaches the full ready-to-dogfood state, a full rebuild runs, and a check reads the live Lararium launching containerized, web3 and local-first.
* ''One root branch, `develop`'' (operator, 2026-11-07: //"So MU the workspace question."// and, truing it, //"the primary repo here will just serve `develop` until the roadmap clears, no split needed, we dogfood and develop on one checked out root-nbrach `develop`, then PR back to main after milestones."//). Once the dogfood stands and its proof set reads green, all outstanding work merges into `feature/green-jello-dinosaur-5`, that merges to `main`, and the main repo checks out one root branch, `develop`, where the house both dogfoods and develops until the roadmap clears; lanes branch off `develop` and land into it, the live wiki's writes land there as source-control write-back, and each milestone opens a pull request back to `main`.
* ''A browser leaf floors the secrets plane by established absence'' (seat, talked out, 2026-11-07; overrulable). The secrets plane stands node-only (the vault rests on the node, never on a surface: the stories' lean 5 above); a browser leaf floors it by established absence (`NO_CONTRACT_SECRETS_PLANE`), and the node's face-catalog arc stays off the browser. A CAS read's audience reads off the asking island's relation: the daemon island in process, a pool wiki its plane, the herm and the HTTP `/cas` face the world; the genesis inventory declares its public bytes. Both ride `P27`'s lane, parked (#/the-order, rank 32).
* ''The memory stage's meme path'' (seat, talked out under the recipe and bags model, 2026-11-07; trued for the layers above working and by a Luna measure; overrulable). The daemon wiki's recipe reads, top to bottom, temp, draft, personal, working, the wiki's own canon bag, the kahua bag, the library bags and the oracle (`wiki-recipe.ts`), and a kāpae shadows downward only (`island-adaptor.ts`). (a) The stage law reads at the WORKING seam, where a meme becomes a stage meme: drafts and the other upper layers hold work in progress, so the stage's readers and `L11`'s index read working and canon, never drafts, and there a stage meme whose title disagrees with its key reads as a named fork, never a stage meme; the backstop writes its refusal as a diagnostic tiddler in the working bag beside its log line (owed on `L11` and the meme backstop). (b) Concurrency catches at the draft-to-working save: the native draft save carries the draft's captured Automerge heads as its base (`island-adaptor.ts`), a tiddler's text rests as one whole scalar (`stored-value.ts`), and `readParallelDrafts` surfaces concurrent values off Automerge's conflict set (`parallel-drafts.ts`), so a moved base keeps both sides visible and the stage's readers read through it; a write that skips a draft meets the same conflict read as its backstop (owed on `L11` and `L12` before multi-device dogfooding). (c) A burned stage meme raises a kāpae in the working bag, never a retract to absent (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae#/core-cut`>>), so it shadows a lower or re-poured copy; since a kāpae shadows downward only and drafts sync across a seated face's fleet (temp stays local), a burn also discards or shadows the open drafts and upper-layer copies of its meme; exports and re-pours carry the working layer and its kāpae records, never drafts. The disk projector writes a raised kāpae onto disk as `<stem>.kapae` and ingest raises it again, so a re-pour of an old export never brings a burned meme back (`L34` keeps the residues). (d) Two memories that choose one readable key settle by authoring discipline (`L19`'s class and slug) and `placeMeme`'s base-hash refusal, with no new mechanism. Stage memes stay in the kahua working projection under the kept state root (cleartext only on disk and in the live wiki), never pushed to git; durability past this machine rides the vault's backup door, as today's flat memories rest outside git.

!!! Operator rulings, 2026-11-09 and 2026-11-10 ~ the placement leap and the locked floor
* ''Placement over patterns'' (operator, 2026-11-09, the placement leap). Where a record rests decides where it reaches disk: a record projects by the bag that holds it and that bag's mirror grant, never by its title or a field it carries; a record that must stay off disk rests where no mirror reaches (the control document, the secret home, the shadow layer, a board no recipe mounts). <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/disk-projection#/projection-filter`>>; `tools/placement-residency-witness.mjs` reads it.
* ''Parity across vessel types'' (operator, 2026-11-09: //"the system get idempotent-by-intent and isomorphic-by-intent partity between all vessel types."//). A host-shaped deviation never rides as a filed item: it cures before its leap lands, behind one shared helper, so node, browser and the CLI's direct holder cannot drift.
* ''A locked hearth fails closed'' (operator, 2026-11-09). Every act that needs the vessel's control document refuses `vessel-locked` until custody opens; the custody door (unlock, lock, census) stays carved out; no receipt claims durability over memory; a refused unlock says why. <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/waking-floor#/the-shape`>>.
* ''The secret home and the control document mint with the face'' (operator, 2026-11-09). A face's founding or admission mints both under one custody record; a place (a herm, a hearth before its face) mints neither, and its control stand-in refuses `control-home-unnamed`.
* ''One control document, face-held'' (operator, 2026-11-09). Every control family rests in the one control document; nothing stands a second control home.
* ''The carriage log, and a sixth floor duty'' (operator, 2026-11-09). A place's relay state rests in its public carriage log: append-only, unsealed, keyed by the carriage's own id, each receipt signed under the place key as the sixth floor duty, `CARRIAGE_RECEIPT_DOMAIN` (`place-signer.ts`), herm and hearth alike. <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/lamplighters#/the-carriage-log`>>.
* ''A herm never unlocks'' (operator, 2026-11-09: //"Herm(s) stand as a Laraium wihtout any unlocked "operator private" capabilities. A quine-relay."//). No herm path waits on, or fakes, an unlock; whatever a herm's relay needs rests in homes that need no operator-private custody. Measured 2026-11-10: the unlock door still opens a faceless place's vessel key and stands it sovereign (`custody-frames.ts` `unlock`, `node-vessel-identity.ts` `sovereignStands`), and the herm lift walks that unlock (`tests/e2e/herm-floor.test.ts` R5); a named refusal stays owed behind the controller's word.
* ''Both confirmations, with a Mu'' (operator, 2026-11-10: //"Approve both confirmations; but we shall MU both after the Lararium stands and we can dogfood the design from inside the system."//). The two: a herm's or locked floor's daemon wiki writes rest in memory alone and drop at recompose; the carriage log takes the sixth floor duty (a peer cosignature and a non-inclusion proof stay owed). Both re-open once the dogfood Lararium stands.
* ''A re-founding keeps the projections'' (operator, 2026-11-08, the survive-refound leap). One named house root; a re-founding clears `hearth/` alone, and `projections/` survives it, private cleartext memory included.
* ''Plain text survives to disk'' (operator, 2026-11-10: //"The state of play now is 'Dogfood our code into a self-improving AI-Operator alignment harness' so some things must wait. The 'plain text survives to disk' law holds because how do we recover running data between alpha refound otherwise (& this lesson carries forwards, etc)."//). The plaintext projection survives every re-founding by design, so running data recovers across alpha re-foundings (`lares export` and `import`, a reset that keeps `projections/`), and the law carries forward past alpha. Work beyond the truths it needs waits behind the dogfood harness. <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/disk-projection#/core-claim`>>.

!!! Operator rulings, 2026-11-11 ~ the house canon, the vessel pin, the workbench and the lit set
* ''House canon rests at contract; its CIDs read public'' (operator, 2026-11-11: //"veil ⊂ persona-group ⊂ contract ⊂ public -> Those note the 'access posture' for CRDTs, and the CRDTs for the canon rest under Kahu cabal contract. The CIDs are public."//). The cap tiers name a CRDT's access posture and nest by inclusion. The house canon (`houseCanonSlugs`: `lares` and `lararium` always, every disk-sourced holding but the public board `crossroads`) rests live in the Kahu realm at contract; the Kahu steward alone writes it, every other face reads the floor, and the published snapshots travel by CID to every DreamNet user. No separate vessel tier stands. Landed (`a3d3dbed5`; <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/cap-tier#/house-canon`>>).
* ''The vessel pins the floor'' (operator, 2026-11-11: //"The pull/push tension can go the way the memegraph/code leans"//). A release gets cut and published; each VESSEL accepts it, one pin per (vessel, Nexus) in its control home, and faces pin nothing. The accept fast-forwards along `prior` and refuses a fork or a non-descendant by name; an island that swaps its floor live swaps, any other raises the reboot alert. `lares vessel floor status|accept`. Landed (`63bc695d9`, `W26` slice 5; <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/w26-floor-lane#/the-vessel-pin`>>).
* ''Faces form a private cabal of one'' (operator, 2026-11-11: //"the multitude of faces reads as a private cabal for 1 human. Pattern integrities!"//). One human's faces sharing a bag or a wiki enact the cabal grammar at the scale of one person: the charter names the share, the membership names the faces, and no parallel ACL stands beside it. An unshared wiki founds a private cabal of one (`privateCabalRealmId(face, slug)`). OWED: the share config (`$:/config/lares/share/<slug>`, `share-config.ts`) still resolves face-to-face shares through its own resolver, beside the cabal's read scope.
* ''Contracted members sync the working layer'' (operator, 2026-11-11: //"We want contracted members to have synced access to the 'working layer' too, even cross operator."//). A shared wiki's working layer rests as one realm-workbench CRDT that every member its contract seats syncs; edits merge in the CRDT, and canon stays promotion-gated. Landed for one operator's faces (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/cabal-realm#/the-workbench`>>); the cross-operator join stays OWED at the realm's member consult.
* ''Personal carries the private margin'' (operator, 2026-11-11: //"Personal can absorb the new caps."//). `personal` holds one face's private margin over the shared workbench as well as its view state, crosses that face's own devices alone, and projects per face to `personal/<F>/<slug>`, seeding back. Landed (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/disk-projection#/personal`>>).
* ''The full new design, no lift'' (operator, 2026-11-11: //"Unpono, rip out old code, enact the full new design, early-alpha law holds."//). No per-face working document stands for any wiki but the daemon's: every other wiki's working layer rests in a realm workbench at `realmWorkbenchDocUrl(realm, slug)`; the daemon wiki stays face-private; workspaces bind at `/lar/workspaces/<name>`; the recipe reads canon -> workbench -> personal -> draft -> temp. Recovery rides the disk mirrors seeding back, never a migration. Landed (`6576c2313`, `594a22378`); `F3` rests in #/ashes.
* ''Faces name themselves at creation'' (operator, 2026-11-11: //"Faces name themselves at creation"//). A face's label (relation -> pet-name) lands the moment the face stands, so a seed after a re-founding adopts its folders by that name with no door having read the face first. Landed on the node (the founding, `found-hearth.ts`; `lares persona`, `persona.ts`). The browser writes the label at its founding too, into OPFS (`browser-face-labels.ts`, one shared seam with the node).
* ''Plaintext survives to disk'' (operator, 2026-11-11: //"Plaintext survives to disk"//, re-affirming 2026-11-10). Every layer that holds a face's content keeps a cleartext mirror that seeds back across a re-founding: the workbench through its one folder `wikis/<slug>` (beside the source for a checkout-sourced wiki) or its workspace, once for its document (operator, 2026-11-14: //"most wikis should NOT seed per-face because shared crdts, while daemon wikis should seed per-face. Same with import/export."//), `personal` through `personal/<F>/<slug>`, the daemon tiers through `wikis/daemon-<F>` and `bags/daemon-<F>`.
* ''The top layer always shadows'' (operator, 2026-11-11: //"The top layer always shadows"//). The recipe stack alone decides what shows: a working record shadows canon's, nothing retracts a higher layer, and a seed's canon leg warns where canon stands fresher (`override-bases.ts`, `seed-mirrors.ts`). Landed.
* ''No version numbers'' (operator, 2026-11-11, LAW: //"NO version numbers enter our stack ('no global now' no clocks, no scalar-clocks either)."//). A format reads by its shape name, identity by CID, and order by causal link alone: a KEL orders by hash-chain descent with `seq` as a cross-check, a release and an announcement by `prior`, a rotated canon by `rotated-from`. The evergreen witness caps version literals and only lowers the cap. Landed across the clockless sweep (evergreen `version 0/0`).
* ''Open faces, one worn'' (house reading of the lit set, 2026-11-11, under the operator's rulings above). A vessel keeps several faces OPEN, each sealing under its own key, and wears ONE; the AI seat marks its calls `"seat": "ai"` and acts as the worn face alone, refusing `face-hand-refused` where a call names another (`seat-hand.ts`). Landed (`029d87df8`).
* ''The leaps run in flowstate'' (operator, 2026-11-11: //"Enact all in flowstate"//). The house walks the leaps of #/the-leaps one after another, each under the gate the operator reconfirmed the same day: one shared implementation in the isomorphic package, substrate bindings alone forking behind capability-named ports, parity cured before a leap lands and never filed, a cross-host test per leap, and a replay landing nothing new (#/rulings, 2026-11-09, parity across vessel types).
* ''The mirror shows the worker's live wiki, whole'' (operator, 2026-11-11: //"The mirror 'should' render the active/pinned VM worker's 'wiki' through the live TW5 projection…"//; //"hold the VMs are 'a third thing, never seen before'… We then focus on the 'full mirror' browser experience. That's where this design drifted, it built the minimal first (unpono in early alpha, we design and land full features)."//). The worker VM stands as the third runtime and holds every tiddler, TW5's kernel among them; the page's mirror boots from what that VM hands it over the port and paints the active wiki through stock TW5 at full width, with skinny as the one named step down. Landed (`231e0c6a4`, `2eeef99fe`); the ruling's reading and what stays owed rest in <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/dreamdom#/open-rulings`>> and <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/d3-glass-lane#/owed`>>.

!!! Operator rulings, 2026-11-12 ~ the veil's upgrade, the leaf fleet, the route door and the next two leaps
* ''A join upgrades the veil, never splits it'' (operator, 2026-11-12: //"The concepts called for an anon face acting to join a PersonaGroup fleet as an upgraded capability, not as a seperate A+B faces."//). A vessel stands at its veil floor first; a join or a founding adds the face as an overlay above that floor, the Lift moves the floor's content into the face once, and no second face stands for the veil. The house's rulings that carry it rest in <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/the-veil-ladder#/the-base-model`>>, each a controller ruling and overrulable: the face's own atom signs the veil-lift record, and the place key signs no act that names a face; the floor stands on a key of its own (the node's from its custody root, the browser's from a dedicated `floor-rest` seed), with no secret home and no control document until a face lands and the floor's registry in the daemon document; the floor's verbs gate on the held floor key (Ruling 1); a wiki's canon re-files in place at the Lift (Ruling 2); a human carries many faces, each an overlay keyed by PersonaGroup; and the controller's U-rulings (U1 to U6) the veil lane built under.
* ''A human carries many faces, and every vessel stands by capability'' (operator, 2026-11-12: //"a human carries multiple faces"//; //"isomorphic by intent for all vessels by capability"//; //"users have leaf-device fleets, operators have leaf+node fleets"//). A user's fleet holds leaves alone, so the fleet-join door, the founding door and the carried seat stand whole on a leaf; an operator's fleet adds nodes. One composer and one seat core serve every vessel, and a host differs only in what its capabilities carry (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/device-capabilities-2026#/device-seats`>>, its in-house rulings 1 to 10; the stories in <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/dreamnet-architecture#/the-walkers`>>).
* ''No query string overrides the page'' (operator, 2026-11-12: //"I don't like query strings as overrides, that interrupts the tw5 native uri handling (or if we can pipe it through the mirror & tw5 vm it may read pono)."//). A page act never rides a query key: the founding ask moved onto the identity panel's own button, carried across one reload (`?found` left, `aca107a97`). The arrival's relay, floor card and anchor left the address (the relay reads off the page's own origin; the card and the anchor come in at the carriage door). The last keys left the address: the floor reads at the page's own origin (`?flow=` burned), a harness raises the boot trace in the tab's sessionStorage (`lares:c4trace`), and `?mesh=` burned with its door owed in #/the-leaps. A census in `lararium-web` `tests/live-status.test.ts` reads every page source: none reads the query, and the fragment keeps the mirror's permalink and the ceremony document's ask as its named readers.
* ''Three leans approved'' (operator, 2026-11-12: //"Pono, approve all three leans. Update any memes that read un-pono."//).
*# ''A leaf binds `hearthTrueName: ""` by design.'' The field names a hearth's seed-charter Nexus AID; a hearth-agnostic leaf states it empty, no place wall stands for a leaf, and the AID's wall arrives by an act when a face founds or joins a hearth (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/lararium-identity#/delegation-edge`>>). No build; the stale-text sweep stays owed in #/the-leaps.
*# ''The integer `boundEpoch` retires'' (the next leap). An edge names the head digest of the PersonaGroup lineage it bound under; supersession rides acts (a Keyhive revoke, a kāpae, a renewal linked by digest), and a hard break forks as exit (a new PersonaGroup id, or a new seed charter for a hearth).
*# ''A hearth founds as an existing face'' (the next leap). The node stands its veil floor, joins the face F through the carriage (the leaves' seat door, with seat verbs on the node's command line), and seals the seed charter as F; today `found` forces a fresh face 0 (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/founding-runbook#/the-growth-rite`>>).
* ''The first fleet offer founds the face'' (controller ruling, 2026-11-12, overrulable). A leaf at its veil floor that offers a fleet join holds no face yet; the offer act founds the face first, through the same one-boot ask the identity panel's founding door carries, and then offers it. Owed in #/the-leaps.
* ''A resend that meets its act still waiting refunds the attempt'' (controller ruling, 2026-11-12, overrulable; built, `50b8fd8ae`). The page's retry bound counts only sends the vessel never acknowledged (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/dreamdom-port-contract#/retry-law`>>).
* ''The Nexus scales, talked out'' (talk-story, 2026-11-12). One human's many nodes stay one Lararium, and the Household, Crossroads, Temple and True-Named planetary Nexuses stand as a recursive stack by contract and treaty, never a chain of command (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/dreamnet-architecture#/the-nexus-scales`>>).
* ''Many floors, innermost first'' (operator, 2026-11-12: //"Pono, approve both leans (many floors, Kahu seats)."//). A vessel that stands in several Nexuses holds one floor pin per Nexus, and its recipe stacks those floors innermost-first: its Household's canon shadows the Crossroads', which shadows the Temple's, with the planetary floor beneath; the top layer shadows across tiers as it does within one (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/w26-floor-lane#/many-floors`>>). Owed: no recipe stacks more than one floor yet (#/the-leaps).
* ''A Kahu seat per tier'' (operator, 2026-11-12, the same approval). Each tier seats its own Kahu quorum; any human may hold seats at several tiers, and no tier's seats count toward another tier's quorum (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/seats-per-tier`>>).

!!! Operator rulings, 2026-11-13 ~ the confirm gate for critical procedures, and the swarm's re-think
* ''Critical procedures take a confirm gate'' (operator, 2026-11-13: //"Note for the future (find the DreamNet/DreamDom roadmaps and survey then true up and add) that critical procedures like `lares("vessel", "reset")` and its UX twin should get gated behind an operator/user confirmation (like the pwd, etc)."//). A critical procedure and its page twin stand behind a confirmation of the operator or user who holds the device. The browser's `lares("vessel", "reset")` and the page's own button raise one reset ask, and the ceremony document burns the place only on the pass its confirm gate mints (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/browser-vessel-lift#/the-confirm-gate`>>; `C4` in #/the-leaps). The host shim's `reset` takes a typed word alone (`C10`). The gate's shape rests in the house's lean below, overrulable (#/open).
* ''The gate's shape'' (the house's lean, talked out 2026-11-13; overrulable, and never the operator's ruling). The house weighed the note against the anon-floor law, under which every vessel MUST always return to its floor (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/dreamnet-rhizome`>> #/the-ground), and against the ward's bound acts (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/dreamnet-architecture#/faces-fleets-and-the-ward`>>), and leans to four lines; the browser stands built as leaned, and the fourth line's census reaches the browser's burn primitives alone.
*# ''Custody held asks the passphrase.'' Where the vessel holds custody (a first passphrase set, or a face joined), the reset asks that passphrase through the ceremony document before anything burns.
*# ''A bare floor takes the typed phrase.'' At a bare floor with nothing sealed, the typed phrase suffices.
*# ''No house door gates the platform.'' A holder who lost the passphrase keeps the platform's own site-data clear, which no house door can gate, so the return to the floor stays whole.
*# ''The class reads wider than reset.'' Any door that destroys a place or a face on a device, on any vessel, takes the gate by default, and so does its UI twin.
* ''Answered with the gate'': a locked vessel verifies a passphrase without unlocking, by the unwrap alone against the trees at rest; the reset proves it in the ceremony document's own worker with the vessel worker stood down, and holds no key by design. The lean above flips on the operator's word.
* ''The swarm's procedures re-think once the Lararium stands'' (operator, 2026-11-13: //"We shall have to completely re-think our multi-model & swarm procedures once we have the fully standing Lararium."//). The multi-model and swarm procedures (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/mixed-model-swarm`>>, the lane law of controller-handoff) take a complete re-think once the Lararium fully stands; until then they hold as written. Owed in #/the-leaps, after the walk.

!!! House rulings under the operator's delegation ~ the questions the operator delegated, each with what flips it
The operator delegated these to the house: each stands as a house ruling the operator may overturn, its law lives in the carrier it names, and its build rides the stride items #/the-loops indexes.
* ''The ember's key at rest'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). A separate serving key (SK), the fifth custody class `serving`, wrapped by the vessel key and by ember slots, seals the serving records alone, and the serving tier holds no minting secret: a walker's grant checks under a public half, a shape the hosted road's re-cut lands before the ember's walker arm. An unattended answer (any slot that answers without a person's confirm on the ember) needs a freshness anchor a disk copy cannot carry, a hardware-sealed hearth key whose attestation the ember checks on each ask; without one every ember slot answers only on a person's confirm, threshold two buys a second confirm and never an unattended raise, and after any counted removal a bare deny-only `sk-rotation-owed` mark refuses every ask until SK rolls, a mark a rolled-back disk copy can omit. ''For the operator'': a hearth that holds no hardware anchor raises only on a person's confirm. The reason: a key that opens serving state opens nothing beside it, and a copied disk replays any freshness it carries; the ruling flips on a threat review showing the serving records hold nothing a thief can use even before the grant re-cut (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-table`>>; <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/ember`>>).
* ''The ember's serving gate'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). A third gate tier, the serving route, takes its bundle from SK, and the floor and sovereign tiers stand untouched; a pin moves only by a signed causal event or a verified key-history step, and no quiet period ages a pin out. The reason: a dialer that pinned the sovereign tier never mistakes a serving hearth for an unlocked one. The ruling flips on proof that a gate pin decides no authority anywhere (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof#/the-gate-exchange`>>).
* ''How a raise lapses'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). A raise lives one boot and lapses at the next causal boundary (a lock, a restart, a human unlock, a counted removal), and that boundary IS the expiry the waking floor requires; no timer and no heartbeat ends a standing, and the count spends at the raise ledger's durable commit before SERVING composes, so a crash before the commit spends nothing and serves nothing and a crash after it burns the raise. The reason: quiet never tells a slow device from a gone one; the residue: a rolled-back ledger on a disk copy restores spent raises. The ruling flips on a causal renewal event every reader receives that also survives the ember's loss (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/waking-floor#/the-shape`>>).
* ''A removal no reader can keep'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). The based removal refuses by name where no parallel-drafts reader keeps the concurrent words, or every reader failed, and the store authors nothing; the refusal carries a durable conflict handle (the document, the removal's request id, the conflicting change and head identities, never the hand's text), so a hand whose editor went offline finds the same conflict on its return. The reason: a draft the store wrote would choose a bag, a tier and a steward no hand named. The ruling flips on an already-authorized destination for such words that keeps provenance and tier without the store choosing either (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/automerge-doc-store`>>).
* ''The outbox at rest'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). Each outbox parcel seals under randomized authenticated encryption with a stable outbox key the vessel key wraps and an unlock hands the daemon island, the way content keys reach it, and rests under a random name with its routing inside the sealed body, so a locked disk names no reader; the `sealed-outbox` carrier leaves `floor-plain` for a sealed class. Before a parcel's atomic commit nothing stands sendable, after it the parcel re-opens and retries at the next unlock, and after a ship and before the local delete the receiver folds a repeat idempotently. The reason: a reader digest on a stolen disk names a relationship; no flip stands expected, since the ruling closes a residue (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-table`>>).
* ''The herm relay leg'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). The ask rides the sealed sibling session under that session's seal with a tagged inner frame, and the delivery receipt stays a bare session note good for the asker alone, bound to a unique request id and the session's causal position, attesting transit and never execution; reach narrows to one PersonaGroup, and the leg lands with `W9`. The reason: the fewest new shapes, and no signing domain minted for a transport fact. The ruling flips on a concrete need for a third party to audit relay delivery after the asker vanishes (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/receipt-ledger#/the-relay-leg`>>).
* ''The disk watcher's reach'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). A pending removal settles with no person present only under a standing instruction the operator signs by a human route, naming one bag and a bounded class of removals (a path pattern and a per-wave count ceiling): the watcher executes within it, every settled removal lands in the audit with the instruction it rode, and `lares shore restore` still answers it. An automated seat never signs one, a standoff (disk and records differ) never settles without a person, and a mass vanish past the instruction's ceiling stays pending for a person. The reason: a person who answered in writing for exactly that class has answered, and a count alone proves no intent; the ruling flips on a single incident where an in-scope instruction deleted what a person meant to keep (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/disk-projection#/projection-law`>>).
* ''The restore's key history'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). A restored face stands PROVISIONAL until at least one party to its relation (a sibling device of the same human, a seated chair of its Nexus, or a recovery contact) shows its own log view and that view agrees with the restore's head; while provisional the face reads, recovers and serves its own content and signs no authority-changing act, the restore prints which head it read and from where, and the provisional state names who could confirm it. Where every such party stays unreachable, the recovery contacts' path (the impersonation quorum) answers, never the local read alone. The reason: no vessel reads a head it never received, and the house keeps no registry of heads; no flip stands, and the residue stays stated: one agreeing party may itself sit stale (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-restore`>>).
* ''The Offering Antigen scope'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). A signed Offering act carries a stable scope, the joint Nexus AID its signed inception mints, beside `sealEpochCid` as the authorizing epoch. The receiver resolves its own charter scope, verifies the act's epoch inside that scope's locally held lineage, checks the quorum at that exact epoch, and only then folds; an act whose scope differs stays as foreign evidence outside the `kapae` and `un_kapae` fold, and an incomplete lineage reads `unavailable`. The joint inception image commits to the offer fingerprint and to both founders' fresh cold successor commitments, and a successor commitment a vault already used refuses, so the AID names one founding statement and two foundings by one roster never alias. The reason: a neighbour's condemnation copied onto another board reads as testimony, never as that board's verdict. The ruling flips on a case where two foundings by one roster must read as one Nexus (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/offering-antigen-scope-decision-record`>>).
* ''The fork-resolve act'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). A fork stays a fork, and every reader names the branch it follows; no clock, no longest chain and no third-party quorum picks a branch. Two acts resolve one. ABANDON: a seal record a branch's own quorum signs at its head, closing that branch for every reader who holds it, erasing nothing and touching no sibling. REUNION: one canonical image naming the scope AID, both held heads sorted, and the new seats, threshold and successor commitments, signed by each branch's quorum at its head over identical bytes; the verifier proves both heads inside the same AID's lineages from a common ancestor, and the record becomes the common head. A seal record's predecessors form a sorted set of one or two, built before the fence; until the reunion verifier lands, a two-predecessor record refuses `REUNION_UNBUILT`. The reason: a split household reunites only by a paper both halves sign. The ruling flips on a fork that neither branch's quorum can still sign for.
* ''The re-seal proof'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). When a content key rolls at a counted removal, the face session that writes each replacement signs one causal re-seal record per replacement: the bag locator, the old and new key-version ids, the old and new ciphertext addresses, the logical entry it replaces and the removal act that authorized the roll. The removal quorum signs the roll, never each body. A reader with partial history verifies the signature and every binding it holds, and reports a missing parent as unverified, never as complete. The format lands with sealed-store step three, before the fence. The reason: a phone offline through the roll needs to know which photo replaced which, and whose hand re-sealed it. The ruling flips on proof that the key-version record, manifests and checkpoints already show every replacement and omission to both kinds of reader (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store`>>).
* ''Recovery contact enrolment's shape'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). Each face founds one-of-one with no recovery contacts, and its log's verifier knows a typed first-enrolment event from the start: the face's own current key signs it, it commits the recovery contact set and threshold, and the face's prefix stays as it stands; every later change to the set takes the recovery contacts' authority. The event shape and its verifier land before the fence, so an enrolment never re-founds a face. The reason: a face founded alone keeps its Handle's history when recovery contacts join a year later, and no third hand holds standing over a one-of-one face. The ruling flips on an existing event kind that already permits that first, face-key-signed transition. The count stands ruled at two of three recovery contacts (#/rulings, 2026-10-31); request authentication, the veto, death and succession stay open (#/open).
* ''Engine cadence'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). Cadence keeps its own lineage and threshold, apart from every seat key. A Nexus's joint founding cites one cadence inception that names each founder's hearth, the device that bears the reboot, as a holder; a household seed carries none, since its one holder bears its reboots. Each hearth mints its cadence key at random on the device, never derived from serving or floor material, and keeps it, with its next-key commitment, as a `cold` record under the vessel key that unwraps per act; no sixth custody class and no extra card at the founding. A holder may later rotate onto a cold card by its own choice. Each island pins one engine epoch, and one daemon hosts two epochs only as two separately pinned islands. The reason: the people who bear a reboot decide it, and a seat-holder who runs no hardware forces no one's restart. The ruling flips on a cadence act that must run unattended where a per-act unwrap cannot (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/contract`>>).
* ''A realm's quorum and its boards'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-14). Each realm stands under ONE Nexus, which the vessel's first act there names and its edge ledger keeps; every reader takes the realm's quorum roster off that Nexus's charter, never off its own primary one; and the realm's boards key on the realm's own id, so a removal's record stays among its parties. The reason: authority comes from the governing charter while the record addresses the relation it affects. The ruling flips on a realm-native charter model with its own seat creation, migration and conflict rule (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae#/edge-hands`>>).
* ''A restore after recovery contact enrolment'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-15). A face's own bundle restores after it enrols recovery contacts, so long as the face's log still holds the set its own root enrolled by the typed first event and no later event moved it; a recovery contact rotation that moves the set cuts every earlier bundle off. The restored face stands provisional as the restore's key-history ruling holds. The reason: enrolling recovery contacts must never silently kill a person's own backups, and the recovery contacts' rotation, not the enrolment, cuts off a stolen bundle. The ruling flips on a provisional gate that cannot stop a restored face from signing an authority-changing act before a party confirms it (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-restore`>>).
* ''Paper restores a root, never memory'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-15). The split-root cards rebuild the persona root alone; content keys stay random and wrap under the vessel key or a bundle's own key, and no key the root derives opens memory. A person recovers memory through a bundle, under the route that bundle seals. The reason: a root card that opened memory would turn every share holder and every thief of the paper into a reader of the face's private bytes. The ruling flips on an explicit choice that root-share holders read memory, with the custody that choice costs stated.
* ''A restore as persona zero'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-15). A restore onto a fresh hearth runs through the founding act: the hearth founds a fresh one-seat seed charter, and the restored face stands as its persona zero and holds its new chair. A bundle's old chair claim carries no authority on the new hearth, since a bundle carries no seat key. A founding that stopped part way resumes only its own persona-zero root and its own seed charter, and refuses a root that differs from the restored one rather than minting over it. The reason: a person who lost their one machine meets their own face, never a second one. The ruling flips on a rule that a lost hearth's chair must survive, which would make a bundle carry seat authority it excludes.
* ''A roll waits on its prior anchor'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-15). A seal roll refuses by name while the prior roll stands with no anchor, so no Nexus stacks a roll over a step its readers cannot yet carry across a removal. The reason: an admit carries across a seal roll only through its roll anchors, and a roll laid over an anchorless one strands every admit between them.
* ''The working address'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-15; the operator's workbench rulings of 2026-11-11 above flip it). The daemon wiki alone keeps a face-private working document. Every other wiki's working layer rests in a realm workbench at `realmWorkbenchDocUrl(realm, slug)`, `deterministicDocUrl` over the realm board's address with `#working/<slug>`: no member change moves it and knowing it grants nothing, and an unshared wiki's realm id derives from the owning face and the slug (`privateCabalRealmId`), so two offline devices of one face converge on one address (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/cabal-realm#/the-workbench`>>).
* ''The admit's gate pin'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-15). The admit carriage and fleet-join answer sign a validated full `GateBundlePin` for the hearth's sovereign sync route at the named Nexus, read by `readGateBundlePin`; its digest derives from that object. Dials index by (hearth, Nexus, route, tier). Floor routes receive their own full pins through an authenticated relation or a carried signed pin set; an announcement never authenticates its own first pin. The reason: a digest alone gives an offline dialer no trusted route or key. The ruling flips on an equally authenticated digest-to-full-pin carrier with an unambiguous route and offline first contact.
* ''Frames after open'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-15). A versioned sealed application header carries kind (`session`, `sync`, or a named control kind), message id, part index and count, total length, then bytes, only after the gate seat opens. Gate tags `0x01`–`0x13` serve the handshake alone. Reassembly and concurrent messages carry bounds; part size follows the Noise limit after header and sealing overhead. Missing, duplicate, mixed-kind, overlong and post-close parts refuse. The reason: a large sync frame needs bounded, unambiguous carriage. The ruling flips on a smaller measured layout that passes the same large-sync, replay, memory-bound and cross-kind vectors.
* ''Sharing after the gate'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-15). The proven same-operator class establishes transport alone. The session's proven face and scoped document view decide each document. A place arm reaches infrastructure alone; a PersonaGroup grant admits a sibling vessel of the same face and grants no blanket document access. Two tabs on one laptop wearing different faces see different books. The reason: transport kinship grants no document readership. The ruling flips on a scoped-view proof that another key carries the same face with exactly the same facets.
* ''A device set aside'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-15). Each vessel face-key certificate binds its face key to its certified device edge. Before sorting a presentation as same-operator, the gate consults the fleet edge's shadow for that device edge and refuses while the mapping or fold reads unreadable. The wire carries only the face-scoped key. A restart and two-face witness gate the cutover. The reason: removal of a device must cut every face it wore. The ruling flips on causal withdrawal of every affected face certificate proven to reach every gate with no gap.
* ''The contract seam'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-15). N2, N4 and N5 stand handed back. Train W owns the admit-carriage hunk of `nexus-contract.ts` for the cutover, keeps N's anchor and prior-roll checks, and lands the contract, `device-admit.ts`, fleet answer, verifier and vectors as one signed-shape change. The reason: one signature shape must cross every producer and reader together. The ruling flips on a proven independent carriage boundary that preserves those checks and signed bytes.
* ''The founder's certificate'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-15). A founder self-issues its vessel face-key certificate through the checked issue door and withdrawal history a joiner uses. The reason: the founder's session needs the same chain and removal law. The ruling flips on a verified equivalent issue path with the same history.
* ''Routes at the cutover'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-15). Walker arms, the sibling dial and the herm membership relay ship SHUT by name; H opens walkers with its live grant reader, W9 opens the sibling at face scope, and an explicit Nexus opens the herm relay. The oracle read face and reader convert where browser boot reads the oracle in production; elsewhere they ship SHUT by name after measuring `pullAndVerifyOracle`'s caller. The waystone reads the Nexus's one public answer before it trusts its engine. An untested old-wire fallback counts as open. The reason: a closed route grants no silent legacy authority. The ruling flips on a proven gate-native caller for each shut route.
* ''The floor announcement'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-15). `waking-floor-announce` signs one canonical body per carried Nexus under that Nexus's floor bundle signing key: `{ kind: "floor-announcement", nexus, floorPin, standing, prior }`, `prior` naming the digest of the announcement this floor made before for that Nexus (null at the first). No counter and no position orders it: a reader holding an announcement takes only the one that names it as `prior`, and `foldFloorAnnouncements` names a fork rather than settling it by recency. It travels only inside an authenticated socket or relay relation. The reader checks the already pinned floor pin, domain, Nexus and route, and treats the body as a peer hint alone. The reason: a floor hint must neither bootstrap its own trust nor replay across Nexuses. The ruling flips on a smaller body that still refuses cross-Nexus replay, stale replacement and first-contact self-certification.
* ''Fleet join serves every face'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-15). `fleet join` names its face with `--face <index>`, default `0`; a nonzero face carries that face's certificates, grants and address secret. The reason: a person wears several faces, and each face needs its own fleet relation. The ruling flips on a face-independent proof that carries the same per-face authority without mixing identities.
* ''A realm's steward map holds leaves'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-16). The co-signed `keptBy` names fill `realmStewardMap`: each name a steward's worn per-Nexus face or a Handle, with the bags it keeps. That map answers which bags a steward keeps, never a root question; the realm consult's ROOT map stays `peerContractNymMap`, filled only by a root edge proven at the wire (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/membership-doctrine#/two-maps`>>). The reason: a leaf that answers a root question links the two maps the doctrine keeps apart. The ruling flips if a root edge proven at the reading seat comes to fill a realm map, which then stands beside this one under the root map's own name.
* ''The re-cut's eight forks'' (house, under the operator's delegation, 2026-10-31). The cut reads left eight forks; the house talked each out two rounds against canon and a Gaia and an Elyncia story, and none survived to the operator: `daemonAuth` lands as one pure shared builder in mesh; node and browser pin one boot list; seven boot cells refuse a read before set by name; the share verdict lands one law per relation, measured before it merges; the stage-cut and the verb math take pure deciders and a pure module first; the shared stages converge on node's fuller form; the login suffix retires and custody resolves its home once at open; and a removed joiner never re-enters on an invite minted before the removal, its mechanism decided by the red. The floor stands at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/test-recut-road`>> #/the-floor, and the T items carry each settlement.

* ''The channel of intent'' (operator, 2026-10-20: //"Approve your leans, land the full models into the architecture now"//). The daemon wiki keeps working memory; a face's intents travel on that face's own intent plane, `@intents-<tag>`, a fifth face plane beside the four; a vessel's own records rest in its daemon bag, unmirrored; the face's pins rest in the face's custody and `@persona`. The approved leans: the plane stands from the first landing, never staged in the daemon bag; the winner reads by the intent's own preference list, else the lowest `H(atom ‖ intent id)`, and never elects; a single-effect fleet-wide verb never addresses "any device", and a human answer may name its executor; a claim never expires, a device's staleness stays a local suspicion, and the operator's release always wins; an `every` intent reaches a later-joined device unless its bound names a roster edge; a far agent reaches the plane through a relay seat, opt-in per face and ring-gated, its research before its build; an intent voids by supersession, a revoked delegation, a passed roster edge or the operator's cancel; compaction folds by the operator's act, never a timer; a local verb that touches its own vessel alone bypasses the plane and leaves its receipt in that vessel's bag. The plane carries a hand's decisions that cross devices, never its motion (the rubric, <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-intent-plane`>> #/the-rubric). Item `I1`.
* ''The place signer's fifth duty'' (operator, 2026-10-20, approving the controller's lean). A place's finish at another place's gate, `GATE_PLACE_FINISH_DOMAIN`, joins the place signer's floor duties: a faceless place that reads another's floor performs a floor act. Each of the five duties binds its domain by an exact head at byte zero, in one table beside the allowlist; `X0` moves the framing into the signer.

* ''Sync carries; verification decides'' (operator, 2026-10-20, approving the research at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/identity-invariants-2026`>> and the controller's leans). A mutable CRDT carries identity claims and never decides them. Each face holds, on each device, a ''sealed identity anchor'' in its own custody (a D record): its PersonaGroup, its persona-KEL prefix, a commitment to its root, its device edge's digest, and a known frontier that never falls. Only founding, admission and the recovery rites (recovery contact rotation, bundle restore, root cards) write an anchor; sync never writes one. Founding and admission acts, device edges and lease advances travel as signed records in the face's `@persona` plane, a signed-entry log every reader verifies against the anchor and the KEL; two signed acts at one sequence surface as a fork, never a CRDT winner. The face planes hold verified projections. A device reads freely; an act that widens authority (admit, grant, re-key, seal to a reader) checks the sealed frontier and refuses a target that names a newer bound, its receipt naming the frontier it acted under. Transparency and witnesses for equivocation wait for their own item. Wave C carries the build inside `K15` step 6.
* ''Wave B's forks'' (operator, 2026-10-20, approving the controller's leans). A fresh PersonaGroup mints a fresh atom, never an orphan's seed. A tab reads its face's pins from its custody anchor before it opens its session, so the tab's own Repo retires; and each face holds its own social planes in the browser, keyed by PersonaGroup as on the node. Both ride Wave C with the anchor.

* ''The order of the next waves'' (operator, 2026-10-20, approving the controller's leans). Wave C (`K15` step 6 under the identity anchor) runs next, with `W26`'s slices 3, 4 and 6 beside it on the floor's own ground, their composer hunks passing to the controller as patches, and slice 5 after it, since its accepted floor rests as a face's sealed record (a D record under the face's own FCK once Wave C lands); `I1` follows the anchor; the far agent's relay seat takes its own research after `I1`; `G37`'s renames ride `X0`'s bake; the sensorium resumes after `X0`, on the operator's word.

* ''The edge frontier'' (operator, 2026-10-20, approving the controller's leans after the research at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/edge-frontier-2026`>>). Nothing signed today lets a reader tell a device never set aside from a raise erased or withheld, so the house adds a signed ''edge frontier'' per board, its own domain-separated certificate type, the persona KEL resolving its signer's key. Every hand that can raise on a board signs its own stream's frontier (a fleet board's root; a realm board's charter quorum, with its holder where vouch edges raise), and a frontier commits its counted acts through its boundary as a content-addressed snapshot, its parent frontier, and its roster and epoch lineage. A raise shadows its target at once for every reader that sees it, and counts toward widening once its signer registers it into the next frontier. Sync always carries acts, frontiers and snapshots. A widening act (admit, grant, a content key sealed or re-sealed to a reader) reads its target against a frontier of every raising hand that extends any head the reader pinned and includes every raise it has seen; it acts as of that frontier, its receipt naming it, and waits as unknown where none qualifies. Every fleet and realm founds with a signed empty genesis frontier, and an admit bundle carries the current frontier and its snapshot. The fleet board's frontier stands built with Wave C, and the realm boards' leg landed with K17-R in PR #97. Item `K17`.

!!! The fresh start
* ''Old shapes burn'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-28: //"O-takiage the old shapes & shape gates now. Its design time crud buildup that unfortunately causes agents to repeat these patterns. Early alpha law holds - we dont know the final shapes yet ... after founding these guards become important, so we should have clean design space to think through"//). Source never recognises bytes or shapes from before a format cut, to refuse them by name or to read them tolerantly: a cut re-founds. Live refusals of today's shapes (a wrong key, a torn record, a foreign face) stand. The evergreen witness holds an `old-shape` class at zero; boundary guards for pinned bytes get designed after the founding, in clean space (porch bundle six, PR #109).
* ''No pave or wipe command'' — such a command carries too much risk. See #/fresh-start.


!!! The test refactor
* ''The test architecture re-cuts before the founding'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-31, lifting the 2026-10-29 ruling that set it after): train T runs beside the founding road and never stalls it (#/stride-t-recut). Until each layer re-cuts, lanes land large batches and run the heavy battery once per batch.
* ''The writable marker and startup order'' (operator ruling, 2026-11-04): the writable marker orders promoted modules after the writable route, and startup takes that marker as its gate.
* ''Unsaved drafts survive a crash'' (operator ruling, 2026-11-04): unsaved drafts survive in CRDT state and file projection, and no TW5 syncer path carries that durability.
* ''Drain pacing and request law'' (operator ruling, 2026-11-04): the Drain runs one latest-state queue across all hand titles, paced by durable acknowledgements and lifecycle requests, with no timer and no syncer.
* ''Island capability record'' (operator ruling, 2026-11-04): each island opens through one record with `caps.receipt`, `caps.cas`, `caps.dyad` and `caps.custodyWrite`.
* ''Code wards bind the daemon and the kahua stage, never a user's wiki'' (operator ruling, 2026-11-04): a StartupAction in the daemon island or a `kahua-<tag>` stage runs only from genesis or the promoted set; every other layer's StartupAction rides inert and names itself in the boot log. A user's own wiki keeps full caps and runs its StartupActions. The hand-gesture ward on `promote-commit` and `kapae-lower` stays a later item, and the promote hand gate (the session profile chooses its own hand) stays held for the operator.
* ''Owed from the promote train's final reads'' (2026-11-04): the CAS holds its pins forever, since `pin` has no unpin; the daemon doc's promote-state snapshot grows without a bound; a draft floor's lift gives its destination no rebind between carry and burn; a receipt wait on the vessel settles on a closed channel but not on a record that withdraws `receipt` mid-wait (the island's teardown closes its capability first); routine daemon restart reasons (a hand's promote verb) exist on the node alone, since the browser has no routine daemon restart; a lift's in-process carry memory cannot tell a user-removed title from a lost one, so the destination-holds-it check stays unbuilt.
* ''Owed from the promote train's last round'' (2026-11-05, the fourth Drain swing cured: a displaced whole-drain target released an act's receipt hold empty, and the pump starved a title behind one re-marked every flight): a `boundary()` caller's continuation stays retained while a receipt hangs, bounded by the acts that asked and released by the receipt capability's close at teardown, since no timer decides; a floor document peers declare unavailable (`find` rejects) wakes no lift pass, because only a peer's connection could wake it, while the seat still wakes on grant, lease and group-document changes; the rehome of a daemon-bag binding mints its fresh document before its flush, so one stall-then-recover leaves one orphan document behind; the daemon holder's restart listeners read the restart's first reason, never the emergency that upgraded it mid-prepare (no listener reads the reason today).
* ''Owed after the 2026-11-05 lands'' (PRs #159, #160 and #162): the store door's budget timeouts (`STORE_DOOR_PHASES`, ten, forty-five and ten seconds) decide a refusal by wall clock, against NO GLOBAL NOW, while the recompose-window wait for place records ends on the unlock event and holds; a behavioural test for the worker's `workerData` path, which hands island workers their CAS dirs (session islands receive none); a herm-daemon entry in the capability inventory; the `foreign-title-projection` turn-order flake, narrowed by a named heuristic stability wait (50 of 50 green under load) and owed a pinned handshake; the harness suite's `run.log` timing case in `tools/launch-codex.test.mjs` ("the child's output landed in run.log through the held fd"), which reddened the span's CI once in the witness cell and joins the harness deep read; the golden-whole-road `W3` flake, which a scout over its failing jobs read as a timing race on the glass port adaptor and no regression of the Drain (the Drain's diff touches waiters alone): its test half landed with the CI container (`ea6e38643`, the walk raises the island barrier before its click and waits the shared bound), and its product half stays owed as `B7`; the mirror's two residuals: a one-tick window where a held peer entry can land before a keystroke's change dispatches, and the record grain's last-writer-wins when a keystroke races a settle; the kahua stage's verdict door and its reader each run the Ed25519 signature equation (the door's double equation), so one check should serve both; the native WebCrypto Ed25519 speed lift (the daemon island's CPU burns in `@noble/curves` while verdicts memoize by exact bytes, true-only); the launcher's seat-law appendix tells every seat to name a stop in `out.md`, which a read seat cannot write (it hands back through its final message), so the read-seat brief line wants its own wording; two Train T pins from the 2026-11-04 test-surface reads (80 PRs grew the suite by 425 test files and 2,072 cases, 73% of the added cases in new files, with five consolidation targets; `file-path-retired.test.ts` outlived the one-commit rule, and `two-vessel-mesh.test.ts` runs in no project).
* ''Owed, carried forward from the record batches'' (2026-10-31 to 2026-11-04; each reads as owed until a lane re-measures it against the tree):
** ''After the founding'': the comms channel for non-Claude seats (lane message tiddlers on the daemon wiki, operator 2026-11-04) rides #/the-order, rank 31. A Sol YIN reader over `operator-daemon-behavior.ts` and its `onEa` boot chain (1555 lines, 108 awaits), applying the house laws, then a YIN lane with five cures: (1) the working attachment resolves only the working slot; (2) authority readiness separates from maintenance, moving the floor migration, unbound-mint enumeration, refusal filing and additional-face boot to owned background stages; (3) every drain breathes, with a bounded batch per host turn and one lifecycle owner; (4) idempotency at the crash boundaries, so binding mint and lift rehome resume an owned durable op; (5) scopes derive and sovereignty stays. The ordered boot runs authority, archive standing, working-only attachment, pointer landed, verified dispatch, `ea`, then seat and lift, intents, extra faces, maintenance; the pointer wait is unbounded today.
** ''Liveness'' (operator coffee-talk, 2026-11-03): fixed liveness bounds (the eight-second document bound on lift reads and flushes, the watchdog's breath silence) move toward breath-accrued suspicion (phi accrual from breath inter-arrival history at the FfzClock grain), per the clockless lease model. Safety stays clock-free. Landed markers say `breath`, never `heartbeat`.
** ''The shared-holder tabs'' (operator, 2026-11-02, the page-owned keys' lane; the keys themselves burned, #/rulings 2026-11-12): a holder-protocol restart for SharedWorker shared-holder tabs (a holder-side swap broadcast to every tab), and the one-by-one fail-closed of those tabs on their own arrival, wait on whether shared-holder mode survives the browser vessel lift.
** ''Promote and lift residuals'': the CAS pin has no unpin, so promoted bytes (the operator's own, possibly private, code in cleartext in a CAS built for public genesis blobs) stay pinned after an unpromote, which weighs the sealed-CAS train up; the kāpae shadow cache has no size bound (name a ceiling of standing edges per face, and a raise past it refuses by name); the browser board watcher has no successful-shutdown hook; the lift-once guard keys on title, so two door instances racing, or another title in one proposal batch, can slip it (bind single use to the proposal digest across the batch); a behavioural test that rotates a root and reads every founding pin unchanged (only a source check stands); `resolve-binding.ts` and `slot-doc-resolver.ts` create docs with an unbounded `whenReady` (bound them with the defer-only pattern); a fresh destination doc's `repo.flush` sometimes never returns (find why in the storage adapter or flush path); when the rehome flush times out a fresh doc and its cap already exist (mint after the flush, or clean up the orphan); deterministic materialization (`deterministic-doc.ts`) catches every find failure as first boot and should tell a confirmed absence from a timeout; the rest door's gate wait has no deadline; the promote live probe proves a module ran by reading the daemon's `unrouted-save` log line, so before that warning quiets, the staged hearth needs a bag that routes the probe's titles; a `lares` CLI verb for `island.restart` and a walk over a held-open UDS session.
** ''L4 security-read residuals'': (LOW) a non-default same-origin genesis base with a foreign-origin descriptor reads `cross` and a no-relay non-default base boots uncompared, so require a descriptor on the page origin whenever the base differs from the default; `bags//daemon/x` with an empty segment reads as not control-plane (fold empty segments or refuse `//` in lift titles); `$:/tags/RawMarkup|Global|Stylesheet`, a charset'd js type and core or plugin title overrides still lift (operator-signed, fleet-reaching); a control that `<root>/wikis/daemon/` stays absent; the island verb's `ensure` comes from its caller (idempotent, a note). The hand-only key or per-call gesture and the self-reported proposal taint with replay stand beside the lift tension. Hypothesis, never a claim: that a hand-only refusal string steers an injected AI; measure it with the sensorium self-measure before any canon vows it.
** ''Harden residuals'': `genesis-floor.ts` and `meme-face.ts` call the bare frame verdict and skip the smol cross-check (a TOML reject loses root fields and never gains an address).
** ''Isomorphism'': the browser vessel composes no recall, telemetry or worldline verb plane, which ride the shared daemon verb provider cap on node; name whether the browser composes them or they stay node-only by nature.
** ''A far pattern integrity'' (operator, 2026-11-02): //user-critical acts take a gesture page-realm code cannot reach//, refined way down the road. The prior art rests in git (the promote branch's closed shadow root, `isTrusted` gate and one-shot claim, burned). The consent research measured that `isTrusted` cannot tell a DOM-only external driver from an in-page `.click()` (CDP, Playwright, WebDriver and OS input arrive trusted; extension content-script clicks arrive untrusted) and that a closed shadow root is no boundary. The lean: a host strip plus a private consent MessagePort registered at trusted bootstrap before promoted code runs, ordinary mirror ports proposing and never committing, a nonce receipt binding {act, wiki, digest, device, frontier}, activation never required; stronger boundaries are a separate-origin mirror iframe or an extension-owned window. It must never block an AI driving the browser.
** ''Grammar, CI and tools'': the weave passes a stored `bcc` through without recomputing, and the seal covers STX to ETX only, so the SOH head (`to=`, namespace) sits outside it (should the seal cover the head?); a sidebar-search autofocus when the mirror's full promotion lands may steal focus mid-fill and feed the draft-save flake; `tsc -p packages/lararium-tw5` reports `TS6059` rootDir errors from memetic-frame imports (the typecheck witness passes; find whether the witness invocation misses them); `tools/land-train.test.mjs` mints `land-train-*` temp dirs under `tmpdir()` and never removes them (an `afterEach` rmSync of every dir it mints); `tools/crossing-witness.sh` fails in lane worktrees because its docker build wants `mempalace/README.md`, which a provisioned worktree lacks; the CI venv cache key ignores the mempalace submodule commit and the Python patch version; CI wants bubblewrap and unprivileged user namespaces so the wall proofs in `tools/commit-relay.test.mjs` run instead of skip; the launcher reads true usage from rollout `token_count` events (`seat-harness` holds the guard), and a lead lifetime cap or fresh-per-step lead stands in the harness.
* ''Mana-triad retirement'' (operator ruling, 2026-11-04): mana, manao and manaoio scalars retire house-wide, and no gate or federation authority derives from them.
* ''Swarm controls at standing'' (operator ruling, 2026-11-04): wiki tiddlers that alter swarm control read valid only when the vessel or the hand signs them, and item 7 lands at standing before founding.
* ''Red-first, homed'' (operator ruling, 2026-11-04): each fix lands a behavior red with its control in the narrowest existing home, and a new file opens only at a distinct runtime, boundary, harness or suite class.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/decisions`>>

!! Decisions ~ what shapes unbuilt work, and why

The hosted walk's own mechanics (the token, the grant, the spent-set, the hosting doc) read in
`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/membership-doctrine#/the-invite`. The decisions below constrain a slice
still on the road.

* ''One sorter, inside the gate, before the verdict.'' Every vessel socket opens through the sealed face gate's attach, verdict and open sequence; the gate sorts before the verdict, so posture reads once at admission and never at share time. `GateSessionChannel` opens only after the verdict (`face-gate.ts`, `gate-session-channel.ts`). One gate stands per (Nexus, route).
* ''The knock.'' A gate answers an upgrade only on a path derived from its own key (`gateKnock` via `knockSegment` in `packages/lararium-mesh/src/gate-bundle.ts`), so a dialer holding no pin draws no HTTP 101 — the WireGuard mac1 shape. The path still rides the upgrade in the clear on plain `ws`; full client-proves-first waits for Noise IK (R-WIRE).
* ''Silence, one deadline.'' No denial frame and no close codes: one drop deadline drawn at accept, so verify time leaks nothing and a PRIVATE stranger looks exactly like a failed proof.
* ''The multi-Nexus fold.'' A gate answers strangers if ANY Nexus it stands in reads OPEN. One gate key serves every Nexus a vessel carries, so this rule reduces exactly to per-Nexus posture once each Nexus carries its own wire key (R-WIRE) — not an interim.
* ''Carriage keys on the guest's PROVEN leaf.'' A hearth-issued stub would read as a bearer capability to another guest's record and as a hearth-minted cross-lineage linker. The leaf already proves on every socket, so keying on it adds no linkage the hearth lacks; a fresh leaf opens a fresh carriage (#/open, carriage across leaves).
* ''One store door.'' A command that touches the store runs inside the standing vessel over the owner-only socket, else owns the store itself for that moment. A second Repo beside a live holder writes bytes the replica never sees. `tools/direct-repo-witness.sh` names the two lawful `new Repo(` sites and reads any other as DRIFT.
* ''No hearth-local deny set.'' Kapae and the roll suffice; a deny set would make a seizable list.
* ''No node carriage.'' A node serves as its own durable store and no story walks its carriage; ruling N holds as a sorter property and W as the hearth's service. Node carry would open a new user story, never owed by N or W.
* ''Hosting stays on the worn persona leaf.'' ROOT-ON-FOUNDER keeps a persona root on one vessel, so one face cannot host from two stores unseen; the first-roll rail surfaces the remaining case. The face-switch act inherits it.
* ''A cliff takes a funnel, then one assembled commit.'' Where one shape change crosses three packages, the fixtures funnel through one helper first; narrow builders each write one patch against a fixed contract; one lander proves them together in a scratch worktree and commits once, so the main tree never holds half a flip. Three cliffs stand on the road: the charter flip, the Nexus rename and the socket cutover.
* ''The socket's handshake changes once.'' R-WIRE and the face proof build as ONE wire train: the signed admit, then the fleet-join door, then one socket cutover (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof`>>). The tree between those steps reads no earlier shape; it stands as a build order, never a compatibility mode.
* ''A pure library names its caller.'' A module built ahead of its consumer stays out of the package barrel until the commit that consumes it. Until a production path calls it, a module stands specified in code, never built.
* ''`vessel found` keeps a charterless road.'' The Stable founding act becomes the HEARTH's rite; a herm still founds as a place with no face, and a joiner still founds through an admit.
* ''The carriage verifier holds the root'': it checks the epoch, the signature AND a subject root it holds locally, and refuses by name without that root.
* ''Recovery contact placement'' reads as advice the house names at enrolment, never a refusal. ''QR pairing'' shows a check string on both screens after the channel opens.
* ''The floor vessel key drops its next-key commitment'', inside the one identity train. It changes the vessel's signed inception bytes; the operator may overturn it.
* ''A long-silent fleet device'' catches up before it acts: a presentation carries its presenter's frontier of the face's withdrawal history, and a gate that holds a later one answers `unconfirmed` and hands over what the device lacks. No clock; a house reading the operator may overturn (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof#/the-withdrawal-proof`>>).
* ''The strangers' dial drops the NEWEST arrival'' when its slots fill; a slot frees by the operator's answer or a roll.
* ''Every browser leaf counts as evictable'': `storage.persist()` refuses in a plain tab, so no leaf holds the only copy of anything, and no passkey slot stands as the only way in (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/device-capabilities-2026`>>).
* ''A restarted always-on node serves no hosted phone until a human unlocks.'' The resolver and the terminator keep answering, so the page loads and the sync waits; the page says so in one line, until the ember lands.
* ''A crossing gets recorded, never interior life.'' Only an authority, vessel, federation or externally meaningful crossing becomes distributed canon (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/mandate-activity-provenance`>>).
* ''A package names no vessel.'' Every entity starts at the herm floor; a seated persona lights the Lararium stack over it. Only a lararium lights a Pronaos, and a herm mirrors a house's published bytes by CID (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/pronaos#/the-placement`>>).
* ''Deferred by the house'': realm feeding (two carriers disagree on max over per-writer slots against a blank merge; it waits for the first-realm arc), community exit, and funding, where payment may buy capacity and never standing.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-road`>>

!! The road ~ what stays unbuilt, in the order a builder walks it

''The organizing law'': identity bytes (the Nexus AID, the device edge, the dyad scope, the seats, the
sentinel) change ONCE, inside one train. ''The owner law'': each slice lands its logic in modules, and one
named owner per step commits the one compose hunk in `open-node-vessel.ts` and `open-browser-vessel.ts`;
every other lane stops at a module boundary. Each lane's first commit carries its canon. ''The fence''
(#/rulings) bounds the whole road: every slice that changes a shape lands before the second operator seats a key in the first Nexus.

''The order'': HEARTH → GROUND → SUBSTRATE → HARBOR → KAHU PLANE → ONE FACE → TWO HEARTHS, with train T's re-cut beside every path (#/the-order ranks the slots). The re-found's train
rides inside it — R-ENGINE's fold and R-CUSTODY's seal in SUBSTRATE; R-CHARTER → R-WIRE, then R-KAHU,
R-ENGINE's offers and R-EMBER, in the KAHU PLANE. Where the train's older order (the engine after the wire)
and the six paths disagree, operator-ruled order governs: the engine fold and the custody
seal land early, and the container's tail lands before the charter train.

!!! 0 · HEARTH ~ the memory-stage hearth (no identity bytes; leads the road; items in #/stride-0-hearth)
* ''The walk'': one machine, one operator. A node founds and stands at its floor, unlocks by the operator's hand, and serves its own page from its own origin; the page stands a browser vessel at its floor, binds a passphrase slot before any face secret, and joins the node's face through the fleet-join acts; a page edit reaches `<projections>/wikis/daemon-<F>`, a CLI write shows in the page, and the MCP seat reads and writes the same wiki (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/pronaos`>> #/the-first-arrival; <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/session-contract`>> #/the-hands). The harness walks it on a scratch root through the test door; the operator walks it on the hearth that holds the house's memory.
* ''The stage'': tags and one index view on the daemon wiki, never a new store or plane. A working stage holds what the controller's scratch files hold today; an episodic tier keeps scout reports, walks and incidents; survivors reach `bags/` carriers by promotion under a person's ruling, and the fire of otakiage forgets what landed (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/otakiage`>>). No item reads a clock: an item's freshness reads as the commit or wave that last re-checked it, and a recurrence counts repeat incidents.
* ''The seat'': an agent writes the stage as a seat, never as a key; its writes land in the working layer, and the profile gate refuses it every authority-changing verb (`F11`).
* ''The owner law holds here too'': a hearth item reaches `open-node-vessel.ts` and `open-browser-vessel.ts` only through the named owner, and the shore's files (`packages/lararium-web/**`) take the hearth's page items as a shore lane.

!!! 1 · GROUND ~ the residue (no identity bytes; waits on nothing; rides beside SUBSTRATE on disjoint files)
* ''The receipt ledger's direct leg'': the three receipts of #/rulings stand where a CLI meets its vessel (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/receipt-ledger`>>). The leg across a herm rides THE WIRE (`W12` of #/stride-1-spine).
* ''The evergreen sweep and custody's edges'', itemized as `G6` (#/after-the-seat) and `G7` (#/stride-1-spine).
* ''Measures'': the full-corpus per-verb flush re-measure; pack-PIN recursion; a production `assertBodyIndexTier` caller.
* ''Canon direction the build owes'': canon crosses hearth → leaf; a leaf never writes the hearth's canon documents, and edits land in the working layer. The admitted leaf-side mount flag stops mount-path writes, while Repo sync carries those documents in both directions.
* ''A binding lands after its document rests'': a slot mint records the binding only on the vessel store's acknowledgment (the receipt capability, `receipt:request` / `receipt:issued`); a mint no binding names stays listed (`lares vessel unbound`), and nothing removes one.

!!! 2 · SUBSTRATE ~ two SOLO relays, the fold first, then the seal
Each runs one spirit per commit, and every commit leaves HEAD building. No other node lane runs beside the
seal: it owns the compose hunk.

''R-ENGINE, the fold.''
* The R-ENGINE fold target dependency line, in fixed order: automerge-repo and its four listed adapters at alpha.5 → `@automerge/automerge` 3.4.1 → `@keyhive/keyhive` 0.3.0-alpha.1; no Subduction or Onomancy dependency → the genesis re-bake (`GENESIS_ENGINE_CID`, every fixture holding the hearth true-name, the untracked CAS in the same commit) → the fold's own live proof on the rebuilt image, with a pushed shard run reading green. The manifests hold all three dependency steps (`packages/lararium-node/package.json`, `packages/lararium-keyhive/package.json`); a Keyhive Archive and a cap event carry no format tag, so bytes one Keyhive version wrote refuse under another and a version move re-founds every vessel. The fold's own live proof on the rebuilt image stands owed.
* TW5 core and the memetic-wikitext bootstrap fold into `engineCid`; the vocabulary leaves the engine as content; the coal-only seed carries no `schemaVersion`; the island `schema_version` drops its counter.

''R-CUSTODY, the seal'' (P3-SEAL).
* ''What stands sealed'': the custody root and the `identity-anchors` family: the keyhive archive, each face's own veil archive (`veil-archive-g<group digest>.bin` on the node, keyed by its PersonaGroup; the browser's `veil-archive-h<N>` record still keys by handle index until `K15` step 6), each face's anchors and the anchor roster. A founding seals them from its first write and hands out every keyhive Archive it stood before the provider leaves; the veil derives from the sealed custody root; a seat (`custody-seat.ts`) carries one holder per identity home, and each record reads whole or refuses by name, never empty. A creator that stands again restores from its Archive: cap events carry membership and never a creator's key agreement state. A stand mints one contact card and its Archive leaves after that mint, so the Archive lineage opens every prekey the identity ever presented. A browser vessel keeps the Archives its founding and its admit hand out in its keystore beside the device seed, in the clear, until browser custody wraps both (`browser-keyhive-archive.ts`).
* ''Every writer family rests under its custody key'': the carrier table (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-table`>>; `vault-carriers.ts`) names `floor` and `floor-plain` outside the vessel's keys, `hot` and `cold` under the vessel key, `serving` under SK, and `outbox` under an outbox key the vessel key wraps. SK mints at the founding; a human unlock opens it through the vessel key's entry in its serving tree (`sk-slots`, a bare `floor` record; `serving-key.ts`, `custody-session.ts`). Serving records and ember slots stay owed (`E2`); sealed outbox parcels stand, while ship and delete stay owed (`K13`). Each sealed record passes through the one sealed writer (sidecar → re-open → compare → rename, never sealing in place) and the sealed open. The per-face backup bundle stands built, cut and restore.
* ''No keyring stands'' (operator ruling, #/rulings): the code holds no per-Nexus convergence secret. No custody record, seat mint, admission delivery or charter commitment carries one, and a persona admission's signed transcript names no keyring digest. A private body seals under its bag's content key, which reaches its readers through a lockbox (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store#/keys`>>). A seat's act and a roll mint nothing beside the charter epoch. The same ruling's standing law binds every later slice: let a counter, wherever one appears, order one writer's own emissions only; keep the face at the session capability; keep every verified fork head visible; retain by causal evidence.
* ''The handle-book'': the recogniser's handle-book rests sealed under the S key. Nothing at its path reads as a book that recognises nobody; a record that stands and does not open, or opens to another shape, refuses by name at every read and every write, and a save lands only over nothing or over a snapshot that reads whole (`packages/lararium-node/src/node-circle-store.ts`). The follow graph holds one home, the persona-scoped circles plane the daemon's circle verbs write, and no custody record carries an edge.
* ''The fleet-join family'': one face's root-signed answer carries its device edge, signed gate carriage, island reach, sealed address secret, vessel face-key certificates and add-a-face grants. A join lands through one checked door. `device-admit` names the internal signed edge format, and `cabal-join` still names people and realm admission.
* ''The vessel-identity family'': the persona roster, the pet-name map, the declaration map and the published-face record rest sealed under the S key as hot records, and the worn face lives as session state and rests in no record (`F9`); each persona root rests as a cold record, opened for one act; the floor vessel key rests beside its signed inception, every load proves that inception, and no next-key seed or commitment rides it. The roster commits a root: a mint writes the root record, then the roster line that carries the root's public key, so a reading of a public key answers off the hot roster and opens no root. A root record with no roster line reads as an orphan: no reader holds it, `lares persona list` names it, and the same mint run again adopts it. A roster line whose root record went missing, or names another key, refuses by name at the act that needs the seed, and nothing mints over it. A hot record with nothing at its path reads as holding nothing; a record that stands and does not open, or opens to another shape, refuses by name at every read and every write. A vessel key mints only inside a held custody, and lands with its inception under the custody's one claim: the key file commits the mint, so two acts never commit two keys, and a boot loads a key and never mints one. The floor reads no record of the family, so a carried-Nexus reading by persona (a seated chair, a kept consent) answers empty until the key arrives, and a sovereign composition reads the roster whole before it stands (`packages/lararium-node/src/node-vessel-identity.ts`). A floor composition reads no roster, so it shuts every fleet lane: it sorts every device as set aside, sets a proven sibling aside, and its fetch door reads no peer as fleet, the pinned join gate's key included (`packages/lararium-node/src/floor-fleet-lanes.ts`). It still folds the one fleet board its own device edge names in the clear, so the Binding Gate reads this vessel's own seat at every standing.
* ''The device recovery share'': each face's device share rests as one COLD record under the S key, opened for the act that mints it and the quorum that assembles it (`packages/lararium-node/src/recovery-share-store.ts`). Nothing at a face's path reads as none landed; a record that stands and does not open, or opens to another shape, refuses the read and the write by name, so a wrong key replaces no share and no split lands over one it could not read. A share lands once per generation: a second split of the same generation refuses, since it would orphan the off-device shares a person already holds. A split hands the person both off-device shares before the device share rests, so a run that stops between the two leaves nothing sealed and the same command splits afresh.
* ''The walk records'': each hearth a vessel walks at keeps one HOT record sealed under the S key, named by a digest of that hearth's gate key (`packages/lararium-node/src/node-walk-store.ts`). A record holds bearer invites, the hearth's grant and one receipt per carried document (`{ cid: "blake3:<hex>", ref, key }`, whose `key` opens that document), so it counts as a secret record. The record rests in the storage home of the custody seat: the store takes no directory, so no caller files it beside a document store the vessel key never spans. Nothing at a hearth's path reads as no walk there; a record that stands and does not open, opens to another shape, or rests filed under another hearth's name refuses the read, the list and the update by name, and an update seals a whole record or nothing. Each change reads, decides and seals under one custody holder, so one invite leaves a wallet once. A floor composition reads no walk record and presents no walk; a fresh browser device stands at the floor under the floor card it carries in at the page's carriage door, and a joined face crosses under its root-signed pin. A key that locks under a live walk costs the walk one message, which it logs. `lares walk`, `walk take` and `walk invite` ride the custody door (`walk.state` · `walk.take` · `walk.invite`): a carried invite as a verb's argument, or an invite as a verb's outcome, would rest in the daemon's document in the clear.
* ''The hosting records'': a hearth's hosting state, each spend and its walkers' carriage rest as HOT records sealed under the S key, in the storage home of the custody seat (`packages/lararium-node/src/hosting-store.ts`, `hosting-carry.ts`). The sealed writer writes a whole file and appends to none, so each spend lands as ONE record of its own, named by a digest of its nonce or marker: the record's presence IS the burn, and it reads, decides and seals under one custody holder. The spend record commits a redemption, and what rides ahead of it re-derives: the grant from the epoch, the leaf, the nonce and the claim, so a crash before the answer replays as `retry`; the operator's counts from the records standing, by name. A roll's state record commits the roll, and the hosting doc re-derives from it: the next roll and each sovereign stand land an act the sealed state holds and the doc lacks (`landHostingActs`). The sweep of dead epochs' records passes through the seat's removal door (`removeCustodyRow`), which removes only a record this vessel's key opens; a spend that sealed after a roll retired its epoch burns nothing live and leaves with the next sweep. Each carried value rests as one record whose name carries its address and its length, so a guest's bytes read off its records and no counter stands beside them. One process serves a hearth's carriage (the standing vessel's own gate, under its store claim), and its contacts and deposits run in turn, so a quota reads the bytes the last deposit left and a reclaim never discards on a reading a contact moved meanwhile. A record that stands and does not open refuses by name: a burn nobody can read never reads as unspent, and a carried value nobody can open never reads as absent. A floor composition reads no hosting record, so a locked hearth sorts no socket as a walker. `lares host`, `host roll` and `host invite` ride the custody door (`host.state` · `host.roll` · `host.invite`): a minted invite as a verb's outcome would rest in the daemon's document in the clear.
* ''The seal ceremony'': the growth rite's two records rest HOT under the S key in the seal home (`packages/lararium-node/src/seal-ceremony.ts`): `transition-pending.json` prepares a crossing, and `transitions.json` commits its record. A seal verifies the crossing whole, appends its record (the commit point), then removes the pending record, only while that record still holds the crossing it sealed; a seal that committed and stopped resumes to one kept record and no pending record, and kept records with no pending record read clean. A chair's own seat key signs for the ceremony behind the custody door (`seat-key-store.ts`), and only what the standing state licenses: `seal.sign` names a seat by its public key and signs a charter roll that steps from the standing head, reveals at least that head's count of the seats it committed one digest each, seats only keys this vessel holds and re-arms every seat it seats, where the charter's lineage verifies and its head binds the seated roster; it refuses every other image however well formed, and a persona root holds no seat record, so it signs no roll; the rite's hands mark through `crossing.open` · `bind` · `sign` · `witness` · `seal`, which take no image and sign the crossing that stands open (a face's seat keys sign; a witness's root attests). The rite arms itself behind the same door (`seat.arm-genesis`, `seat.arm-roll`): each standing chair's seat key mints there, each chair's NEXT key prints once as a cold-key card the operator reads back before the charter moves (a staged test root alone stands in), a roll adopts each card's key as its chair's seat key, and a superseded seat key retires once no held charter seats it and no open crossing names it (`seat.retire`). An earlier charter shape reads by name (`nexusCharterShape`) and licenses nothing. ''No command file writes a custody record'': a census derives every sealed record's name, every write door and every writer function from the carrier table and the custody modules, and reads red on a command source that calls a door, names a writer function, or names a record beside a file-system write of its own (`packages/lares-cli/tests/laws/custody-stays-behind-the-door.law.test.ts`).
* ''The face proof's records'': `face-proof-key` rests COLD, one face's proof key at one Nexus, held by the root-holding vessel alone, its file named for the face so that face's backup bundle carries it; `vessel-face-key` rests HOT, this vessel's own face-scoped key, and stays on its vessel, outside every bundle. Fleet join calls the certificate and grant path, and the socket cutover (train W) carries the removal and gate session paths. Two doors on the custody seat sign and return (`signAsFace`, `signAsVesselFace`): each signs exactly the image handed in under the key its scope names and hands out no secret, and a store never lands over another standing key (`packages/lararium-node/src/face-key-custody.ts`; the library names the shapes in `FACE_PROOF_CUSTODY_ROWS`).
* ''The gate's two root doors'' (the node composer seats both, `open-node-vessel.ts`): a floor gate's bundle derives through the floor key's door, which answers at a locked vessel, hands back a fresh child and never the floor key (`vesselFloorKeyDoor`, `packages/lararium-node/src/node-vessel-identity.ts`); a sovereign gate's bundle derives through the custody seat's door, which answers only while the seat holds its key (`custodyGateDoor`, `packages/lararium-node/src/custody-seat.ts`). The doors carry two types, so no line swaps them by accident; a root refuses the other tier's door by name; each seat's door refuses the other tier's child; and a weld holds the two mints to the two seats (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof#/the-gate-exchange`>>).
* ''The gate's memory records'' (the vessel's gates read and write them, `vessel-gates.ts`): a sovereign gate's withdrawal memory rests one record per face key in the vessel's storage home, named by the face's proof-subject digest and sealed under the S key, so a locked vessel's disk names no face key (`gate-memory` in the carrier table; `packages/lararium-node/src/gate-memory-rows.ts`). Each write reads, decides and seals under one custody holder, and refuses where it would drop an event the record holds; a locked seat refuses by name. An older copy of a record restored by hand makes a gate forget until the next honest vessel dials, and no fence stands against that hand. A record that stands and does not fold whole refuses by name and reports it (`GateMemoryRowTorn`): the asking gate falls silent, no keep writes over the record, and a person repairs it by hand, where a record read as nothing would let a fresh memory admit a vessel the record once denied. No backup bundle carries the record: a vessel that stands again knows nothing (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof#/the-withdrawal-proof`>>).
* ''The session and the boot'': the daemon stands at the floor holding no S key and unlocks on a custody frame of its owner-only socket (`unlock` · `lock` · `custody` · `act` · `backup`, `packages/lararium-node/src/custody-frames.ts`), handled ahead of every verb, so the credential the CLI reads on its TTY reaches no receipt, outcome or log. One holder keeps the opened S key in node-main (`custody-holder.ts`); an unlock tears the standing composition down and stands a fresh one on the same floor (`open-node-vessel.ts`), a lock returns it to the floor, and three failed unlocks spend a boot's attempts. A composition that refuses above the floor leaves the floor standing and names its fault once. The settle sweeps a sidecar whose write never landed. The test door (`tools/unlock-test-custody.mjs`) opens marked scratch roots alone, and the marker earns one loud line in `vault status` and the boot log. Every boot composes at the floor: nothing stored lifts it. A daemon island holds no S key, so its archive exports cross the custody write door (`custody:write`) and the main thread seals them. Every broad sync port, the daemon island's and each wiki island's, crosses one hardened facade on both ends: an arrival that reads as no sync message drops at the port and never reaches a listener that would throw on it (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/island-repo.ts`). The daemon island's gates ask the main thread what a device's edge reads on its fleet's board through the dyad capability (`dyad:ask`), which answers `aside` wherever it cannot read. Each fold of the fleet's edge board sets off the key side's settle on an unlocked vessel, with no timer and no person: the main thread places the island's `seat-duties` verb, awaits its answer and keeps the signed rolls (`packages/lararium-node/src/seat-settle.ts`; <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae#/what-the-code-holds`>>). Those two capabilities and the receipt capability answer the daemon island's channel alone: each worker's class binds at its spawn (`packages/lararium-node/src/worker-handle.ts`), a wiki island reaches the cas capability alone while its custody write and its receipt ask refuse by name and its dyad ask draws `aside`, and a session island reaches no vessel capability. A command that acts with no vessel standing opens its own key session at the first sealed record it touches (`packages/lares-cli/src/offline-custody.ts`): a terminal prompt, and under a marked scratch root the published test route. A sealed record's act rides an `act` frame of the custody door and never a store verb: one typed table bounds the acts (`packages/lararium-node/src/sealed-acts.ts`), a standing vessel answers with no second prompt, and beside a standing vessel a command opens no key session of its own, while a root's recovery split refuses there (`packages/lares-cli/src/sealed-act-door.ts`). The fleet mirror of a pet-name and a declared Handle rides that door too: `selves.carry` names a face and which name, the vessel's main thread reads the name off its sealed record and writes it onto that face's own persona plane and no other face's, only where that plane's rest seals (a clear rest refuses the carry and the name stays on its record), `lares persona sync` fills and never replaces, and no verb takes or answers a face's own name (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/waking-floor#/the-shape`>>).
* ''The per-face backup bundle, its cut'': `lares vault backup --face <N> --out <file>` cuts one face's bundle through the standing vessel's custody door, under the law at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-cut`>>. The carrier table's bundle column picks the rows, the frame asks the vessel's own route again, the daemon island seals the memory section to the pair the bundle key derives, and the command binds the bundle's routes in its own process (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/face-bundle.ts`; `packages/lararium-node/src/face-bundle-cut.ts`, `custody-frames.ts`; `packages/lares-cli/src/vault-backup.ts`). The handle-book tags each record with the face that recognised it, so a bundle carries its own face's records alone. A node vessel's daemon island stands its content-key holder off the document it owns; no content key mints on a production vessel until a session signer stands (`U1`), so a cut taken before one stands carries an empty memory section.
* ''The per-face backup bundle, its restore'': `lares vault restore <file>` brings a face back onto a fresh vessel as a recovery act, under the law at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-restore`>>. The vessel checks the root against the newest key history it reads, seals one commit record, lands the carried records additively, and lights the face through the face rite: a new seat under the same fixed prefix (`packages/lararium-node/src/face-bundle-restore.ts`; the act table's `bundle.restore` and `bundle.import` rows; `packages/lares-cli/src/vault-restore.ts`). The rehearsal runs end to end on scratch roots, on the real doors and on standing vessels of the built tree. `--memory` lands the bundle's content keys through the island's import door; where no session signer stands (a vessel at the floor) it refuses `signer-unavailable`.
* ''The custody door and its census'': `lares vault` answers seven verbs (`status` · `unlock` · `lock` · `bind` · `unbind` · `backup` · `restore`), and a credential rides a terminal alone: no flag, environment variable or pipe carries one. `vault status` gives ONE reading of each standing file, derived from the carrier table: a `hot` or `cold` row reads sealed or reads as a fault, a row outside the S key prints the law it rests bare under or reads as a fault where the file breaks that law, and a private file in the three homes that no row names prints by name (`vault-carriers.ts` `outsideTheTable`; `custody-session.ts` `custodyStatus`). `vault unbind --rotate` runs as one act: the rotation's census and its opening of every carrier pass before any byte moves, so a carrier that refuses the rotation leaves the slot bound. `tools/custody-census-witness.sh` founds a vessel on a marked scratch root with the built tree and refuses an owner-only file, a seal or a sidecar outside the table; it carries that rotation to its end on the founded vessel. The law, each bare row's reason and the residue a row's name leaves at rest read at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-table`>>.

!!! 3 · HARBOR ~ the container's tail
* ''A persona door before `up`'': `tools/lares-container.sh` routes CLI acts through the offline door before `up`; after a successful act in a standing vessel the host shim reads the standing verdict and restarts the vessel on `restand`. The verdict reads a LIT face alone, so a `persona wear` that changes the worn face on a standing vessel waits on ONE FACE.
* ''LAN candidate discovery'': the module and wiring stand off by default; `LAR_LAN_DISCOVERY=announce` enables announcements without keys. A real-LAN run and the network mode remain owed (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/harbor#/lan-discovery`).
* ''Household HTTPS'': the compose `https` profile supplies a terminator and a resolver slot, the `issuer` profile supplies a one-shot DNS-01 issuer that reads the registrar's credential from two owner-read files outside both mounts (`docker-compose.surface.yml`; `tools/household-https-witness.sh` walks issuance against a test authority), the resolver answers each household name with its own address from one records file the operator keeps (`tools/household-resolver-witness.sh` walks it against a test zone), and `deviceAdmitSyncUrl` derives relay reach from `LAR_PUBLIC_URL`. The node entry names itself by that setting alone: a boot without one, or with one that names loopback, says so in one loud line, and the first request that arrives under another host logs once per boot (`packages/lararium-node/src/reach-face-watch.ts`). The operator supplies the domain and the credential files. Owed: the renewal verb with its reload, and a first live issuance. No device takes an install (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/harbor#/household-https`, `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/secure-context`).
* ''The harnesses stand on the image'': harnesses run the built image by digest and bind no source tree; the mesh scenarios name their own compose project (`MESH_PROJECT`) and feed their browser vessels from the image; the browser CI runner alone reads the repository read-only. Each harness opens the vessel it staged through the test custody door and reads a hearth as stood only once that door answered an open key; `mesh-scenarios all`, `harbor-walk-witness`, the crossing and herm witnesses, the browser walks and the household HTTPS witness all stand on that shape (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/harbor#/the-harnesses`).
* ''The release manifest'': `tools/release-manifest.mjs` emits the record shape; a real multi-arch index, its push and signing remain owed.

!!! 4 · KAHU PLANE ~ the realms, the signer, and the one AID train
Build order: the realm and signer shapes against the rulings; then R-CHARTER, the one train that moves
identity bytes; then R-WIRE, so every pin changes once.

* ''Realms'': a vessel stands one plane per realm, and a verb names its realm (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/cabal-realm#/many-realms`>>). A cabal reads as its relation closure and takes no key or roster of its own. A cabal realm's documents cross between two operators once the realm's member consult answers for a peer.
* ''The steward signer'': the worn face or a quorum Handle, never the root (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/cabal-realm#/the-steward-signer`>>). The seal, the fold and the steward door stand in code; the composing vessel's `realm-bag` verb takes the door through the compose owner's hand. The docker `meme` scenario then proves it by hand at "B edits, promotes", in the asker's order: B asks to keep the bag with her worn face, and A consents.
* ''Thresholded admission and promotion'' stand built: the chartered classes derive from the charter's own seats and threshold (`chartered-classes.ts`); promotion approves the exact bytes' hash with purpose, surface, class and epoch (`promotion-approval.ts`, `lares nexus promote open|sign|check`); Kahu-cabal admission stands as its own act at the high-impact threshold, where a vouch admits nobody (`kahu-cabal-admission.ts`, `lares nexus cabal`) (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/chartered-classes`>>).

''R-CHARTER, the one AID train.'' Its first stage stands landed (train K of #/the-trains): the chairs sign with seat keys of their own and pre-rotate on cold cards, a Nexus answers to `nexus-<sha>`, the floor key founds by a clockless self-signed inception (its next cold key committed since `W29`), per-Nexus keys mint at random, the PersonaGroup stands as a Keyhive group, and the Stable founding act founds a hearth (`lares vessel found --hearth`): the place, persona zero and a PRIVATE seed charter at one seat, with no keyring, every fleet relation bound to that charter's AID, and every harness that founds a hearth founding through it (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/founding-runbook#/found`>>). What stays owed:
* ''The Nexus founding act'': two founders, each from their own vault, seat one key each in a fresh joint genesis at two of two; each reads the other's seat fingerprint aloud; each seat commits its own cold next digest; each founder then signs a carriage contract that both seats countersign (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/seed-and-nexus`>>). Train N.
* ''The cadence inception'' cited once by a joint Nexus founding, never by a household seed: its shape stands in <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/contract`>>, and its holders stand ruled (#/rulings; `K9`).
* ''Boards resolve per relation''; the fleet-join door carries a device edge and one face's grants; add-a-face (A); the bare primary-path seal import goes; the mirror guard keys on posture.

''R-WIRE, the per-Nexus wire key'' (waits on R-CHARTER).
* A per-Nexus wire key in two tiers (operator ruling): a floor route's bundle derives from the floor key and answers at a locked vessel; a sovereign route's bundle derives from the sealed custody root and stands only while the vessel stands unlocked. One gate per (Nexus, route), so knock, pin, verdict and posture read per Nexus exactly (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof#/the-gate-exchange`>>). The serving route's third tier rides train E (`E3`).
* ''Noise IK, proof-first'': the gate emits nothing before a valid first message; the knock from `gateKnock` and `knockSegment` in `gate-bundle.ts` selects the sealed face gate, and Noise IK carries message one.
* The floor announcement signs under the wire key, folded in here, so gate and announce never sign under two keys.
* One client dial per (hearth, Nexus), so a vessel walks at more than one hearth; until then a second walk hearth refuses aloud.
* The vessel key stops linking a walker's leaves across Nexuses on the wire (`two-maps` clause 5's residual), and the oracle pointer's vessel-key correlation goes with it (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/pronaos#/the-rung-ladder`).
* ''Per-dial transport keys on the sibling channel'': a leaf proves to a herm under a key fresh per dial, and the leaf-peer transcript binds that key in place of the device key the relay stamps, so a herm colluding with a revoked leaf reads no sibling's device key off its own gate (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/identity-slot-policy#/the-persona-group-secret`).
* ''The admit signature the build owes'': the canon-document list and full sovereign sync `GateBundlePin` enter the signed bytes through the fleet answer and admit carriage; `device-admit.ts` mints the internal `device-admit` edge. The browser's first proof rides `Sec-WebSocket-Protocol` (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof`).
* ''Owed (b), the knock tests offline'': the knock path computes an HMAC-derived segment (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/gate-bundle.ts`, `knockSegment`, `gateKnock`); an observer holding candidate keys tests a sniffed knock offline, so not echoing the key protects only against an observer with no candidates.

''Behind the train, each waiting on R-CHARTER''.
* ''R-KAHU'' stands witnessed: a two-of-two charter whose seats live on two vessels opens, cosigns and lands a cabal admission and a promotion, each vessel under its own scratch root (`nexus-cabal.test.ts`, `nexus-promote.test.ts`). The co-signed `keptBy` fills the realm's steward map (#/rulings).
* ''R-ENGINE, the offers'': the engine-offer lineage signs under the cadence threshold, adopted within a k-hop window along `prev`.
* ''R-EMBER'': a household's second device lifts a resting hearth to SERVING and to nothing more (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/ember`>>). It needs the fleet-join door; the slot tree carries passphrase and paper pins, and the ember pin binds in a serving tree of its own.

!!! 5 · ONE FACE ~ the recorded switch act (waits on the signer and the realm shapes)
* The slice builds `session-contract.mem` in its build order and `face-proof.mem`. A session wears the face; the vessel wears none. The switch acts on ONE session and closes and re-dials only that session's links under the incoming face's scoped proof.
* ''Working-layer law the build owes'': one working document per (face, wiki, slot kind); its address derives from a secret only that face's vessels hold. The working layer follows its wiki across recipe recomposition. A leaf with an admit and no seat writes nothing to face wikis until join completion. Vessel removal leaves the address standing; the gate and document membership refuse the removed vessel (operator ruling).
* A wiki names one face's composition over bags; sharing happens at the bag (operator ruling).
* A personal Handle never rides without its owning face; one current personal Handle per PersonaGroup; a collective quorum Handle stays separate.
* ''Every hand opens a session'' (operator ruling; <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/session-contract#/the-hands`>>): a tab, a CLI run, the disk watcher and a spirit's MCP seat differ by capability profile and never by key; MCP and the CLI stand as two doors onto one contract. The slice owes the watcher as a leasing session, the seat under a standing grant, the Mask as a record field, and door parity at the session. A tasked spirit comes down to an AI, a Mask and the vessel's seat, attended or standing; the vessel's own key serves as its system identity and the house mints no other.
* The compose hunk in `open-node-vessel.ts` belongs to this slice's owner; cabal runtime work stops at its module boundary.

!!! 6 · TWO HEARTHS ~ the fresh start, then the ceremony (waits on everything above)
* The fresh start by hand (#/fresh-start) on both machines, on the re-found build.
* The ceremony: both found their seeds through the Stable act and stand; both found the Nexus fresh through the Nexus founding act; each signs a contract the two seats countersign; both join one shared cabal realm beside others; each wears a face; an edit crosses both ways and reads back after a restart on each machine. Probes use synthetic names; no fixture forges a cast persona's key or Handle.

!!! The sealed store ~ five steps threaded through the paths (operator-approved model; step one's format stands as law and in code; each later step lands in its place before the fence, under #/the-attractor's ruling)
''Step three's launch kit'': <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/docs/train-s3-launch`>>.
* ''The model'': cleartext lives in the live wiki and in the operator's projected files alone. A private bag rests as authenticated ciphertext chunks, the same bytes at rest and in a blind carrier's hands; a node that holds the capability and stands locked rests sealed. Private bytes take randomized authenticated encryption addressed over the ciphertext, so convergent sealing leaves the private tier. A bag's content key rolls at a causal removal act, never by a clock.
* ''Scope'' (operator ruling): the sealing covers the wiki content stores, node and browser, and the content-addressed store, in carriage as well as at rest. The memory palace, the sensorium, receipts and logs stand outside it. Public and crossroads content stays readable to whoever holds the public keys or lookups.
* ''Faces stand as facets of one operator'' (operator steer): no per-face projection root. Each kind of reader gets named — own vessel, own facet, a friend's face, a hosted guest, a carrier that holds none — before any admission-history rule gets written. One tree on disk serves the person: a projected wiki stands as a surface a session leases, and an edit from disk returns under that session's face (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/session-contract#/the-hands`>>). Whoever holds the disk reads every facet's projection and may link them.
* ''Step 1, the one private-byte format'': it stands as law at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store`>>, and that carrier names each part the code holds: the sealed value with its ciphertext address and named refusals; the key version, manifest and checkpoint records; the lockbox payload; shareable-history spans; the key lineage and the roll a seat roll feeds. The content-addressed store and the walker's carriage seal and open through it, no opener returns unauthenticated bytes, and no key derives from content or from a Nexus secret. The format changes ONCE, there. The content-key roll stands subscribed to the daemon island's seat rolls. What steps two to five take from the format reads at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store#/owed`>>.
* ''Step 2, the key's seat'': a content key's lockbox, the store that rests lockboxes, records and the chunk index, and the content-key holder stand as law at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store#/the-store`>>, and that carrier names each part the code holds. A node vessel's daemon island seats the holder beside its Keyhive identities and subscribes the content-key roll to its own seat rolls; a removal's successor wraps for the readers that remain, each lockbox into a Keyhive document of its own, so a removed device opens nothing a vessel seals after it counts the raise. Owed from this step, each to its owner: the session open that hands the island a signer and the chain check, without which no content key mints on a production vessel; a door from the island to the vessel's fold for the raises it counts; the face join's call for a from-now admission or a hand-over; and the browser shore's rest and Archive writer. A raise over the vessel that founded a face's key group names no seat a roll can leave out, so that vessel keeps its keys until `K12` stands a fresh group. What steps three and four take from this step reads at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store#/owed`>>.
* ''Step 3, after ONE FACE'': sealed storage values and hydration scoped by the session's face; every fetch, write, catalog lookup and content-addressed read gets checked against that face, since a key alone cannot stop a trusted daemon handing one face another's document.
* ''Step 4, after the KAHU PLANE's seats and wire'': sealed history published to and fetched from a blind carrier by the house's own carriage protocol, with a causal index. A carrier never joins document sync.
* ''Step 5, after ONE FACE'': a public bag served at the floor of a locked node; today the floor serves the oracle snapshot alone.
* ''History at admission'' (operator ruling): sharing a bag's past with a newcomer must have stood declared when those edits got written. An inviter chooses between history the realm already marked shareable and a present-state admission, and never widens the audience for another writer's past. The design target keeps one logical bag name whose key rolls mark epochs: a removal rolls, a from-now admission rolls, and a with-its-past admission hands over the earlier epochs' keys.
* ''Mutual removal'' (operator ruling; <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae`>>): both signed acts and both key branches stay, every member sees the conflict, and it ends in an explicit fork unless the realm's charter named a recovery council.

!!! The shore ~ the browser's five rungs, beside the paths
* ''The rungs'': 0, an admitted crossing; 1, a carrier the node holds arrives and renders; 2, a typed field edit reaches the node and survives its restart, read from a fresh browser profile; 3, one human, many faces; 4, two hearths. Rungs 0–2 give the one-machine witness the working-Lararium ruling asks for, and the node's own read decides each rung, never a screenshot or a connected socket.
* ''What blocks rungs 0–2'': rungs 0 and 1 pass live (`tools/browser-ci.sh`, the crossing and render walks); rung 2's leaf half saves and survives a reload of the same profile. The working layer (a realm workbench, #/rulings 2026-11-11) and the durability receipt on the authenticated session stand built (`F5`; the workbench lane), so the node half now waits on a walk that reads it: the hearth's stage walk (`L4`) reads rung 2 whole, page to disk and back, on a node that serves its own page (`L1`) to a page that dials its own origin (`L2`).
* Device capabilities get measured by `tools/capability-probe/` on real devices, never assumed.
* ''What blocks rung 3 onward'': rung 3 waits on ONE FACE. Rung 4 waits on HARBOR's household HTTPS, on the KAHU PLANE's grant for a foreign leaf, and on browser custody: a browser leaf holds a passphrase or paper slot before it holds a persona root (#/rulings).
* The walks ride `tools/browser-ci.sh`, and build under `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/dreamdom#/the-ruling`: a frame carries absolute state, and a worker defines no global `window`.

!!! The test re-cut ~ train T beside the paths (items in #/stride-t-recut; kit <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/test-recut-road`>>)
* ''What it builds'': the house's code made testable at the grain its claims live at, before the founding (#/rulings, 2026-10-31): a shared scratch fixture, events where tests poll, pure readers lifted out of the boot composer, instance-owned doors, the composer cut along capability lines into stages both vessel kinds share, and a mutation runner that scores what a test proves.
* ''Four waves'': wave 0 holds the security, custody and correctness cures the cut reads found, each red-first, and a red that stays green retires its item; wave A runs parallel and touches no composer; wave B pins the boot's order and its events; wave C cuts the seams, and `T12`, the composer's stage-cut, runs as the one named single-owner step after `T6`'s characterization pins.
* ''It never stalls a path'': a T item reaches the compose hunks of `open-node-vessel.ts` and `open-browser-vessel.ts` only through a module boundary or through `T12`'s window. The kit holds the measured ground, the god-method's lift map, the floor's talk-story, the reads behind the items and the laws each item keeps; this carrier holds the rulings and the items.

!!! Off this road, by ruling
* ''Cloud carriers'': capability and carrier law, private sealed carriage, per-store lifecycle, causal wake and its measurement. Deferred until the operator can deploy; the reading of "delegated keys" (a narrow carriage grant, or a backup that can act) stays open with them.
* ''The Sensorium'': waits behind the working-Lararium milestone. The memory stage (`L8` to `L13`) runs on plain tiddlers with no embedding and no sidecar, and lifts nothing of this wait (#/rulings, 2026-10-31). Its return waits on `G48` (a rewound turn stays recallable) and retires `G47`'s three placeholders.
* ''Streams'' (`saqimtiaz/streams`, BSD-3-Clause): a deferred crossroads offering; it never rides the genesis boot set.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-loops`>>

!! The loops ~ the strides that hold every open item

No item rests here: each sits in the stride below that holds it, and a citation names that stride. Each item names what stays owed, the train of #/the-trains that closes it, and who owes a ruling first
(''operator'', ''house'', or nobody). An item names its file so a builder reads the code before leaning on
the item; a line number drifts, so none rests here. Train letters: ''L'' the memory-stage hearth ·
''T'' the test re-cut · ''S'' the custody seal · ''G'' ground ·
''K'' the seat · ''N'' the Nexus founding act · ''P'' the Kahu plane · ''W'' the wire and the face proof ·
''E'' the ember · ''O1'' ONE FACE's sessions · ''H'' the hosted road ·
''A'' HARBOR's tail · ''V'' recovery's verbs · ''B'' the browser and the phones · ''X'' TWO HEARTHS · ''C'' the contract leaps of 2026-11-14 (#/the-leaps) · ''R'' block and inline sigils at parsing parity (#/o-takiage; its R-prefixed namesakes, R-CHARTER and its kin, name the re-found's stages).
DreamDOM's train ''D'' keeps its items in its own roadmap (#/side-lanes).

''The strides serve as the plan the sprint overran'' (operator, 2026-11-13: //"The 'Strides' served as the current milestones ahead, before we lightning-sprinted in the scratch directly (moved into the repo as scratch/ history)."//). The strides name the milestones as planned; the sprint's planning records rest in the repository's `scratch/` directory (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history`>>), which informs and never governs. A stride item reads as owed only where the tree still lacks what it names; the tree and git decide, and an item the tree has outrun leaves its stride at the next true-up.

* ''#/stride-0-hearth'' — the memory-stage hearth: the joined walk, the stage, the seat.
* ''#/stride-t-recut'' — the test re-cut: wave 0's cures, then waves A to C.
* ''#/stride-1-spine'' — face and custody; sealed identity and private authority.
* ''#/stride-2-floor'' — floor, wire and realm release.
* ''#/stride-3-bake'' — one re-bake, rename and founding format.
* ''#/after-the-seat'' — remaining work and witnesses.
* ''#/operator-gates'' — operator rulings and hands.
* ''#/o-takiage'' — complete items moved by the rite.
* ''#/ashes'' — finished items in one line each, until the operator burns them.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/stride-0-hearth`>>

!! Stride 0 Hearth

The memory-stage hearth (#/the-attractor; #/rulings, 2026-10-31): the joined walk first, then the stage on it, then the move. Each item runs red-first with its control; an item's harness half walks the test door, and the operator's hand performs each founding and custody act (#/cold-start).

''Reading note'': the tree and git decide what stands (#/the-loops). Each item names the shape the tree holds today and what stays owed on it; landed work reads in git and #/ashes.

!!! Seven-league stride notes

* ''The stage's shape''. The stage rests in the face's own daemon wiki and climbs by two crossings: the working top, where a stage meme stands under `lar:///ha.ka.ba/kahua/memory/<class>/<slug>`, its path carried by title and `uri-path`; the face's local canon `bags/daemon-<F>`, unsigned, by `lares kahua stage`; and the signed stage `bags/kahua-<F>`, by the hand's lift (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/kahua-memory-stage#/as-built`>>). Stage memes stay out of git (#/rulings, the memory stage's meme path). The stage scout's lean (two rounds, 2026-10-31) stays a lean the controller rules through `L8`: a `stage/*` tag family beside `lifecycle/*`, so an unpoured meme never enters the governed count and a meme poured as a carrier takes `lifecycle/*` at the pour; one index view; survivors reaching `bags/` carriers by promotion; consolidation through a sitting, and through a talk-story floor where a stage meme contradicts held canon; no stage meme reads a clock.
* ''Where the operator's hearth stands'': the house as a container puts all code in the image (#/rulings), and `tools/lares-container.sh` stands it on the one house root `LARES_HOUSE` names, bound at `/lar`.
* ''The operator's hand on the hearth'', each when its item stands: move the old homes aside and found fresh by hand (#/fresh-start; #/rulings, 2026-10-31, a single operator's re-found); unlock; bind the browser's passphrase slot; carry the fleet-join offer, request, answer and land; run `lares home tend` from the main clone. None of these gates a lane.

!!! The items

* `F11` The hands: the watcher as a leasing session, the seat under a standing grant, the Mask as a record field, door parity at the session (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/session-contract#/the-hands`>>). An agent's dissent drafts and a person's direct ask edits (#/rulings, 2026-10-16). O1. The four profiles stand in the session contract (`HAND_PROFILES`, `session-contract.ts`), and a session seated `seat` refuses the hand-only verbs at the socket by name: the kahua operator verbs `beyond-profile`, the promote and kāpae verbs `promote-needs-hand` and `kapae-needs-hand` (`uds-channel.ts`, `store-door-verbs.ts`). That refusal reads advisory, since a client names its own hand. Owed, before a second human, a remote seat or a non-operator process writes the hearth (#/the-leaps, the items with wake conditions): the signed local channel that binds the hand; the profile-verb table over the vault, admit and face-switch verbs, where `session:call` and `session:act` check the face and the anchor alone; the Mask carried on the seat's `meme_put`; the watcher's lease and door parity behind them.
* `L4p` The stage walk's proof (walk gap G3; `L4` rests in #/ashes). Owed: the walk itself on `tools/browser-weld/`, red then control. A joined browser edits a tiddler in the daemon wiki, the file lands under `<projections>/wikis/daemon-<F>`, `lares meme get --recipe default` returns it, a CLI `meme put` shows in the page, and the MCP seat reads the page's edit; weakening control: with the relay killed, no file lands. It reads rung 2 whole (#/the-road, the shore). Waits on a stood hearth and on `L4f`. L; B.
* `L4f` `L4`'s fork (a) (operator ruling, 2026-11-04; ruled, unbuilt). The admit carriage widens so the operator's own-hearth page mounts the hearth daemon document, where today an own-hearth crossing seats the hearth's canon documents alone (`hearth-canon.ts`, `seatHearthCanon`). Red: a joined own-hearth page mounts the daemon wiki through the widened carriage and edits land under `<projections>/wikis/daemon-<F>`; control: a guest page's admit carries no daemon document. Sighted twice: the fourth rehearsal's page write as face 1 reached no hearth file in eight seconds, and on the live walk of 2026-11-14 a tiddler saved in a joined browser's daemon wiki never reached the node. Rides `L4p`. The slice's design rests in <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/kahua-memory-stage#/the-hearth-lane`>>. Stands in code, walked by no live page: the name rides the per-device `device-edge` act as `hearthDaemon`, root-signed at each seat from the hearth's own control record (never `hearthDaemonUrl`, which names the door document); the hearth's vessel-only verdict yields that one working slot to a session that proved its face, and the device table answers the current binding alone (`workingOf`), so a moved slot shares nowhere. Owed: `L4p`'s walk on a fresh `fleet join answer`; a joined device's earlier working slot stays orphaned until a lift. L; B.
* `L8` The memory stage's law. <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/kahua-memory-stage`>> holds the stage as built: it lives in the `daemon` wiki itself (operator ruling, 2026-10-31, #/rulings), its working layer projects to `<projections>/wikis/daemon-<F>`, the face's daemon canon to `<projections>/bags/daemon-<F>` and the face's kahua stage to `<projections>/bags/kahua-<F>` (`bag-paths.ts`), and no other wiki domain hosts it, since each serves its own intent. Owed in that carrier, as law before `L10` to `L13` build on it: the tiers as tags inside that one wiki (the `stage/*` family beside `lifecycle/*`), how a survivor promotes into a bag carrier, the consolidation rite, the cold-boot index, and the rhymes with the field (complementary learning systems, hippocampal indexing, reconsolidation, the agent-memory systems), each with its source and where it breaks. L.
* `L33` ''The stage-meme gate's residues'' (`L9`'s blind read's P3s). The gate compares raw strings, so `Memory/`, a `%2F` spelling or a title with no trailing slash skips the missing-key check while the stage's readers filter on the exact prefix; a keyed stage meme's `/path` children can sit at another key's derived title; a noop or conflict verdict, and a write that bypasses `placeMeme`, skip the gate (the read-side stage law, `L11`, covers them). `tools/dogfood-proof.sh` unsets `LAR_BAGS` and `LAR_GENESIS` beside its exports, so an operator shell that exports `LAR_BAGS=<checkout>/bags` never writes back into the tracked tree; corepack's `pnpm` under the proof's fresh `HOME` may fetch. L.
* `L34` ''The kāpae on disk's residues'' (lane `kapae-disk`; the seat's decisions, overrulable). The disk watcher sends a `.kapae` shadow the way it sends a carrier, both ends treating a repeat as a no-op; a tombstone raised while the projector stood stopped writes no shadow at boot (a gap older than the lane); a title ingest-back cannot address (a foreign title, a `FileSystemPaths` rule) gets no usable shadow, a named limit. Accepted: a rename re-link retracts its source to absent (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae#/core-cut`>>), so a lower copy surfaces. L.
* `L10` The lifecycle tools read the stage: `meme check` counts no `stage/*` meme among governed carriers, and `lares meme sitting` names closed stage memes with a landed witness as burn candidates and burns none (`carrier-lifecycle.ts`). Red: a `stage/working` meme with no witness passes `meme check`, and a closed meme with a witness appears in the sitting; control: a `lifecycle/designed` carrier still meets its gate. L.
* `L11` The cold-boot index: one filter view on the daemon wiki lists the open stage memes, each beside the commit or wave that last re-checked it and an `unchecked` mark where none did, and the boot set stays one fixed small read whatever the history holds. Red: a stage meme whose named file moved since its last check reads `unchecked`; control: a re-checked meme reads its stamp. Owed with it (#/rulings, the memory stage's meme path, (a) and (b)): the index and every stage reader read working and canon, never drafts, and a stage meme whose title disagrees with its key reads as a named fork, the backstop writing a diagnostic tiddler in the working bag; the readers read a stage meme through `readParallelDrafts`, so concurrent same-key edits surface both sides before multi-device dogfooding. L.
* `L12` The recurrence loop: an incident record carries `recurs/N`, counted by repeat incidents, never a clock; at two a sitting proposes a procedural change (a feedback line or a guard test, red-first), the operator rules it, and a lesson stays `unverified` until a later incident shows it held, a lesson that never fires reading as a burn candidate. Red: a second incident of one tell raises the proposal; control: one incident raises none. L.
* `L13` The move, the rung's acceptance: the controller's plan, its owed list, the scout reports, the lane briefs and the resume sheets move onto the stage, and a cold session reads the stage index, this carrier and the controller handoff (#/cold-start). Until then the sprint's planning records rest in the repository's `scratch/` directory as history (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history`>>), live plans rest in canon (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/controller-handoff#/state`>>), and the stage holds none of them. The harness's own auto-memory keeps its seat, since its host loads it through its own door; the reality tunnel's memory shore may later point it at the stage (#/side-lanes). Its witness: one cold session resumes the controller's work from the stage alone. L.
* `L14` The promotion verb, landing last: `meme promote` holds no in-wiki path, its admin check answers `false` (`meme.ts`), and the hand-edit weld stays unbuilt (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/wiki-layer-ontology`>>). Red: a person holding admin promotes a working-layer meme into a bag as a residency move, its `lar:` URI unchanged; control: a seat's promotion refuses. Until it lands, a stage meme reaches `bags/` as a carrier poured by pull request. L.
* `L15` A live house beside the land train. A house's projection lands under `<LAR_ROOT>/projections`, outside the checkout; the tracked tree takes a house's writes only where its canon source resolves to the checkout's `bags/` (`LAR_BAGS=<checkout>/bags`, source-control write-back by design), and `tools/tree-clean-witness.sh` reads any write a test run adds to the tracked tree as red. Owed for the write-back case, where a dirty tracked file blocks the land train's fast-forward on that path: the house's canon edits commit as their own record commits (or the train reads and carries them) before a land, and the witness reads the house surface apart from a test boot's writes. Red: a standing house edits a canon tiddler, a train lands a commit touching another file, and the land succeeds with the house edit kept. L.
* `L17` The whole TiddlyWiki surface on every door, as phases (operator, 2026-10-31: //"The MCP server should also have full TW5 caps on any wiki loaded."//; a priority lane since 2026-11-14; the lean approved: //"Pono!!! I approve all!"//; shape, reds and files at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/wiki-surface-scout#/phases`>>, the door as it stands at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/wiki-surface-scout#/what-stands`>>). Base operations stop wearing `meme_*`: "meme" names a family of tiddlers (a root and its slot children), the meme door keeps the family verbs, and every write-bearing action reads meme-aware. Phase 1 stands: one family law with the orphan folder and its flag, and the tiddler door on the anchor, worn by the CLI and the MCP seat, its delete joined to the family law and naming its meaning by `mode` (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/recipe-layer-model#/delete-reveals-or-shadows`>>). ''Owed in phase 1's wake'': `meme delete` on the anchor lands one change per member and takes no mode (`L37`); a slot's delete leaves its root's `kahea` call dangling (the consequence names it; no door rewrites the root); caps against the family live in the pure function alone; a COPY of a root copies one record; a rename lands as ordered writes, never one change; the wiki's indexes count a hidden record, so a `count[]` over an indexed lookup can tell one exists; a `$:/` write refuses `tiddler-put-code-class` until the act ward's `codeClassOf` stands.
** `L17-2a` (wave two) The relay to named wikis: a width-`none` port under a lease minted from the session's face. Reds: `wiki-filter-named-wiki-engine`, `relay-lease-from-session-face`, `relay-second-face-refuses`. Files: `open-node-vessel.ts`, `vessel-island-pool-core.ts`, `port-lease.ts`; it uses the port's standing kinds.
** `L17-2b` (wave two) The `act` and `catalog` asks, the recorder in a throwaway copy, preview by default and apply by digest, bulk by filter, the empty `tm-*` allowlist; the switcher's rename to `wiki_switch|hold|release|active` rides with `wiki_act`. Reds: `wiki-act-preview-then-apply`, `preview-bound-to-cursor`, `wiki-act-custody-refuses`, `wiki-act-tm-allowlist-empty`, `bulk-act-half-family-refuses`, `cap-family-split-names-layers`. Files: `record-port.ts` (`2b` alone edits it), `record-port-handler.ts`, a new `act-recorder.ts`, `codeClassOf` in mesh.
** `L17-2c` (wave two, after `2b`) The meme door's remainder on `familyOf`: `meme put|get|delete` compose a port write, and a root's rename carries its family as one change. Red: `rename-root-carries-family`. Files: `place-meme.ts`, `meme-verbs.ts`.
** `L17-2d` (wave two) The road's items as slot children carrying fields (`status`, `priority`, `depends-on`, `lane`), so a filter reprioritizes the road; a content lane on this carrier alone, after a ruling on the road's slot law.
** `L17-3` (after wave two) The console verbs (`console-verbs.ts`, `console-door.ts`), the filter and action references as MCP resources, a change feed by filter and `wiki_since`, a cross-wiki search naming each hit's wiki, the AI's own draft slot, new-from-template; DreamDOM's `D19` carries the surface half.
** `L17-4` (later) Signed provenance telling an operator's verbatim ruling from a session's paraphrase (after `F11`'s signed local channel), a family-aware rename with relink across wikis, an L-Prime lint operator.
* `L18` The narration fence: self-narration, design notes, talk-story floors and their moʻolelo and hoike, scout reports, lane briefs, resume sheets, and the controller's plan and owed items land in the daemon wiki through `meme_put`, never as files on disk. The fence rides three hands: the lead law and `mixed-model-swarm` carry the rule; a harness hook refuses a write to the controller's scratch notes and to the flat memory store beyond its pointer file, naming `meme_put` (installed through the operator's own config act); a witness counts stray narration files. Red: an agent's write to the fenced path refuses and names the wiki verb; control: a lane's code edit in its worktree passes. Lands after `L13`. L.
* `L19` The memory o-takiage: the flat memory store moves into the daemon wiki, each file a tiddler keyed by its slug (`stage/feedback`, `stage/semantic`, `stage/reference`, the `[[name]]` links kept as wiki links), checked against the repo as it moves (a memory that names a file, function or flag gets re-verified, and a stale one burns rather than moves); each moved file burns from the flat store; the index file shrinks to a pointer whose first line names the MCP capabilities and the cold-boot filter. Red: a cold session reads a moved memory through `meme_get` and the filter view, with the flat file gone; control: the pointer's first line answers before any wiki stands. Lands after `L18`, and in this shape (talk-story, 2026-11-04): the first copy lands in the kahua working top, titles `lar:///ha.ka.ba/kahua/memory/<class>/<slug>` (the slug holds the filename stem, hyphenated; fields `name`, `description`, `type`, `source-file`, `source-sha256`); dangling links report and stay uninvented; the flat files stay source of truth until each receipt stands; a stale memory burns instead of moving; the pointer file shrinks last. It needs `L4p`'s stage walk and `L17`'s filter verbs first, and `L21` lifts the verified batches by hand. The stage meme law already binds every writer (`stageMemeRefusal`, `place-meme.ts`), and the burn it rests on holds on disk (#/rulings, the memory stage's meme path, (c); `kapae-disk` in #/ashes): the projector writes a burned meme's kāpae as `<stem>.kapae` and ingest raises it again. Owed beside the move: a burn discards or shadows the open drafts and upper-layer copies of its meme, so no synced draft brings a burned memory back. L. Its cold-boot reads ride the tiddler door: `wiki_filter` for the index, `tiddler_get` for one meme.
* `L21` The batch lift (`L19`'s owed half, 2026-11-04). `L19` lands its first copy in the kahua working top, never the daemon wiki, and verifies there (a `meme_get` round-trip against `source-sha256`, a receipt tiddler per file, the filter view, a cold-session sample); the operator's hand then lifts the verified tiddlers in BATCHES, one fresh ward unlock per batch (#/rulings, 2026-11-05), each item still signing its own lift act, all items preflighted before any lands, the epoch pinned once, and a drifted digest refusing by name. Red: a batch of N working-top tiddlers lifts as N signed lifts under one warded hand act, and a tiddler whose digest drifted from its receipt refuses by name; control: a single lift stays as before. Waits on `L4p`, `L17` and the kahua stage. L.
* `L35` ''The memory stage goes primary'' (operator, 2026-11-14). Once the hearth answers, the wiki replaces the flat markdown memory: a face reads its own daemon wiki and every topic wiki shared to it, so the house's shared memory lives in a shared topic wiki and a face's own memory in its daemon wiki; a verbatim search of past chat stays the deferred sensorium's. It builds on `L8`'s law, `L17`'s verbs and `L19`'s move with `L21`'s lift, and closes with `L13`: a rule in the harness's `CLAUDE.md` naming the wiki as the memory, and the burn of the flat files once each receipt stands; the wake's memory index and the compaction hook's carried addresses stand beneath it (`L43`, `L44`). The rule lands through the operator's own config act, as `L18`'s hook does. It carries the AI's memory frame (a memory's structure and provenance as fields, never bare data) and the kahua two-tier lift. Reds: a cold session with the flat store gone reads its memory index at wake and a shared house memory from the topic wiki shared to its face; after a compaction the session reads a meme again by the address the hook carried. Control: a face no share names reads nothing of that topic wiki. Waits on the first use (#/the-order, rank 5). L.
* `L22` The swarm's controls and the ward (operator rulings, 2026-11-04 and 2026-11-05; the slices, their state and the design of `S3` to `S6` rest in <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/seat-harness#/the-swarm-slices`>>). Item 7 of the seat-harness lane enacts the wiki-daemon key cap at standing: the house cap, the lane shares, the push lei, the lane state, the roster and the receipts rest as signed records, the ward binds the daemon and kahua wikis' code-bearing acts and the kahua lift, and the swarm refuses by name before a wiki stands. `S1` to `S3` with `S2b`, `S3-pre-b` and `S3b` stand (#/ashes, the `row7` lines). Owed: `S4` to `S6`, after the standing, red-first per slice, each with its control, homed in the suites the carrier names. The reads' residues ride `L24` to `L27`. L.
* `L24` ''The stuck joinee'' (`S3a`'s cure R1; the house lean the operator approved, 2026-11-05). A seated admission whose device edge lapsed across a lease roll refuses `replay-edge-lapsed`. The renewal writer stands: `packages/lararium-node/src/commands/device-admit.ts` appends one `device-edge` act for the seated admission at the current epoch when the seated digest changes, signed by the face root, and the persona fold takes the later act's digest. Owed: re-measure, never rebuild: which acts roll the delegation lease epoch, whether every joined device strands at such a roll and whether the renewal covers every seated device, read over every lease-advancing act until `M6`'s lease burn retires the roll. Red: a joinee seated before a lease roll rejoins and stands on the operator's answer; control: a replay with no operator answer still refuses. L.
* `L29` ''The teardown drain'' (Terra read, 2026-11-06). The island submits every owed write, then settles in one round on the vessel's close: the vessel answers each receipt whenever asked, the island sends `receipt:asks-done` once every owed write has asked, the vessel answers and sends `receipt:close`; no island-side clock; a title still owed reaches the vessel by name as a `teardown-fault`, never a silent ack. Every vessel class tears its daemon island down before its floor (the herm's order cured; the browser's island takes the graceful shutdown, its hard kill the liveness for a worker wedged alive). Owed: the two ask doors that still end on any fault (fail-safe; narrowing after a measure), a dedicated browser island's owed writes at `pagehide`, a full herm boot teardown proof. L.
* `L30` ''The swarm moves into the Lararium'' (operator, 2026-11-07, #/rulings). After standing and the vault: the multi-model swarm harness now in `tools/` (the broker `commit-relay.mjs` and `seat-broker.mjs`, the walls `seat-walls.mjs`, the launcher `launch-codex.mjs`, the lane state, the push grant, the gate, the land train) moves into the capability stack, each part to the home its nature names: product code, plugin code running inside the VM, or a shore connector at a causal-island boundary; the daemon then drives the swarm from inside the Lararium. Builds on `S4` to `S6` (signed roster, receipts and controls in the daemon wiki) and the vault (`L22`, #/the-order rank 31). Owed after standing.
* `L28` ''The renewal at the roll stands unwired'' (`L24`'s roll half; the unwired-code scout, 2026-11-06). `rollEnrolments` (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/persona-group-secret.ts`) seals one enrolment per remaining device at a rotation, and only tests call it; the renewal at the roll, which moves each seated device's pin to a renewed edge the root signed (the lease guard, <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/seat-harness#/the-swarm-slices`>>), holds no production caller. Owed: a zero-trust critical review decides retire / wire / reshape, then the work (#/rulings, 2026-11-06, the unwired code); the Terra review's verdict sets its next step. Before the rite. L.
* `L32` ''The lease end state's remaining slices'' (merged into the epoch burn, #/the-order rank 18; `M6`; lane `lease-end`, whose slices 1 and 2 stand; after the dogfood stand, operator 2026-11-07). Slices 3 and 4: slice 2's owed items (KEL freshness, the tri-state ring, the founder join's check-then-use window, the post-commit set-aside, the census that refuses an incomplete view, `runApplyAdmitPayload`, `holder-session-proof`, `leaf-peer-proof`), then the roll guard and the epoch burn (#/rulings, the face lease's end state, reshaped). Slice 2's blind read owes its P3s: the commit boundary's wording (a microtask queued ahead of admit still runs before the wasm put) with an optional real-Keyhive residual test; the window between the ring's generation and the island replica (fold the generation into admit, or name it at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae#/four-edges`>>); `proofVersion` bumps on every daemon-bag title (bump on proof-carrying titles alone, or compare board heads); the node wake test crosses no sync port and the browser test passes no `dyadLedger`; the epoch-free tamper control's reason pattern reads too broad; an aside grant re-verifies its KEL each pass (cache lineage per `record.sig`). Outside that diff, `open-browser-vessel.ts`'s `stands === true -> aside` reads inverted on its face; the every-reader sweep checks it. L.
* `L25` ''Vessel tags across `rotatePlace`'' (2026-11-05). The tag secret rests random per face (device-minted, a `D` record, `bundle: "stays"`, sealed under the face's FCK on the node and under the face's own tree on the browser, `browser-custody.ts`), never derived from the place seed (row7-s3a), so a place rotation leaves every face's tag standing, and no re-admission rides a rotation. Owed: the ward's threat model names "the root holder can mint its own second device" as out of scope; and a browser tab that clears its origin storage mints a fresh tag for a face it already joined, which reads as a new vessel, while the fold's view of that case stays owed. Before the rite. L.
* `L26` ''`S3`'s read residues'' (the co-sign reads, 2026-11-05). Swept on `row7-s3b` (alpha line, no back-compat): a browser face's sealed ward-key record from before the key moved under its own ward (`sealed/ward-sign/<group>`) stood on disk, read by nothing; the face's next tag ask now removes it. Owed, each red-first with its control: no act frees a tag, so a vessel that lost its atom never rejoins that face; the `signedInput` fallback after a lease roll; a seated admission with no linked edge still mints anew, while the fold refuses the duplicate tag, so no second seat stands; every face already on disk folds to zero devices, which fits no back-compat and re-genesis freely, and the `S3` land names it. The tag-secret reading against the 2026-10-19 ruling, the HKDF-under-a-TPM bar and the founding resume across a rotation closed by the per-face tag secret, row7-s3a; the rest build before the rite. L.
* `L27` ''`S2`'s read residues'' (the ward reads, 2026-11-05). A planted marker locks no root out and stands in for no card read-back (the waiver stands as an explicit, process-local harness seat in the process that reads the card); the harness refuses route seating when `found --storage` points elsewhere; a face route parked through the custody frame becomes a one-use, unguessable reference bound to a digest of its operation's identifying arguments; the ward digest folds a conflicting handle index; the harbor walk founds through its own seated route. Owed: re-measure the ward refusal that named no reason against `awaitOnRolled`, which carries no budget; a witness for Kahu-gated canon-bag projection (the harbor leg moved to crossroads). L.
* `L20` The fiction and art domains' o-takiage (#/rulings, 2026-11-01). Once the lararium stands, the RPG chapters and the concept art take wiki domains of their own under `edition/*` and `art/*` tag families, each with an o-takiage rite fitted to it and designed from the pono sweep; the domain sweep of 2026-11-01 found no RPG or art wiki yet, and lifecycle tags already read opt-in. The lares design laws never bind these families by default. Red: a carrier tagged `edition/*` passes `meme check` under its own family's law and meets no `lifecycle/*` gate it never opted into; control: a `lifecycle/designed` carrier in `bags/lares` still meets its gate. Waits on `L13`. L.
* `L31` ''The drop floor's named limits'' (`L23`, #/ashes; <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/seat-harness#/the-ward`>>). A writer that holds both custody and the board's local storage on a face with no sibling device can roll both back, so a single-device rollback still replays (a sibling's copy heals it at sync); a device that rotates its atom voids its own bound records until the hand commits them again (it fails closed); records signed before the floor carry no owner and void until recommitted (alpha, no back-compat); the re-read's text-edit and overwrite-then-delete cases read green only, with no red on base. The fork's other branch, a ward-signed ledger head, still falls to a whole-ledger rollback and makes a brake wait on the ward, so it stays unbuilt. L.

