<<~ ahu slot=`#/stride-t-recut`>>

!! Stride T Recut

The test re-cut (#/rulings, 2026-10-31; #/the-road, train T; kit <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/test-recut-road`>>). The train rides after the standing (#/the-order, ranks 33 and 37), with `T21` to `T25` before `X0` (#/rulings, 2026-11-04); inside it wave 0 leads, waves A and B follow as slots free, and wave C waits on its gates. No lane opens on a cut item until its cold read and its collision read stand (#/rulings, 2026-10-31). A complete item moves to #/o-takiage at its land.

''Reading note'': the tree and git decide what stands (#/the-loops). Each item names the shape the tree holds today and what stays owed on it; landed work reads in git and #/ashes.

!!! Wave 0 ~ the cures the cut reads found, ahead of any cut

Each item here stands confirmed by two readers from source; each item's first commit runs its red, and a red that stays green retires the item or its clause.

* `T21` ''Cabal invites run more than once'' (security). `cabalInviteId` names a seen-cache that no code builds; only the epoch lease expires an invite, so a removed joiner re-presents it until the epoch advances. The law stands: a removed joiner never re-enters on an invite minted before the removal; the red decides the mechanism (#/the-floor). Red: remove a joiner, re-present the invite inside the same epoch, assert refusal.
* `T23` ''An admitted browser leaf bricks on reload'' (correctness, fails closed). The admit bootstrap keeps the hearth gate key but drops the island kind and scope; later boots pass a null anchor, the island falls to the leaf's own key, and the Binding Gate throws "chain absent". A same-tab reboot hides it; a reload exposes it; it bites when the gate key stands. Node cures the same case by re-reading its pin each boot. Red: admit, then boot again with no admit, assert the same island key; controls: a null-gate admit, a founded leaf. The cure reads no old bootstrap shape.
* `T24` ''The browser catalog orphans wikis'' (correctness). After a data-amnesia re-found the browser never rewrites its catalog pointer, so every later boot re-founds a blank catalog and orphans the wikis registered since; the first boot also writes the pointer before a store flush. Red: boot twice on a missing catalog document, assert the second boot stays quiet; cure includes a flush before the boot keys write.
* `T25` ''A failed wiki-switch flips the browser surface''. The browser handler tests the result object, always truthy, where node reads `.ok`. `T13`'s shared switcher cures it; the red lands first.
* `T26` ''A herm registers wiki verbs it cannot run''. They throw a TypeError on use (the CLI shore reaches `wiki-act`); the wiki verbs register behind the wiki capability.
* `T28` ''Wax-stamp verification owes its test''. `verifyWaxStampSig` and `mintWaxStamp` stand with no caller and fail closed; the test rides `W30`.
* `T29` ''Unborn seats state their law''. `peerContractNymMap` (the realm consult's root map, `membership-doctrine#/two-maps`) and `walkerBoard` (the reader half of the owed E walker arm) read as unborn seats, never dead code: their comments restate as law and name their owed items. A seat with no road item and no canon behind it burns.
* `T37` ''The keyslot's key hygiene'' (custody, security read). The KDF half stands (lane `kdf-native`): the passphrase KDF rides a minted port at one cost (native scrypt on the host, noble in the browser, identical bytes); a pin derives a passphrase only when it tries it and stops at the first share, so a right first guess pays one derivation where it paid three; each distinct passphrase counts as one attempt, charged before the open and refunded only on an open or a reading that ran no derivation; a face founding derives before its index entry lands; a KDF refusal zeroes the keys the unlock already opened. Owed, each red-first with its control: the keyslot never zeroes its scrypt material, KEK, slot key or shares (`keyslot.ts`); `doUnlock` leaks the tree key and secret key, and `doUnlockFace` the face key, when the root carrier's open throws, and the CLI's proof leaves its tree key unzeroed; a route keys on encoded bytes, or refuses a passphrase `isWellFormed` rejects, since lone surrogates collapse today; the door types `kdf-refused` as its refusal reason, where it reads `act-failed`; the window where a face's index entry stands before its key stores stays narrowed, never closed. The lazy open's timing reads as a MEDIUM oracle the day any remote unlock or proof door stands. S.

!!! Wave A ~ parallel, none touches the composer

* `T2` The shared scratch fixture: its codemod. The fixture (`tests/support/scratch-fixture.ts`) sets and restores `HOME`, `LAR_ROOT` and `GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL` and refuses a leak by name; it serves the `lararium-node` suites that import it and no other package. Owed: the codemod by package. Red-first per package: a test that leaks its env reads red; the fixture twin stays green.
* `T4` The dead-export sweep, regenerated by a whole-tree token index: 67 names occur once in the tree. 35 burn; 12 carry a value another file re-spells inline, and those get imported where they belong rather than deleted (the node carrier-name regexes `vault-carriers.ts` re-spells); 14 wait on rulings; 6 stand (`serveHostingMint` for train H, the two `SIBLING_*_KIND` constants, the two `session-contract` compile-time witnesses, `TW5SyncAdaptor`). About 25 of the mesh names ride `export *` or a package subpath, so the sweep changes the published surface of `@lararium/mesh`. Red-first: the typecheck witness and `tools/evergreen-boundary.mjs` must bite on a deleted name that keeps a consumer before their zero counts. Lands as one announced batch right after a controller land. The 487 barrel names wait on their own ruling.
* `T5` The pure readers out of the composer: the torn-face check (after `ralias`), `diskMirrorGrant`, the pure parts of `postureOf`, `contractHolds` and `gateNexuses` (with a case-fold row for the dedup), the three cloned `askIslandMemory` getters collapsing to one (the getter shells stay, reading the atom lazily), and the wiki-act router. The router and its twin `routedWikiSlug` (`lararium-tw5/src/verb-local-dispatch.ts`) agree except on an explicit `wiki:"daemon"` and the no-slug fallback, so they become one pure function with the daemon passthrough as a named parameter, proven by a seed table against both callers. The bootstrap parse leaves this item: `ralias` replaces it with `readBootTiddlers`. Three source-regex witnesses retarget in the same commit as a cut: `faced-boot-requires-proof.test.ts`, `kahu-canon-wiring.test.ts` (three patterns) and `tools/seed-census.mjs`. The `daemonAuth` builder lands as one pure shared builder in mesh with a sentinel-seed no-leak test (it carries a seed for every unlocked joined face), and the boot pin list unifies across node and browser (#/the-floor).
* `T36` ''The founded-custody template'' (the fixture's probe, 2026-11-01). Each run founds real custody about 600 times at about 1.2 s each, and that founding holds the node suite's wall. Owed: ONE real `foundCustody` per run per standard route (the fixture's own route, `FIXTURE_PASSPHRASE`, 388 foundings in 54 files; the published harness route, `TEST_PASSPHRASE`, 177 in 37), copied elsewhere, opt-in per file, every unlock still paying real scrypt; it serves about 309 foundings in 59 files, about 90 to 125 s of a node suite near 1200 s, while 34 files keep fresh foundings (founding, restore, rotation, keys, two vaults). Hazard: copies carry identical key bytes, so no two-vessel test takes one. Red: a templated file opens a copy under its own route and reads its own writes; control: a two-vessel test refuses the template by name.
* `T38` ''The canon-law lane''. `status-why` and the shape laws a canon-reading test enforces move into `meme check` and the commit hook, scoped by domain (a tag family, a path class, or universal), and a CI canon cell runs them on a meme-only head, which `paths-ignore` skips on a push, so only a hand raises one through `ci.yml`'s `workflow_dispatch`. Red: a staged governed carrier with no `status-why` refuses at the hook and in the cell; control: a governed carrier carrying one passes.

!!! Wave B ~ beside the road, after T2 and T3

* `T6` Boot characterization: pins the stage order, port contracts and teardown-on-refuse of both vessels' boots, test-only. Its first red records the cap build order through an injected list and asserts `systemCanon` (inside the substrate cap) fires before the daemon cap's first gate, dial or sibling channel: no test pins that order today, and reordering the cap list or adding an extra cap with no `requires` breaks nothing. `systemCanon` becomes required in the keel type, so the compiler holds the share verdict's order too. Each pin lands with a mutant that breaks the order and must go red. The compose-hunk owner updates a pin in the same commit. `T12` waits on it.
* `T7` Events for the remaining flake families: the TW5 dropdown's ready signal plus failure artifacts, the browser two-tab unlock event, the staged unlock waiting on the store's acknowledgement (that last item routes through the compose-hunk owner). Red-first per item: a late event reds the fixed wait; a never-event control fails loudly, naming the missing edge. It carries `G46`'s load flakes whole: the custody census's staged unlock, the mesh scale ratio (`sensorium-consistency.scale.test.ts`, a wall-clock ratio a count of operations replaces) and the `shore-writes` dropdown case, each red reproducing its flake by the product's own event, never by load; the six `lararium-node` suites that read the operator's global git email took a hermetic `GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL`, and custody names read no login (`T20`, #/o-takiage).
* `T8` The test-only export census, built by the compiler; the moves ride whichever lane cuts each module.
* `T30` The layer map: what each test layer proves (unit, island, vessel, mesh, held-out), what runs on every push and what runs on a batch, and where a slow witness waits on an event it means rather than a turn count, written into the kit before wave C cuts. Its inputs beside `T7`: fixed waits become events (`setImmediate` turn loops and fixed `setTimeout` ordering waits, `owned-store` first). Red-first per moved wait, as `T7` reads.
* `T31` The tw5 corpus scans share one measured budget in the vitest config, measured before it binds. Red: a scan past the budget reads red by name; control: the measured corpus passes.
* `T32` Node's slowest test files get profiled from the per-file durations CI records, and the profile names the first cuts for `T2` and `T9` to take.

!!! Wave C ~ the seams and the cut

* `T10` Boot cells, in two kinds: seven cells refuse a read before set by name (`vmManager`, `wikiActivation`, `mailbox`, `daemonVm`, `eventBus`, `bootstrap`, `pendingVerbContribution`; today `sync-wiki` and `wiki-act` throw a bare TypeError before the pool stands), and the share-verdict inputs keep a typed closed default and ride with `T11`. Waits on `T6`, `u1` and `ralias`.
* `T11` Share inputs: one cell feeds the already-pure `selfSlotShareDecision`, which moves to mesh as the peer share verdict, tested as a decision table that keeps every fail-closed branch wave one walked. Each null input withholds by name rather than allowing (`T22`). Two share verdicts stand today (the browser runs `federationShareDecision`); the lane reads both first and lands one law per relation (#/the-floor). Waits on `ralias`.
* `T12` The stage-cut: the composer splits into the cap-stack stages and the orchestration record, behind capability-named ports, landing whole with no second shape. `openDaemon` fuses four jobs (face and custody resolve, board stand and climb, dial and register, daemon open with its verb providers), and its phase order and fail-closed throws survive the split: pure deciders come out first, then stages after `T6`. The custody half never becomes a stage a surface reaches. One named single-owner road step; every other lane stops at module boundaries for its window.
* `T13` Shared cap-stack stages: one stage per PR into mesh or tw5, the browser twin burning in the same PR. Ready: dyads, the sibling channel, the climb call, the offering-lookup half of the pool wiring, the switcher verbs, the after-daemon pins once `ralias` lands. Converging on node's fuller form (#/the-floor): the fleet shadow read, face proof and plane registration, island resolution (with `T23`'s cure), founding and admit, and the persona, face and circle verbs (a lift that grows node's verbs, not a collapse). Stand apart by design: the catalog, wiki-sense and the pools. The gate sweep already stands shared (`gateSweep`). A stage that reads one of the process-wide states `T9` names (custody `seats`, `mountedFace`, the Kahu classification writes, hosting-carry, the switch-act log, face-route, the env `LAR_ROOT`) takes it through its port, never the global. The net collapse reads 200 to 400 lines.
* `T14` The verb math: a plain pure module first (its `coupleMesh` and `crystallize` closure runs pure and deterministic), then a daemon-only `*-vm` startup module after `T15`, copying the startup pattern `worldline-read-vm.ts` (`lararium-tw5`) holds, since ''Q'' burned `query-derive-vm.ts`, with two new mesh subpath exports and an async channel round-trip. On the host binding stay the py and R pipe, the fs signal extraction, the coupling readers, the sense lifecycle verbs, the human-in-the-loop guard, telemetry and the palaces. TW5's boot leaves a startup whose `after` names an unrun task silently unrun, and a throw in `startup` kills the island, so the witness asserts the module's handle after boot and the body wraps its throw; new startup modules move the `filter-cts` goldens; TW5's `platforms` knows only browser and node, so daemon-only comes from plugin selection, which `T15` witnesses.
* `T15` The mirror witness: the glass mirror's plugin set never holds a daemon-only title.
* `T16` Injected homes, building on `CustodyHomes` and `vesselCustodyHomes()` (and `sealHome`, already a parameter through about 25 calls): retires `withLarRoot`, the CLI `--root` env mutation and the git-global shell-out. It also carries the daemon's no-argument genesis readers, which read only the `LAR_ROOT` env while `stand` spawns with `--root` and no `LAR_ROOT`, a split `L5`'s one resolver leaves standing. Mesh source reads no env. Needs `T2`; folds into or follows `T12`. Carries `T20`'s cure.
* `T17` Deadlines arrive injected, after the `dialDeadlines` precedent in `hearth-gate-pins.ts`: the 120 s and 3x pair (two literal copies, in the node composer and `daemon-vm-core.ts`) becomes one named deadline, and an absent deadline refuses rather than falling to the pool's 10 s default, which re-creates the fatal mount false-timeout. Two more fixed 10 s deadlines join the item: the teardown ack and the wiki place-verb. Red: a boot injecting 200 ms; control: the exact numbers at the pool constructor.
* `T18` Mutation as an inspection list, on `T1`'s recipe, in the long tier: diff-scoped runs with survivor lists and no score gate, an optional scoped incremental run on the box, the hand catalog kept beside it. Zero-kill tests become a review list, controls and seeds whitelisted, never an auto-delete. First survivors to answer, each red-first: `held >= floor` mutated to `held > floor` survives at `lares-cli` `kinds.ts` (an untested boundary); removing the `.sort()` in `quorum-entry.ts` survives (canonical parent order unasserted); the cycle-error text and the `?? []` defaults stand untested. Then the law pattern (fast-check, fixed seeds, a control, named mutants) extends to one more package, scored by the runner; `lararium-node`'s heavy suites take their own probe first. Adopting Stryker lands its lockfile delta (about 149 packages) in that lane. Unit shards and witness consolidation wait behind `T12`.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/stride-1-spine`>>

!! Stride 1 Spine

Face and custody ground, then sealed identity and private authority; the face anchor precedes the planes that inherit it.

''Reading note'': the tree and git decide what stands (#/the-loops). Each item names the shape the tree holds today and what stays owed on it; landed work reads in git and #/ashes.

!!! Seven-league stride notes

* ''Integrity owed: face isolation, end to end'' (`K15` step 3, `B1`). Its witness: one adversarial multi-tab, multi-face run in a real browser, an unadmitted port, a stale generation, a cross-face sealed write and the last session's lock. The guards and their reds stand on fake islands and the node, and the one SharedWorker per house stands (`open-browser-vessel.ts`, `shared-holder-production.test.ts` on every engine that attaches one); the real-browser adversarial run stays owed.
* ''Identity authority enforced, beyond the prose'' (`K15` step 6). The anchor's suite holds it (`packages/lararium-mesh/tests/face-anchor.test.ts`): a forged selector and a coherent foreign bundle refuse, one sequence signed twice surfaces as a fork, and a frontier never falls. What stays owed beyond it rests in <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-wave-c-lane#/owed`>>.

!!! The items

* `G7` Custody's edges on train N's ground: a short seat can write a charter that boots torn (`nexus-seal.ts`); a floor-class carrier standing alone founds, which decides whether a new custody adopts a standing vessel key (identity ground); `setFoundingRoute` stands as a test seam in source (`found.ts`). N.
* One charter per vessel rests on a single seal home (`larSealHome`), a guard on one file and never a law; a vessel that carries a second Nexus wants a second seal home, and the charter train names none.
* `K15` One hive per face (#/rulings, 2026-10-18): each PersonaGroup owns its Keyhive identity, event store, registry, archive and content-key seat, and no identity stands above them. The tree holds steps 0 to 3 and 6, their kits in `docs/pono/k15-wave-b-lane`, `k15-wave-c-lane`, `k15-wave-d-lane` and `k15-bonds-lane`. (0) The custody contract (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero`>>): the node and browser record census sorted D, F or S, the FCK key class, each face's own slot tree and recovery route, the carrier binding of face, record name and kind, and the overwrite contract by record-class key (#/rulings, a face's custody records seal under its own key; a shared act never makes a shared secret). (1) Two Keyhive instances in one process hold apart under interleaved acts, a reboot and a cross-read (`packages/lararium-keyhive/tests/two-hives-one-process.probe.test.ts`), and a face context carries its own store, registry, archive and key seat. (2) The face stands as the sole owner of its identity and group (`makeFaceBagDoor`, `faceOwnerVerifier`); a bag keeps one Document across boots (`registerBagsDurably`), the door granting the face's group before it records a Document, so a crash between them re-enters to the same end; and a random atom per (vessel, PersonaGroup) under that face's FCK signs every act on the face's behalf, the place signer keeping the floor's duties alone (operator ruling, 2026-10-19). On the local presence channel the session wears the mounted face, so a verb's presenter derives from the island's own atom and never from the CLI's unsigned `requestedBy`, the floor presents the place for floor acts alone, and a verb that names another face refuses by name (#/rulings, a session wears the face, 2026-10-11; a published atom key would link face and vessel on a wire). (3) Sessions and registries route by one face, no union, each sealer holding its record class's key. (6) One identity model (#/rulings, sync carries, verification decides: the sealed anchor, the signed `@persona` log, verified projections): `readFace` stands as the one door with its census; each D record seals under its face's FCK and keys by its PersonaGroup on node and browser alike; each face unlocks apart, bundles and restores alone, lifts its anon veil on join and seats when its grant arrives; the place mints no Keyhive identity, and every face operation signs or opens by the explicitly selected face's atom, a thief holding the place seed, face B or no face refusing at each gate; a faceless floor boots no Keyhive and refuses face verbs by name; the relay key rests in its own random S record with the relay service its principal (`lares herm relay-rotate`); old mixed bytes refuse by name; and the seed census reads no exception. The isolation red it keeps: unlock face A alone, bypass every route, and try to open and replace B's occupied carriers with every key A holds, the S key included; each open fails, B's bytes stand unchanged, and A's bundle restored on a fresh vessel holds nothing of B. Owed, each red-first: (4) content keys and removal per face, `K12`'s fresh group inside it; (5) a one-face restore and a herm that carries yet authors nothing. K, W.
* `B1` Browser custody, and the browser vessel lift. The lift rises before the rite (operator, 2026-11-05: //"Browser + Node is part of founding"//), every slice with it (//"Land all before the rite, no 'build a now, b later' in early alpha."//). <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/browser-vessel-lift#/the-slices`>> names what stands built: the vessel runs on a vessel worker's thread, each tab carries its own live session by its own unlock, and the holder keeps a face's keys only while some session of that face stands unlocked (#/rulings, 2026-10-18; the research and its leads at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/session-unlock-2026`>>). Custody stands per face (`browser-custody.ts`): a passphrase, paper (2-of-3) or passkey slot binds a tree, a passkey never alone; each face's records rest under that face's own tree, named by its PersonaGroup; a face's root and its ward key rest under a ward of their own; a wrong route refuses and changes no byte, a locked keystore refuses `sealed-locked` before any mint, and the clear act names one face, asks through the confirm door and answers only to a literal yes (#/rulings, a shared act never makes a shared secret). The holder answers a port only for a session it admitted, an unadmitted port asking for a sealed body refuses by name, and the last session of a face to close locks that face's hive (`shared-holder-production.test.ts`). Owed: the slices that carrier leaves without a built mark (idempotence, the vessel reading its own durability and the `X0b` sweep over the browser vessel among them); the tree key's rotation, since a copy of the tree taken before an unbind still opens through the removed slot until it rolls; and the passkey slot's device measure (`B3`). B.
* `W4` The face-scoped proof chain: face key, the vessel's face key, the session key. The subjects, private authority, the session signer and the session face supplier stand in code, and the withdrawal proof reads as a house model the operator may overturn (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof#/the-withdrawal-proof`>>). The two custody records stand in node (`face-key-custody.ts`) and in the browser under the face's own tree (`vessel-face-key/<G>|<nexus>|<faceKey>`, `browser-custody.ts`); fleet join calls the certificate and grant path, and `openJoinedFaceSession` stands on both shores. Owed: the browser's switch between faces, which rides `B8` (#/after-the-seat). House. W.
* `F12` A parallel draft names its writer by the record's own `modifier` value, else by the bare Automerge actor id; neither binds a face, so a reader learns only what a writer chose to say (`parallel-drafts.ts`). The session's face on each change answers it. O1.
* `F10` A person's fleet view and removal; the long-silent device rule of #/decisions. O1.
* `K12` A fresh PersonaGroup for a seat no hand can revoke (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae#/keys`>>). The PersonaGroup stands as a Keyhive group, so every other seat's revocation rolls the bags the group reaches (`persona-group-roll`); revoke authority follows the seating lineage, so a seat whose seating hand left, and the founder's own seat, leave only by a fresh group. Owed first: a name the face keeps across groups, since its plane names key on the group's id. It builds inside `K15`'s per-face removal step. K.
* `S7` Standing leans the seal met, kept as built unless the operator overturns one: an unlock mints a fresh Keyhive document for a binding whose floor-minted document died; one contact card per stand; the browser's creator veil stays unwired; a browser founded before the Keyhive move refuses to boot. Answered by rule and still open: a resumed face founding mints a new group; a founding stopped before its anchors re-founds with a fresh veil.
* `H5` An invited user's root mints on her device, sealed; the herm carries a sealed copy. B.
* `G13` Summons: no check of who wrote one; a consume tombstones every layer; a delivery claims its request id before it relays, so two deliveries of one pending summons place its verb once and a failed consume releases the claim (`verb-summons-claim.test.ts`), and the claim lives in memory (`verb-dispatcher.ts`, `verb-summons.ts`). Owed: a writer check, a working-layer tombstone alone, durable dedup, lease and fence, and honouring `aud`. House. G.
* `G34` A removal no reader can keep refuses by name (#/rulings; <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/automerge-doc-store`>>). The store lands a based removal a concurrent writer overrode, then surfaces it to a listening reader alone: with no parallel-drafts reader it drops the surfacing with no word, and a reader that throws earns one console line while the write stands (`automerge-doc-store.ts`, `parallel-drafts.ts`). Owed: the refusal by name before any change lands, wherever no reader stands or every reader failed, with the store authoring no draft of its own; and the durable conflict handle the refusal carries (the document, the removal's request id, the conflicting change and head identities, never the hand's text), at a home where a hand whose editor went offline finds it again. G.
* `G35` The disk watcher's standing instruction (#/rulings; <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/disk-projection#/projection-law`>>). Owed: the instruction as a record the operator signs by a human route, naming one bag, a path pattern and a per-wave count ceiling, which no automated seat signs; the watcher's settle of each pending removal it covers through the shore's removal door; the audit record that names the instruction a removal rode; `lares shore restore` over a settled removal; and a mass vanish past the ceiling, a standoff, or a removal outside the pattern left pending for a person (`packages/lares-cli/src/commands/watch.ts`, `shore-verb.ts`, `disk-projector.ts`). G.
* `K18` The recovery door: recovery-contact-quorum rotation of a persona root beneath its KEL prefix, then `reanchorAfterRotation`, then a re-admit carrying the current `@persona` log and signed frontier. Enrolment takes exactly three recovery contacts at threshold two (#/rulings, 2026-10-31), signed into the enrolment and carried by every later rotation, each reveal opening three distinct keys at two, and the face's committed self-recovery digest counting as none of the three at a reveal. Owed: no production door rotates a root today, and the ceremony rotates under two of three recovery contacts; `reanchorAfterRotation` stands proven as a library function (Wave C, <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-wave-c-lane`>> #/owed). It also closes `K17`'s window: a closed roster's keys can still raise the lease (stale more, never less) until this door rotates them out; the fold never lowers. K.
* `K22` ''The recovery readmit stands unwired'' (the unwired-code scout, 2026-11-06). `reconstructAndReadmit` (`packages/lararium-node/src/recovery-keel.ts`) and `runReadmitEdge` (`packages/lararium-keyhive/src/recovery-core.ts`) run under tests alone, so no production path recovers a device; a production recovery verb under the recovery contacts' quorum (`K18`) stands as the wiring's candidate. Owed: a zero-trust critical review decides retire / wire / reshape, then the work (#/rulings, 2026-11-06, the unwired code); the Terra review's verdict sets its next step. It rides `C6`, whose answer on what recovery restores under random roots decides it (#/the-leaps). K.
* `K16` The Teshima Family Council (#/rulings, 2026-10-18; <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/cabal-realm`>>), `K15`'s acceptance story. Kalani founds a private council; Leilani joins it with two of her three faces. Red-first, after `K15`'s face context: (1) the council's hive as a Keyhive group holding no key of its own, its members the chosen faces' own identities; (2) an invite walks Leilani to the door, the threshold grafts each chosen face, a face leaves by its own act, and another's removal rides `kapae` or a fork by omission; (3) a member face's photo lands in its working layer of the council wiki and reaches the council's canon by `MOVE` (`W27`); (4) both chosen faces read it, the face kept back reads nothing and learns nothing, and no event in the council's history names a creator or issuer that ties two of Leilani's faces together; (5) a herm carries the sealed book and authors nothing (`K15` step 5). The e2e set stands four vessels: Kalani's, one per chosen face of Leilani's, and a herm. The e2e set walks the council at one hearth first: Kalani's seed charter holds the realm and Leilani's chosen faces walk there as guests, no Nexus founded (#/rulings, one charter). The two-hearth council follows, under the Nexus the two found, and there the member consult that lets a realm's documents cross between two operators reads a root map nothing fills (cabal-realm #/many-realms, owed), and the climb from seed charter to Nexus re-pins the realm by a named act (owed). Open joints: a quorum Handle with a seated key stands only where the council signs as one name, and that key signs collective acts alone, reaching no member's hive (#/the-steward-signer, owed). K, W.
* `K20` A face record's answer rides the socket alone. Owed: the cabal-feed receipt's timing beside the leaf's slot write across the two daemon documents (a device-local ledger for sensitive act receipts); scrubbing a consumed summons; a caller-owned request id for `session:act` recovery; the maintenance frame's face-signing `realm-bag`. K; ahead of `K21`.
* `K21` Per-face content-key seats and mints, the end state that `K19`'s fence holds the place for: each joined face seals its own content keys, holds its own outbox and mints its own bags, so a verb stops running on the mounted face's resources whichever provider cleared it. It carries `U1`'s named regression: sibling devices of one face read none of each other's seals, since `holdsWrite` accepts only this vessel's own chain until the grant ring reads by face key. It rests on sealed-store step 3 and answers through the socket-only shape of `K20`. K.
* `P1` The steward signer: the composing vessel's `realm-bag` verb calls the steward door (`realm-steward.ts`); the docker `meme` scenario by hand, in the asker's order; a door for a collective Handle's seal waits on the chartered classes; the vouch door, the feed's writer and a realm's holder hand sign with the worn face's key at the Nexus the realm stands under (`cabal-face.ts`). P.
* `P9` The re-seal proof and the fork-resolve act stand ruled (#/rulings): the re-seal record builds with sealed-store step three, the fork-resolve act in train N.
* `P13` Per-meme grants; a leaf honouring a session's effective recipe. O1 for the recipe.
* `I3` The intent plane under re-pin, re-target and growth (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-intent-plane`>>; built on `I1` and `I2`; the once-under-re-pin half stands). Owed, each red-first with its control: the cross-device fence (S2); the receipt's one-digest heads (S3); the class audience in production and the issuer's own cancel and supersede (S4); the pane's and `intent show`'s denials, `outOfAudience` outcomes and refusal ids, a signed fault record and a finite `every` that waits (S5); compaction (S6). Relay.
* `W9` The sibling channel at face scope, on per-dial transport keys. W.
* `W12` The herm route for cancel and the three receipts, as ruled (#/rulings; <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/receipt-ledger#/the-relay-leg`>>); no code asks, cancels or answers across a herm. Owed, with `W9`: the ask, the cancel and the vessel's answer as a tagged inner frame under the sealed sibling session's own seal, the vessel reading that session's proof as the asker's; the herm's bare delivery note inside the session, bound to the ask's unique request id and the session's causal position, attesting transit and never execution; and the rule for which verbs a sibling's standing reaches across the session (`sibling-channel.ts`, `uds-channel.ts`). W.
* `E2` The serving key's custody and its unattended road (#/rulings, the ember's key at rest; <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-table`>>). Built: the fifth class `serving` in the carrier table, read by the census and `vault status`; SK minted at the founding; `sk-slots` with the vessel key's entry, opened at every human unlock and re-wrapped by a vessel-key rotation. Owed: the serving records under SK and SK's own rotation; no minting secret in any serving record; the anchor path, where an ember slot answers unattended only against a hardware-sealed hearth key whose attestation the ember checks on each ask; the confirm path, where every slot of a hearth with no such anchor answers only on a person's confirm and threshold two asks a second confirm, never an unattended raise; and the bare deny-only `sk-rotation-owed` mark that any counted removal sets and that refuses every ask until SK rolls (`vault-carriers.ts`, `keyslot.ts`, `custody-seat.ts`). E.
* `E3` The serving gate's tier (#/rulings, the ember's serving gate; <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof#/the-gate-exchange`>>). Owed: a third tier name in the route type, its bundle derived from SK under its own registered domain, its pin, and a pin's move by a signed causal event or a verified key-history step alone, with no quiet period that ages a pin out (`gate-bundle.ts`). E.
* `E4` The raise's commit point and its lapse (#/rulings, how a raise lapses; <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/waking-floor#/the-shape`>>). Owed: the count spent at the raise ledger's durable commit, before SERVING composes, so a crash before the commit spends nothing and serves nothing and a crash after it burns the raise; and the raise's lapse at a lock, a restart, a human unlock or a counted removal, with no timer and no heartbeat that ends it (`custody-seat.ts`, the raise ledger's writer). E.
* `H2` The delegation and three dials stand specified in `hosting-delegation.ts`. Owed: the live issuer, reader, hosting store and socket sorter in one signed-act cut. H.
* `H8` The grant's signed shape and public-half verifier stand specified in `walker-grant.ts` (#/rulings; <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/ember#/the-tiers`>>). Owed: the live roll that derives grant public halves for serving records inside H's one act cut, before E's walker arm. H.

