* `T27` ''Teardown seams''. A refused boot leaves a stale cas door; the sync gates carry no torn-down guard, so a sweep in flight during teardown can register a route that makes the next composition throw "already owned" and stand no gates until restart; the custody and dyad doors drop before the daemon island shuts. Lane `recut-gates`: the refused boot's door in slice 1, the rest in slice 2. Landed, PR #141: the island's doors drop after the island shuts, and a refused boot leaves no door answering. What stays owed rests in the porch (the rig's fixed settle window).
* `T33` ''A planted roll mark skips a roll'' (security, MEDIUM, predates `U1`; the train t2 cold read, wave three). `raisesMarkedAnswered` trusts any `rolled/<raise>` ledger entry and `markRaiseRolled` stores no receipt; `ledger:edge` reads as synced (`operator-daemon-behavior.ts`) and the vessel-private class does not list it, so a planted mark makes seat-duties skip a removal roll and, beside `U1`'s durable owed list, clears that list. Cure: a mark counts only beside a kept roll whose receipt this vessel's key signed, and the ledger joins the vessel-private class. Red: plant a `rolled/<raise>` entry from another device, remove a reader, and the roll still runs and the owed list keeps the raise; control: a vessel's own signed roll clears it. Landed, PR #144 (`13d24a218`): one entry predicate shared by every reader and the writer counts a mark only under a receipt this vessel's own key signed, and `ledger:edge` crosses to no peer (vessel-private, a `device` owner no relation opens), classed for every face whose key the vessel holds at boot. What stays owed rests in the porch (faces whose keys open after boot).
